WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Welcome to
the Huberman Lab podcast,

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.920
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:05.700
for everyday life.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.235
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.235 --> 00:00:11.080
I'm Andrew Huberman,
and I'm a Professor

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:13.210
of Neurobiology
and Ophthalmology

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:15.010
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:17.920
Today, my guest is
Dr. Lex Fridman Dr.

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.290
Lex Fridman is an expert
in electrical and computer

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:23.170
engineering, artificial
intelligence, and robotics.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.500
He is also the host of the
Lex Fridman Podcast, which

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.270
initially started
as a podcast focus

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:30.860
on technology and
science of various kinds,

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.100
including computer
science and physics,

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:36.520
but rapidly evolved to include
guests and other topics

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:40.210
as a matter of focus,
including sport.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:43.090
For instance, Dr. Lex
Freedman is a Black Belt

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:44.350
in Brazilian jujitsu.

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:45.880
And he's had numerous
guests on who

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.770
come from the fields
of Brazilian jujitsu,

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:52.180
both from the coaching side
and from the competitor side.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:54.430
He also has shown
an active interest

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:57.730
in topics such as chess
and essentially anything

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:01.330
that involves intense activation
and engagement of the mind

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:02.570
and/or body.

00:01:02.570 --> 00:01:04.120
In fact, the Lex
Fridman podcast has

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.850
evolved to take on
very difficult topics

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:09.440
such as mental health-- he's
had various psychiatrists

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.440
and other guests on that
relate to mental health

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:15.700
and mental illness, as well as
guest focused on geopolitics

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:17.680
and some of the more
controversial issues

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:19.000
that face our times.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.680
He's had comedians, he's had
scientists, he's had friends,

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.960
he's had enemies on his podcast.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.860
Lex has a phenomenal, I would
say a 1 in an eight billion

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:34.210
ability to find these people,
make them comfortable,

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:38.530
and in that comfort, both
try to understand them

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:42.520
and to confront them and to
push them so that we all learn.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.310
All of which is to say that
Lex Fridman is no longer just

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:48.010
an accomplished scientist,
he certainly is that.

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:50.590
But he has also become one of
the more preeminent thought

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:51.950
leaders on the planet.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.800
And if there's anything
that really captures

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.320
the essence of Lex
Fridman, it's his love

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.200
of learning, his desire to share
with us, the human experience,

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.520
and to broaden that experience
so that we all may benefit.

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:05.950
In many ways, our discussion
during today's episode

00:02:05.950 --> 00:02:08.350
captures the many
facets of Lex Fridman,

00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:11.560
although no conversation, of
course, could capture them all.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:13.360
We sit down to the
conversation just days

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.480
after Lex returned from
Ukraine, where he deliberately

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.570
placed himself into the
tension of that environment

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:22.480
in order to understand the
geopolitics of the region

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.880
and to understand
exactly what was

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:28.180
happening at the level of the
ground and the people there.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:32.350
You may notice that he carries
quite a lot of both, emotion

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.210
and knowledge and understanding.

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.830
And yet in a very
classic Lex Fridman way,

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:38.650
you'll notice that
he's able to zoom out

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:41.500
of his own experience around
any number of different topics

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.210
and view them through
a variety of lenses

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:48.010
so that first of all, everyone
feel included, but most of all,

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:50.560
so that everyone learned
something new, that

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.510
is to gain new perspective.

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:55.570
Our discussion also ventures
into the waters of social media

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:57.640
and how that
landscape is changing

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:00.430
the way that science and
technology are communicated.

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:03.730
We also get into the topics of
motivation drive and purpose,

00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:07.430
both finding it and executing
on that drive and purpose.

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:10.840
I should mention that this is
episode 100 of the Huberman Lab

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:11.620
Podcast.

00:03:11.620 --> 00:03:14.170
And I would be remiss
if I did not tell you

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:16.750
that there would be no
Huberman Lab Podcast,

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:18.400
were it not for Lex Fridman.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:20.380
I was a fan of the
Lex Fridman Podcast

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:23.500
long before I was ever invited
on to the podcast as a guest.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.840
And after our first
recording, Lex

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.270
was the one that suggested
that I start a podcast.

00:03:28.270 --> 00:03:30.320
He only gave me two
pieces of advice.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:33.310
The first piece of advice
was, start a podcast.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:35.230
And the second
piece of advice was

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:38.710
that I not just make it me
blabbing into the microphone

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:40.120
and staring at the camera.

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.610
So I can safely say
that I at least followed

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:46.000
half of his advice, and that
I am ever grateful for Lex,

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.850
both as a friend, a
colleague in science,

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:52.150
and now fellow podcaster for
making the suggestion that we

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:53.470
start this podcast.

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:55.240
I already mentioned
a few of the topics

00:03:55.240 --> 00:03:56.740
covered on today's podcast.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:00.310
But I can assure you that
there is far more to the person

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:02.423
that many of us
know as Lex Fridman.

00:04:02.423 --> 00:04:04.840
If you are somebody interested
in artificial intelligence,

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:07.300
engineering, or robotics,
today's discussion

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:08.870
is most certainly for you.

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:10.630
And if you are not,
but you are somebody

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:13.480
who is interested in world
politics, and more importantly,

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:16.240
the human experience,
both the individual

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.579
and the collective
human experience,

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:21.130
Lex shares what can
only be described

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:24.430
as incredible insights
into what he views

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:28.240
as the human experience and what
is optimal in order to derive

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:29.830
from our time on this planet.

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:31.602
Before we begin, I'd
like to emphasize

00:04:31.602 --> 00:04:34.060
that this podcast is separate
from my teaching and research

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:35.140
roles at Stanford.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:37.420
It is however, part of
my desire and effort

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.370
to bring zero cost to
consumer information

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:41.230
about science and
science-related tools

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:42.520
to the general public.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:44.020
In keeping with
that theme, I'd like

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:46.430
to thank the sponsors
of today's podcast.

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:48.280
Our first sponsor is LMNT.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:50.980
LMNT is an electrolyte drink
with everything you need

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:52.120
and nothing you don't.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:54.950
That means the electrolytes,
sodium, potassium,

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:57.730
and magnesium are in LMNT
in the correct ratios.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:59.680
But it has no sugar.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:01.510
As I mentioned before
on the podcast,

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:03.400
electrolytes are
critical to the function

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.650
of every cell in the
body, and especially

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:09.070
the cells in your brain,
meaning neurons or nerve cells.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:10.680
Indeed, the ability
for nerve cells

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:13.170
to be active and communicate
with one another critically

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:16.020
depends on sodium,
potassium, and magnesium.

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.600
You can get electrolytes
from a variety of sources.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.910
But it's often hard to get
them in the proper ratios,

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:21.990
even from food.

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:24.633
So if you're somebody who's
exercising a lot and sweating,

00:05:24.633 --> 00:05:26.550
or if you're somebody
following, for instance,

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:29.880
a low carbohydrate or even a
semi-low carbohydrate diet,

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:32.040
that will cause you to
excrete electrolytes.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:33.780
I tend to have my
LMNT first thing

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:36.540
in the morning when I wake up
or within the first few hours

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:39.570
of waking, any time while
or after I'm exercising,

00:05:39.570 --> 00:05:41.760
or I've sweat a lot, such
as exiting the sauna.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:44.340
If you'd like to try LMNT
you can go to drinkLMNT--

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:48.420
that's LMNT..com/Huberman to
claim a free element sample

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:49.530
pack with your purchase.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:54.240
Again, that's drinkLMNT,
LMNT.com/Huberman to my free

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:55.020
sample pack.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:57.510
Today's episode is also
brought to us by Levels.

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:59.010
Levels is a program
that lets you

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:01.343
see how different foods affect
your health by giving you

00:06:01.343 --> 00:06:03.990
real-time feedback on your
diet using a continuous glucose

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:04.770
monitor.

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:07.495
Now blood glucose or blood
sugar is a critical aspect

00:06:07.495 --> 00:06:09.120
of your immediate
and long term health,

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:11.010
and indeed, your
feelings of vigor

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:14.100
and mental clarity and
well-being at any moment.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:16.740
One of the key things is to know
how different foods and food

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:19.110
combinations and
timing of food intake

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:20.850
is impacting blood glucose.

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:22.800
And with Levels,
you're able to assess

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:24.870
all of that in real time.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:25.950
I tried Levels.

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:27.490
And what it taught
me, for instance,

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:29.910
