00:11:00.340 --> 00:11:04.990
I've followed a number of
your structural formats.

00:11:04.990 --> 00:11:06.845
Attire, I don't wear a tie.

00:11:06.845 --> 00:11:08.845
I'm constantly reminded
about this by my father.

00:11:08.845 --> 00:11:09.010
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:10.700
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Who says
what-- he'd saw my podcast.

00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:12.340
And he was like, why
don't you dress properly

00:11:12.340 --> 00:11:13.215
like your friend Lex?

00:11:13.215 --> 00:11:15.190
He literally said that.

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:17.510
And it's a debate that
goes back and forth.

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:18.770
But nonetheless--

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:20.020
LEX FRIDMAN: How does it feel?

00:11:20.020 --> 00:11:21.220
Episode 100.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:22.230
How does it feel?

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:22.720
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
You know, I think.

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:24.670
LEX FRIDMAN: Can you
imagine you're here?

00:11:24.670 --> 00:11:27.550
You hear after so many
episodes and done so much.

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:31.065
I mean, the number of
hours is just insane.

00:11:31.065 --> 00:11:32.440
The amount of
passion, the amount

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:36.670
of work you put into
this, what's it feel like?

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:38.470
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It feels great.

00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:41.860
And it feels very much
like the horizon is still

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:43.360
at the same distance
in front of me.

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:46.642
Every episode, I just try
and get information there.

00:11:46.642 --> 00:11:48.850
And the process that we
talked about on your podcast.

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:51.383
We won't go into it of
collecting information,

00:11:51.383 --> 00:11:53.050
distilling it down
to some simple notes,

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:54.580
walking around,
listening to music,

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:57.898
trying to figure out
what the motifs are,

00:11:57.898 --> 00:12:00.190
and then-- as just like you,
I don't use a teleprompter

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:01.270
or anything like that.

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:02.650
There's very minimal notes.

00:12:02.650 --> 00:12:04.520
So it feels great,
and I love it.

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:08.350
And again, I'm just grateful
to you for inspiring it.

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:10.790
And I just want to keep
going and do more of it.

00:12:10.790 --> 00:12:15.310
And I should say
I am also relieved

00:12:15.310 --> 00:12:19.210
that we're sitting here because
you recently went overseas

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:25.060
to a very intense war zone,
literally, the Ukraine.

00:12:25.060 --> 00:12:28.300
And the entire time
that you were there,

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:30.160
I was genuinely concerned.

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:33.250
The world's a unpredictable
place, in general.

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:36.650
And we don't always get the only
vote and what happens to us.

00:12:36.650 --> 00:12:39.670
So first of all,
welcome back safely,

00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:41.870
one peace, one alive peace.

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:45.210
And what was that like?

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:49.110
I mean, at a broad level,
at a specific level,

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:50.400
what drew you there?

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:51.780
What surprised you?

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:56.680
And how do you think it changed
you in coming back here?

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:58.430
LEX FRIDMAN: I think
there's a lot to say.

00:12:58.430 --> 00:13:00.610
But first, it is
really good to be back.

00:13:00.610 --> 00:13:02.590
One of the things
that when you go

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:05.380
to a difficult part of the world
or a part of the world that's

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:07.270
going through
something difficult,

00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:11.830
you really appreciate how
great it is to be an American.

00:13:11.830 --> 00:13:12.640
Everything.

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:15.670
The easy access to food.

00:13:15.670 --> 00:13:21.330
Despite what people think, the
stable, reliable rule of law.

00:13:21.330 --> 00:13:26.910
The lack of corruption in that
you can trust that if you start

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:30.990
a business or if you take
on various pursuits in life

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:34.890
that there's not going to
be at-scale manipulation

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:36.910
of your efforts such
that you can't succeed.

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:41.730
So this kind of
capitalism is in it's--

00:13:41.730 --> 00:13:45.390
the ideal of capitalism is
really still burning bright

00:13:45.390 --> 00:13:46.090
in this country.

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:48.720
And it really makes you
appreciate those aspects.

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:54.870
And also just the ability to
have a home for generations,

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:56.110
across generations.

00:13:56.110 --> 00:13:58.530
So you can have your
grandfather live

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:00.300
in Kentucky in a certain city.

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:04.590
And then his children lived
there, and you live there,

00:14:04.590 --> 00:14:06.960
and then it just
continues on and on.

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:10.380
That's the kind of thing you can
have when you don't have war.

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:13.860
Because war destroys
entire communities.

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:17.970
And it destroys
histories, generations,

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:20.485
like life stories that stretch
across the generations.

00:14:20.485 --> 00:14:20.985
So--

00:14:20.985 --> 00:14:21.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah yeah.

00:14:21.860 --> 00:14:24.152
I didn't even think about
that until you said just now.

00:14:24.152 --> 00:14:27.510
But photographs, hard drives
get destroyed or just abandoned.

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:28.260
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:30.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Libraries.

00:14:30.690 --> 00:14:32.640
I mean, nowadays, things
exist in the cloud

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:34.020
but are still a lot of--

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:34.260
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:35.635
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
--material goods

00:14:35.635 --> 00:14:37.710
that are irreplaceable, right.

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:41.490
LEX FRIDMAN: Well, even in rural
parts of the United States,

00:14:41.490 --> 00:14:43.140
they don't exist in
the cloud, right.

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:46.620
A lot of people still, well,
even in towns, they still

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:50.400
love the physical photo
album of your family.

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:52.860
A lot of people still
store their photographs

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:57.330
of families in the VHS tapes and
all that kind of stuff, yeah.

00:14:57.330 --> 00:15:03.170
But I think-- there's so many
things I've learned and really

00:15:03.170 --> 00:15:04.850
felt the lessons.

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:09.440
One of which is nobody gives a
damn when your photos are gone

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:12.330
and all that kind of
stuff, your house is gone.

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:15.710
The thing time and time
again I saw for people

00:15:15.710 --> 00:15:20.690
that lost everything is how
happy they are for the people.

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:24.440
They love the friends, the
family that are still alive.

00:15:24.440 --> 00:15:26.930
That's the only thing
they talk about.

00:15:26.930 --> 00:15:31.160
That, in fact, they
don't mention actually

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:35.910
with much dramatic sort
of vigor about the trauma

00:15:35.910 --> 00:15:36.840
of losing your home.

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:41.010
They're just non-stop saying
how lucky they are that person X

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:42.810
person Y is still here.

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:46.630
And that makes you realize
that when you lose everything,

00:15:46.630 --> 00:15:47.280
it's still--

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:49.450
it makes you realize
what really matters,

00:15:49.450 --> 00:15:51.060
which is the people
in your life.

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:53.520
I mean, a lot of people kind
of realize that later in life,

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:56.250
when you're facing mortality,
when you're facing your death,

00:15:56.250 --> 00:15:59.220
or you get a cancer
diagnosis, that kind of stuff.

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:02.640
I think people here in
America, in California,

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:06.120
with the fires, you you
can still lose your home.

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:07.620
You are going to
realize, like, nah.

00:16:07.620 --> 00:16:08.890
It doesn't really matter.

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:12.330
It's a pain in the ass
but what matters is still

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:15.090
the family, the
people, and so on.

00:16:15.090 --> 00:16:18.508
I think the most intense thing--

00:16:18.508 --> 00:16:20.550
I talked to several hundred
people, some of which

00:16:20.550 --> 00:16:21.540
is recorded.

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:25.500
I've really been
struggling to put that out

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:27.580
because I have to
edit it myself.

00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:30.810
And so you're talking about 30,
40 hours of footage, and it--

00:16:30.810 --> 00:16:31.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Is
emotionally struggling?

00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:32.430
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.
It is extremely difficult.

00:16:32.430 --> 00:16:33.450
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Are you
like emotional struggle?

00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:34.810
LEX FRIDMAN: It's
extremely difficult.

00:16:34.810 --> 00:16:36.310
So I talked to a
lot of politicians.

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:38.160
The number two in the
country, number three.

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:40.050
I'll be back there to
talk to the president

00:16:40.050 --> 00:16:42.600
to do a three-hour conversation.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:43.920
Those are easy to edit.

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:47.700
They're really heartfelt
and thoughtful folks

00:16:47.700 --> 00:16:51.900
from different perspectives
on the geopolitics of the war.

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:53.640
But the ones that
really hard to edit

00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:56.580
is like grandmas that are
in the middle of nowhere.

00:16:56.580 --> 00:16:57.608
They lost everything.

00:16:57.608 --> 00:16:59.400
They still have hope,
they still have love.

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:01.650
And some of them have--

00:17:01.650 --> 00:17:03.630
some of them, many of
them, unfortunately,

00:17:03.630 --> 00:17:05.400
have now hate in their heart.

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:11.829
So in February, when
Russia invaded Ukraine,

00:17:11.829 --> 00:17:14.530
this is the thing I
realized about War.

00:17:14.530 --> 00:17:17.440
One of the most
painful one lessons

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:23.339
is that war creates
generational hate.

00:17:23.339 --> 00:17:27.359
We sometimes think about war
as a thing that kills people,

00:17:27.359 --> 00:17:30.930
kills civilians, kills
soldiers, takes away lives,

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:32.160
injures people.

00:17:32.160 --> 00:17:35.100
But we don't
directly think about

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:37.230
the secondary and
tertiary effects

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:39.910
of that which lasts decades.

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:43.110
Which is anyone who's lost
the father or a mother

00:17:43.110 --> 00:17:49.520
or a daughter or a son,
they now hate not just

00:17:49.520 --> 00:17:51.500
the individual
soldiers or the leaders

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:55.080
that invaded their country but
the entirety of the people.

00:17:55.080 --> 00:17:59.810
So it's not that they
hate Vladimir Putin

00:17:59.810 --> 00:18:01.820
or hate the Russian military.

00:18:01.820 --> 00:18:04.480
They hate Russian people.

00:18:04.480 --> 00:18:06.850
So that tears the
fabric of a thing

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:12.280
that, for me-- my half
my family's from Ukraine,

00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:14.110
half of my family
is from Russia.

00:18:14.110 --> 00:18:15.490
But there is--

00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:21.310
I remember the pain the
triumph of World War two

00:18:21.310 --> 00:18:24.940
still resonates through
my entire family tree.

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:27.370
And so, you remember when
the Russians and Ukrainians

00:18:27.370 --> 00:18:30.400
fought together against
this Nazi invasion.

00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:33.050
You remember a lot of that.

00:18:33.050 --> 00:18:38.930
And now, to see the fabric
of this peoples torn apart

00:18:38.930 --> 00:18:41.690
completely with hate is really,
really difficult. For me,

00:18:41.690 --> 00:18:44.600
just to realize that
things will just never

00:18:44.600 --> 00:18:48.050
be the same on this particular
cultural, historical aspect.

00:18:48.050 --> 00:18:52.850
But also, there's so many
painful ways in which

00:18:52.850 --> 00:18:54.480
things will never be the same.

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:56.660
Which is we've seen that
it's possible to have

00:18:56.660 --> 00:19:01.120
a major hot war in
the 21st century.

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:02.870
I think a lot of people
are watching this.

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:03.950
China is watching this.

00:19:03.950 --> 00:19:04.920
India is watching this.

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:08.810
United States is watching this
and thinking we can actually

00:19:08.810 --> 00:19:11.040
have a large-scale war.

00:19:11.040 --> 00:19:13.800
And I think the lessons
learned from that.

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:18.600
Might be the kind that lead
to a major World War III

00:19:18.600 --> 00:19:20.710
in the 21st century.

00:19:20.710 --> 00:19:25.765
So one of the things I realized
watching the whole scene

00:19:25.765 --> 00:19:27.640
is that we don't know
shit about what's going

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:29.390
to happen in the 21st century.

00:19:29.390 --> 00:19:32.035
And it might-- we kind of have
this intuition like surely

00:19:32.035 --> 00:19:33.535
there's not going
to be another war.

00:19:33.535 --> 00:19:34.345
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Like
we'll just coast.

00:19:34.345 --> 00:19:35.170
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.

00:19:35.170 --> 00:19:36.087
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:19:36.087 --> 00:19:37.370
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah, pandemic.

00:19:37.370 --> 00:19:37.870
Yeah--

00:19:37.870 --> 00:19:38.920
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
And back to normal.

00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:39.290
LEX FRIDMAN: Back to normal--

00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:40.707
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Whatever that is.

00:19:40.707 --> 00:19:43.210
LEX FRIDMAN: But you
have to remember,

00:19:43.210 --> 00:19:47.980
at the end of World War I,
as Woodrow Wilson called it,

00:19:47.980 --> 00:19:49.480
the war to end all wars.

00:19:49.480 --> 00:19:52.870
Nobody ironically,
in a dark way,

00:19:52.870 --> 00:19:56.020
it was also the roaring 20s
when people believed this.

00:19:56.020 --> 00:19:58.300
There will never be
another World War.

00:19:58.300 --> 00:20:04.510
And 20 years after that,
the rise of Nazi Germany.

00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:06.400
A charismatic leader
that captivated

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:08.500
the minds of
millions and built up

00:20:08.500 --> 00:20:11.540
a military that can
take on the whole world.

00:20:11.540 --> 00:20:15.460
And so it makes you realize
that this is still possible.

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:16.660
This is still possible.

00:20:16.660 --> 00:20:18.760
And then the tension.

00:20:18.760 --> 00:20:22.720
You see this-- the media
machine, the propaganda

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:26.370
machine, that I've gotten
to see every aspect of.

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:31.140
It's still fueling that division
between America and China.

00:20:31.140 --> 00:20:34.320
Between Russia and India.

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:37.263
And then Africa has
a complicated thing

00:20:37.263 --> 00:20:39.180
that's trying to figure
out who are they with,

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:40.290
who are they against.

00:20:40.290 --> 00:20:43.060
And just this tension is
building and building.

00:20:43.060 --> 00:20:47.070
And like it makes you
realize like we might--

00:20:47.070 --> 00:20:51.690
the thing that might
shake human civilization

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:55.640
may not be so far off.

00:20:55.640 --> 00:20:59.390
That's a realization
you get to really feel.

00:20:59.390 --> 00:21:01.680
I mean, there's all
kinds of other lessons.

00:21:01.680 --> 00:21:03.920
And one of which is propaganda.

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:05.300
Is I got to--

00:21:05.300 --> 00:21:06.890
I get a lot of letters, emails.

00:21:06.890 --> 00:21:11.360
And some of them are full
of really intense language,

00:21:11.360 --> 00:21:15.650
full of hate from
every side toward me.

00:21:15.650 --> 00:21:20.210
Or, well, the hate is
towards me as representing

00:21:20.210 --> 00:21:26.230
side X. And X stands as a
variable for every side.

00:21:26.230 --> 00:21:30.310
So either I'm a Zelensky
show, or I'm a Putin show,

00:21:30.310 --> 00:21:35.620
or I'm a NATO show,
or I'm an America--

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:38.350
America show--
American empire show.

00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:41.140
Or I'm a Democrat
or a Republican.

00:21:41.140 --> 00:21:44.910
Because it's already been,
in this country, politicized.

00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:49.200
I think there's a sense of
Ukraine is this place that's

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:50.320
full of corruption.

00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:51.660
Why are we sending money there?

00:21:51.660 --> 00:21:53.700
I think that's kind
of the messaging

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:55.410
on the Republican side.

00:21:55.410 --> 00:21:56.890
On the Democratic side--

00:21:56.890 --> 00:22:00.030
I'm not even keeping track
of the actual messaging

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:02.910
and the conspiracy theories
and the narratives,

00:22:02.910 --> 00:22:05.160
but they are-- the
tension is there.

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:07.020
And I get to feel it directly.

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:09.000
And what you get to
really experience

00:22:09.000 --> 00:22:12.750
is there's a large
number of narratives

00:22:12.750 --> 00:22:16.620
that all are extremely
confident themselves that they

00:22:16.620 --> 00:22:18.880
know the truth.

00:22:18.880 --> 00:22:21.300
People are convinced,
first of all,

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:22.890
that they're not being lied to.

00:22:22.890 --> 00:22:25.950
People in Russia think
there's no propaganda.

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:28.380
They think that,
yes, yes, there is

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:31.080
like state-sponsored
propaganda, but we're all

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:37.710
smart enough to ignore the lame
propaganda that's everywhere.

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:40.680
They know that we can think
on our own, we know the truth,

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:42.630
and everybody kind of
speaks in this way.

00:22:42.630 --> 00:22:44.560
Everybody in the
United States says,

00:22:44.560 --> 00:22:46.080
well, yes, there's
mainstream media,

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:49.350
they're full of messaging and
propaganda, but we're smart.

00:22:49.350 --> 00:22:50.860
We can think on our own.

00:22:50.860 --> 00:22:52.410
Of course, we see through that.

00:22:52.410 --> 00:22:53.940
Everybody says this.

00:22:53.940 --> 00:22:56.130
And then the conclusion
of their thought

00:22:56.130 --> 00:23:00.510
is often hatred towards some
group, whatever that group is.

00:23:00.510 --> 00:23:02.160
And the more you've
lost, the more

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:04.480
intense the feeling of hatred.

00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:10.660
It's a really
difficult field to walk

00:23:10.660 --> 00:23:13.420
through calmly and
with an open mind

00:23:13.420 --> 00:23:17.140
and try to understand
what's really going on.

00:23:17.140 --> 00:23:19.270
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
It's super intense.

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:21.850
That's the only words that
come to mind as I hear this.

00:23:21.850 --> 00:23:25.540
You mentioned something that
it seems that hate generalizes.

00:23:25.540 --> 00:23:30.860
It's against an entire
group or an entire country.

00:23:30.860 --> 00:23:35.260
Why do you think it is that
hate generalizes and that love

00:23:35.260 --> 00:23:37.900
may or may not generalize?

00:23:37.900 --> 00:23:39.400
LEX FRIDMAN: I've
had-- sort of one,

00:23:39.400 --> 00:23:44.040
as you can imagine, the
kind of question I asked

00:23:44.040 --> 00:23:48.710
is, do you have love
or hate in your heart?

00:23:48.710 --> 00:23:50.800
It's a question I
asked almost everybody.

00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:52.930
And then I would dig
into this exact question

00:23:52.930 --> 00:23:55.740
that you're asking.

00:23:55.740 --> 00:23:58.500
I think some of the most
beautiful things I've

00:23:58.500 --> 00:24:00.930
heard which is people
that are full of hate

00:24:00.930 --> 00:24:03.990
are able to
self-introspect about it.

00:24:03.990 --> 00:24:07.440
They know they shouldn't feel
it, but they can't help it.

00:24:07.440 --> 00:24:10.560
It's not-- they know that
ultimately the thing that

00:24:10.560 --> 00:24:15.210
helps them and helps everyone
is to feel love for fellow man,

00:24:15.210 --> 00:24:18.790
but they can't help it.

00:24:18.790 --> 00:24:19.470
They know.

00:24:19.470 --> 00:24:23.700
It's like a drug, they
say like hate escalates,

00:24:23.700 --> 00:24:25.590
it's like a vicious spiral.

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:27.430
You just can't help it.

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:30.300
And the question I
also asked is, do you

00:24:30.300 --> 00:24:33.610
think you'll ever be
able to forgive Russia?

00:24:33.610 --> 00:24:38.680
And after much thought almost--

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:43.650
it's split, but most
people will say no.

00:24:43.650 --> 00:24:45.737
I will never be able to forgive.

00:24:45.737 --> 00:24:47.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And because
of the generalization

00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:50.195
you talked about earlier, that
could even include all Ru--

00:24:50.195 --> 00:24:50.470
LEX FRIDMAN: All Russians.

00:24:50.470 --> 00:24:50.885
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
In that statements,

00:24:50.885 --> 00:24:52.050
they mean all Russians.

00:24:52.050 --> 00:24:59.340
LEX FRIDMAN: Because
if you do nothing

00:24:59.340 --> 00:25:06.750
that's as bad or worse
than being part of the army

00:25:06.750 --> 00:25:07.780
that invades.

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:09.750
So the people that are
just sitting there,

00:25:09.750 --> 00:25:12.750
the good Germans, the people
that are just quietly going

00:25:12.750 --> 00:25:16.590
on with their lives, you're
just as bad, if not worse,

00:25:16.590 --> 00:25:18.353
is their perspective.

00:25:18.353 --> 00:25:19.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Earlier, you said

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:24.510
that going over
to the Ukraine now

00:25:24.510 --> 00:25:26.850
allowed you to
realize just so many

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:30.150
of the positives of being
here in the United States.

00:25:30.150 --> 00:25:31.422
I have a good friend.

00:25:31.422 --> 00:25:32.130
We both know him.

00:25:32.130 --> 00:25:33.960
I won't name him
by name, but we've

00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:36.240
communicated the
three of us from

00:25:36.240 --> 00:25:37.710
tier-one Special Operations.

00:25:37.710 --> 00:25:39.600
He spent years
doing deployments.

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:41.460
Really amazing individual.

00:25:41.460 --> 00:25:46.830
And I remember when the pandemic
hit, he said on a text thread

00:25:46.830 --> 00:25:49.740
you know, Americans aren't used
to the government interfering

00:25:49.740 --> 00:25:50.820
with their plans.

00:25:50.820 --> 00:25:53.340
Around the world,
many people are

00:25:53.340 --> 00:25:56.352
familiar with governments
dramatically interfering

00:25:56.352 --> 00:25:57.060
with their plans.

00:25:57.060 --> 00:25:59.760
Sometimes even in a
seemingly random way.

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:02.880
Here we were not
braced for that.

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:06.690
I mean, we get speeding
tickets, and there's lines

00:26:06.690 --> 00:26:07.930
to vote and things like that.

00:26:07.930 --> 00:26:10.477
But I think the pandemic
was one of the first times,

00:26:10.477 --> 00:26:12.810
at least in my life, that I
can remember where it really

00:26:12.810 --> 00:26:15.750
seemed like the government was
impeding what people naturally

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:16.800
wanted to do.

00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:19.710
And that was a shock
for people here.

00:26:19.710 --> 00:26:23.580
And I have a what might
seem like a somewhat

00:26:23.580 --> 00:26:25.080
mundane question,
but it's something

00:26:25.080 --> 00:26:26.220
that I saw on social media.

00:26:26.220 --> 00:26:29.490
A lot of people were
asking me to ask you,

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:31.920
and I was curious about too.

00:26:31.920 --> 00:26:34.170
What was a typical
day like over there?

00:26:34.170 --> 00:26:37.648
Were sleeping in a bed, were
you sleeping on the ground?

00:26:37.648 --> 00:26:38.940
Everyone seems to want to know.

00:26:38.940 --> 00:26:39.660
What were you eating?

00:26:39.660 --> 00:26:40.785
Were you eating once a day?

00:26:40.785 --> 00:26:42.090
Were you eating your steak?

00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:45.630
Or were you-- were you in fairly
deprived conditions over there?

00:26:45.630 --> 00:26:50.550
I saw a couple photos
that you posted out

00:26:50.550 --> 00:26:53.550
of doors in front of rubble.

00:26:53.550 --> 00:26:55.080
With pith helmet on in one case.

00:26:55.080 --> 00:27:00.870
What was that typical
day like over there?

00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:03.250
LEX FRIDMAN: So
there's two modes.

00:27:03.250 --> 00:27:06.210
One of them-- I spent
a lot of time in Kyiv,

00:27:06.210 --> 00:27:11.785
which is much safer than--

00:27:11.785 --> 00:27:14.160
it may be obvious to state
but for people who don't know,

00:27:14.160 --> 00:27:15.577
it's in the middle
of the country,

00:27:15.577 --> 00:27:18.380
and it's much safer
than the actual front.

00:27:18.380 --> 00:27:20.070
The word the battle
is happening.

00:27:20.070 --> 00:27:22.920
So much, much safer
than Kyiv even

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:25.630
is Lviv which is the
Western part of the country.

00:27:25.630 --> 00:27:27.480
So the times I
spent in Kyiv were

00:27:27.480 --> 00:27:30.250
fundamentally different than
the time I spent at the front.

00:27:30.250 --> 00:27:32.860
And I went to the
Kherson region,

00:27:32.860 --> 00:27:35.983
which is where a lot of really
heated battle was happening.

00:27:35.983 --> 00:27:36.900
There's several areas.

00:27:36.900 --> 00:27:38.610
So there's Kharkiv.

00:27:38.610 --> 00:27:40.710
It's in the Northeast
of the country.

00:27:40.710 --> 00:27:43.860
And then there's Donbas region,
which is East of the country.

00:27:43.860 --> 00:27:47.370
And then there's Kherson
region, which, by the way,

00:27:47.370 --> 00:27:52.587
I'm not good at geography, so
is the Southeast of the country.

00:27:52.587 --> 00:27:54.420
And that's where, at
least when I was there,

00:27:54.420 --> 00:27:57.100
was a lot of really
heated fighting happening.

00:27:57.100 --> 00:27:59.370
So when I was in
the Kherson region,

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:04.370
it's what you would imagine.

00:28:04.370 --> 00:28:07.220
The place-- I stayed in a
hotel where all the lights

00:28:07.220 --> 00:28:08.540
have to stay off.

00:28:08.540 --> 00:28:11.720
So the entire town,
all the lights are off.

00:28:11.720 --> 00:28:13.790
You have to navigate
through the darkness

00:28:13.790 --> 00:28:15.715
and use your phone
to shine, and so on.

00:28:15.715 --> 00:28:17.840
ANDREW HUBERMAN: This is
terrible for the circadian

00:28:17.840 --> 00:28:18.320
system.

00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:18.710
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:28:18.710 --> 00:28:19.350
LEX FRIDMAN: That's exactly--

00:28:19.350 --> 00:28:20.960
I was this-- how can I do this?

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:23.210
Where's my element
and Athletic Greens?

00:28:23.210 --> 00:28:24.440
How can I function?

00:28:24.440 --> 00:28:26.870
No.

00:28:26.870 --> 00:28:31.090
There's I think it was balanced
by the deep appreciation

00:28:31.090 --> 00:28:31.715
of being alive.

00:28:31.715 --> 00:28:32.490
[LAUGHTER]

00:28:32.490 --> 00:28:33.990
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Right now I-- mean,

00:28:33.990 --> 00:28:35.050
this is the reason
that I asked--

00:28:35.050 --> 00:28:35.330
LEX FRIDMAN: Stress-wise.

00:28:35.330 --> 00:28:36.788
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
This is the reason

00:28:36.788 --> 00:28:39.362
I ask is we get used to all
these creature comforts.

00:28:39.362 --> 00:28:40.070
LEX FRIDMAN: Yes.

00:28:40.070 --> 00:28:43.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
we don't need them,

00:28:43.310 --> 00:28:45.410
but we often come
to depend on them

00:28:45.410 --> 00:28:47.660
in a way that makes us
feel like we need them.

00:28:47.660 --> 00:28:49.610
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah,
but very quickly,

00:28:49.610 --> 00:28:54.965
there's something about
the intensity of life

00:28:54.965 --> 00:28:56.840
that you see in people's
eyes because they're

00:28:56.840 --> 00:28:59.060
living through a war that makes
you forget all those creature

00:28:59.060 --> 00:28:59.870
comforts.

00:28:59.870 --> 00:29:01.940
And it was actually--

00:29:01.940 --> 00:29:03.920
I'm somebody who hates
traveling and so on.

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:05.930
I love the creature habits.

00:29:05.930 --> 00:29:10.070
I love-- I love the
comfort of the ritual right

00:29:10.070 --> 00:29:12.230
but all of that was
forgotten very quickly.

00:29:12.230 --> 00:29:14.900
Just the intensity of
feeling, the intensity of

00:29:14.900 --> 00:29:18.290
love that people have for
each other, that was obvious.

00:29:18.290 --> 00:29:19.350
In terms of food--

00:29:19.350 --> 00:29:21.410
so there's a curfew.

00:29:21.410 --> 00:29:24.530
So depends on what
part of the country.

00:29:24.530 --> 00:29:28.880
But usually, you basically have
to scamper home like 9:00 PM.

00:29:28.880 --> 00:29:34.070
So the hard curfew in a lot of
places is 11:00 PM at night.

00:29:34.070 --> 00:29:37.670
But by then, you
have to be home.

00:29:37.670 --> 00:29:40.320
So-- in some places, it's 10:00.

00:29:40.320 --> 00:29:43.050
So at 9:00 PM, you
start going home.

00:29:43.050 --> 00:29:45.440
Which, for me, was
kind of wonderful

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:49.310
also because I get to spend--

00:29:49.310 --> 00:29:55.280
I get to be forced to spend time
alone and think for many hours

00:29:55.280 --> 00:29:57.140
in wherever I'm staying.

00:29:57.140 --> 00:29:58.100
Which is really nice.

00:29:58.100 --> 00:30:00.225
And everywhere there's a
calmness and the quietness

00:30:00.225 --> 00:30:01.160
to the whole thing.

00:30:01.160 --> 00:30:04.430
In terms of food, once a day.

00:30:04.430 --> 00:30:10.050
Just the food is incredibly
cheap and incredibly delicious.

00:30:10.050 --> 00:30:11.730
People are still--
one of the things

00:30:11.730 --> 00:30:16.800
they can still take pride
in is making the best

00:30:16.800 --> 00:30:18.180
possible food they can.

00:30:18.180 --> 00:30:22.712
So meat-- but they do
admire American meat,

00:30:22.712 --> 00:30:25.170
so the meat is not as great as
it could be in that country.

00:30:25.170 --> 00:30:28.350
But I ate borsch every day,
all that kind of stuff.

00:30:28.350 --> 00:30:30.210
Mostly meat.

00:30:30.210 --> 00:30:32.490
So spend the entire day--

00:30:32.490 --> 00:30:34.530
wake up in the
morning with coffee,

00:30:34.530 --> 00:30:36.520
spend the entire day
talking to people.

00:30:36.520 --> 00:30:39.630
Which for me is very
difficult because

00:30:39.630 --> 00:30:40.880
of the intensity of the story.

00:30:40.880 --> 00:30:42.690
It's one after the
other after the other.

00:30:42.690 --> 00:30:45.140
We just talk to regular
people, talk to soldiers,

00:30:45.140 --> 00:30:49.850
talk to politicians,
all kinds of soldiers.

00:30:49.850 --> 00:30:53.690
I talked to people there who
are doing rescue missions, so

00:30:53.690 --> 00:30:55.295
Americans.

00:30:55.295 --> 00:30:57.887
I hung out with Tim Kennedy.

00:30:57.887 --> 00:30:58.970
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, yeah?

00:30:58.970 --> 00:31:00.260
The great Tim Kennedy.

00:31:00.260 --> 00:31:03.530
LEX FRIDMAN: The great
Tim Kennedy, who--

00:31:03.530 --> 00:31:08.780
also him and many
others revealed to me

00:31:08.780 --> 00:31:10.850
one of the many
reasons I'm proud to be

00:31:10.850 --> 00:31:18.230
an American is how trained
and skilled and effective

00:31:18.230 --> 00:31:19.640
American soldiers are.

00:31:19.640 --> 00:31:21.560
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I guess for
listeners of this podcast maybe

00:31:21.560 --> 00:31:22.970
we should familiarize
them with who

00:31:22.970 --> 00:31:25.100
Tim Kennedy is because I
realized that a number of them

00:31:25.100 --> 00:31:25.550
will know, but--

00:31:25.550 --> 00:31:26.883
LEX FRIDMAN: How do you do that?

00:31:26.883 --> 00:31:28.430
How do you try to
summarize a man?

00:31:28.430 --> 00:31:29.388
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

00:31:31.340 --> 00:31:33.380
We can be accurate
but not exhaustive,

00:31:33.380 --> 00:31:38.150
as any good data are
accurate but not exhaustive.

00:31:38.150 --> 00:31:40.880
Very skilled and accomplished
MMA fighter, very skilled

00:31:40.880 --> 00:31:44.750
and accomplished former
Special Operations member,

00:31:44.750 --> 00:31:47.870
American Patriot, and
podcaster too, right?

00:31:47.870 --> 00:31:49.490
Does he have his own podcast?

00:31:49.490 --> 00:31:50.450
LEX FRIDMAN: Maybe.

00:31:50.450 --> 00:31:51.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN: OK.
LEX FRIDMAN: Maybe.

00:31:51.350 --> 00:31:53.180
ANDREW HUBERMAN: We know Andy
Stumpf has his own podcast.

00:31:53.180 --> 00:31:53.660
LEX FRIDMAN: Yes.
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:31:53.660 --> 00:31:54.740
LEX FRIDMAN: Which is
an amazing podcast.

00:31:54.740 --> 00:31:55.550
Yeah, Andy's great.

00:31:55.550 --> 00:31:56.900
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

00:31:56.900 --> 00:31:58.700
Clearing Hot podcast
with Andy Stumpf.

00:31:58.700 --> 00:32:00.075
LEX FRIDMAN: But
also Tim Kennedy

00:32:00.075 --> 00:32:03.950
is like the embodiment of
America to the most beautiful

00:32:03.950 --> 00:32:05.540
and the most ridiculous degree.

00:32:05.540 --> 00:32:10.030
So he's like what you imagine--

00:32:10.030 --> 00:32:13.900
what is it, Team America?

00:32:13.900 --> 00:32:16.960
I just imagine him
shirtless on a tank rolling

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:18.960
into enemy territory
just screaming

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:19.960
at the top of his lungs.

00:32:19.960 --> 00:32:21.430
That's just his personality.

00:32:21.430 --> 00:32:23.015
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
But not posturing.

00:32:23.015 --> 00:32:24.640
He actually does the
work, as they say.

00:32:24.640 --> 00:32:26.057
LEX FRIDMAN: So
this is the thing.

00:32:26.057 --> 00:32:27.730
He really embodies that.

00:32:27.730 --> 00:32:31.030
Now, some of that is just
his personality and humor.

00:32:31.030 --> 00:32:33.220
I'd like to sort of comment
on the humor of things,

00:32:33.220 --> 00:32:34.330
not just with him.

00:32:34.330 --> 00:32:36.670
There's very one other
interesting thing I've learned.

00:32:36.670 --> 00:32:39.520
But also when he's
actually helping people

00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:41.780
he's extremely good
at what he does,

00:32:41.780 --> 00:32:44.590
which is building
teams that rescue, that

00:32:44.590 --> 00:32:48.640
go into the most dangerous areas
of Ukraine, dangerous areas

00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:50.960
anywhere else, and
they get the job done.

00:32:50.960 --> 00:32:54.680
And one of the things I
heard time and time again,

00:32:54.680 --> 00:32:57.600
which what's really
interesting to me,

00:32:57.600 --> 00:33:02.840
that Ukrainian soldiers said
that comparing Ukrainian,

00:33:02.840 --> 00:33:06.050
Russian, and American
soldiers, American soldiers

00:33:06.050 --> 00:33:09.140
are the bravest, which was
very interesting for me

00:33:09.140 --> 00:33:10.910
to hear given how
high the morale is

00:33:10.910 --> 00:33:12.260
for the Ukrainian soldiers.

00:33:12.260 --> 00:33:14.540
But that just
reveals that training

00:33:14.540 --> 00:33:17.310
enables you to be brave.

00:33:17.310 --> 00:33:20.180
So it's not just about how well
trained they are and so on,

00:33:20.180 --> 00:33:23.300
it's how intense and ferocious
they are in the fighting.

00:33:23.300 --> 00:33:26.900
And it makes you realize,
this is American army,

00:33:26.900 --> 00:33:30.990
not just through the technology,
especially the special force

00:33:30.990 --> 00:33:31.490
guys.

00:33:31.490 --> 00:33:34.460
They're still one of
the most effective

00:33:34.460 --> 00:33:36.660
and terrifying
armies in the world.

00:33:36.660 --> 00:33:39.110
And listen, just
for context, I'm

00:33:39.110 --> 00:33:44.250
somebody who is, for the most
part, anti-war, a pacifist.

00:33:44.250 --> 00:33:49.810
But you get to see some of the
realities of war kind of wake

00:33:49.810 --> 00:33:55.870
you up to what needs to get
done to protect sovereignty,

00:33:55.870 --> 00:34:00.645
to protect some of the values,
to protect civilians and homes

00:34:00.645 --> 00:34:01.770
and all that kind of stuff.

00:34:01.770 --> 00:34:04.430
Sometimes war has to happen.

00:34:04.430 --> 00:34:06.180
And I should also
mention the Russian side

00:34:06.180 --> 00:34:11.300
because while I haven't gotten
to experience the Russian side

00:34:11.300 --> 00:34:15.469
yet I do fully plan to travel to
Russia, as I've told everybody.

00:34:15.469 --> 00:34:18.870
I was very upfront with
everybody about this.

00:34:18.870 --> 00:34:21.139
I would like to hear
the story of Russians.

00:34:21.139 --> 00:34:24.710
But I do know from the Ukrainian
side, like the grandmas--

00:34:24.710 --> 00:34:25.969
I love grandmas.

00:34:25.969 --> 00:34:28.699
They told me stories that
the Russians really--

00:34:28.699 --> 00:34:32.870
the ones that entered their
villages, they really, really

00:34:32.870 --> 00:34:35.210
believed they're saving
Ukraine from Nazis,

00:34:35.210 --> 00:34:37.380
from Nazi occupation.

00:34:37.380 --> 00:34:42.440
So they feel that
Ukraine is under control

00:34:42.440 --> 00:34:45.650
of Nazi organizations
and they believe

00:34:45.650 --> 00:34:47.319
they're saving
the country that's

00:34:47.319 --> 00:34:48.444
their brothers and sisters.

00:34:52.100 --> 00:34:59.510
I think propaganda and I think
truth is a very difficult thing

00:34:59.510 --> 00:35:01.530
to arrive with in that war zone.

00:35:01.530 --> 00:35:03.560
I think in the 21st
century one of the things

00:35:03.560 --> 00:35:07.640
you realize that so much of war,
even more so than in the past,

00:35:07.640 --> 00:35:09.680
is an information war.

00:35:09.680 --> 00:35:14.120
And people that just use Twitter
for their source of information

00:35:14.120 --> 00:35:17.540
might be surprised to know
how much misinformation there

00:35:17.540 --> 00:35:21.950
is on Twitter, like real
narratives being sold,

00:35:21.950 --> 00:35:25.040
and so it's really hard
to know who to believe.

00:35:25.040 --> 00:35:28.760
And through all of that you
have to try to keep an open mind

00:35:28.760 --> 00:35:32.330
and ultimately
ignore the powerful

00:35:32.330 --> 00:35:35.030
and listen to actual
citizens, actual people.

00:35:35.030 --> 00:35:37.400
That's the other
maybe obvious lesson

00:35:37.400 --> 00:35:43.900
is that war is waged by
powerful, rich people,

00:35:43.900 --> 00:35:46.760
and it's the poor
people that suffer.

00:35:46.760 --> 00:35:50.410
And that's just visible
time and time again.

00:35:50.410 --> 00:35:53.290
