00:46:17.900 --> 00:46:19.310
I wanted to see how much that--

00:46:19.310 --> 00:46:20.180
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh my goodness.

00:46:20.180 --> 00:46:21.170
LEX FRIDMAN: Riding
the crypto wave.

00:46:21.170 --> 00:46:23.587
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, your
children, your future children

00:46:23.587 --> 00:46:26.270
and my future children, are
supposed to do jujitsu together

00:46:26.270 --> 00:46:28.160
since I've only done
the one jujitsu class

00:46:28.160 --> 00:46:31.820
so I'm strongly vested
in you having children.

00:46:31.820 --> 00:46:32.840
LEX FRIDMAN: Yes.

00:46:32.840 --> 00:46:36.020
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But only
in the friendly kind of way.

00:46:36.020 --> 00:46:37.315
LEX FRIDMAN: Well, yes.

00:46:37.315 --> 00:46:38.690
Friendly competition
kind of way.

00:46:38.690 --> 00:46:39.380
Yeah.

00:46:39.380 --> 00:46:40.980
Dominance of the clan.

00:46:40.980 --> 00:46:42.800
Yep.

00:46:42.800 --> 00:46:44.910
For sure.

00:46:44.910 --> 00:46:49.100
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So moving
on to science, but still

00:46:49.100 --> 00:46:51.260
with our minds in the Ukraine.

00:46:51.260 --> 00:46:56.080
Did you encounter any scientists
or see any universities?

00:46:56.080 --> 00:46:59.900
As we know, in this country
and in Europe and elsewhere,

00:46:59.900 --> 00:47:01.970
science takes infrastructure.

00:47:01.970 --> 00:47:04.610
You need buildings, you need
laboratories, you need robots,

00:47:04.610 --> 00:47:09.680
you need a lot of equipment,
and you need minus 80 freezers

00:47:09.680 --> 00:47:12.080
and you need incubators
and you need money

00:47:12.080 --> 00:47:13.370
and you need technicians.

00:47:13.370 --> 00:47:15.650
And typically it's been
the wealthier countries

00:47:15.650 --> 00:47:18.380
that have been able to
do more research for sake

00:47:18.380 --> 00:47:22.640
of research and development
and prioritization.

00:47:22.640 --> 00:47:25.250
Certainly the Ukraine had
some marvelous universities

00:47:25.250 --> 00:47:28.440
and marvelous scientists.

00:47:28.440 --> 00:47:32.930
What's going on with science
and scientists over there?

00:47:32.930 --> 00:47:37.100
And gosh, can we even
calculate the loss of discovery

00:47:37.100 --> 00:47:40.500
that is occurring as a
consequence of this conflict?

00:47:40.500 --> 00:47:44.540
LEX FRIDMAN: So science goes on.

00:47:44.540 --> 00:47:48.020
Before the war Ukraine had
a very vibrant tech sector,

00:47:48.020 --> 00:47:51.440
which means engineering
and all that kind of stuff,

00:47:51.440 --> 00:47:53.390
and Kyiv has a lot of
excellent universities

00:47:53.390 --> 00:47:55.070
and they still go on.

00:47:55.070 --> 00:47:58.340
The biggest hit, I
would say, is not

00:47:58.340 --> 00:47:59.760
the infrastructure
of the science,

00:47:59.760 --> 00:48:02.910
but the fact, because
of the high morale,

00:48:02.910 --> 00:48:05.400
everybody is joining
the military.

00:48:05.400 --> 00:48:06.870
So everybody is
going to the front

00:48:06.870 --> 00:48:11.040
to fight, including you, Andrew
Huberman, would be fighting,

00:48:11.040 --> 00:48:14.100
and not because you have
to but because you want to.

00:48:14.100 --> 00:48:15.960
And everybody you know
would be really proud

00:48:15.960 --> 00:48:19.080
that you're fighting,
even though everyone tries

00:48:19.080 --> 00:48:21.480
to convince, Andrew
Huberman, you

00:48:21.480 --> 00:48:24.700
have much better
ways to contribute.

00:48:24.700 --> 00:48:26.970
There's deep honor in fighting
for your country, yes,

00:48:26.970 --> 00:48:30.360
but there are better ways to
contribute to your country

00:48:30.360 --> 00:48:34.080
than just picking up a gun that
you're not that trained with

00:48:34.080 --> 00:48:35.310
and going to the front.

00:48:35.310 --> 00:48:37.650
Still, they do it.

00:48:37.650 --> 00:48:43.078
Scientists, engineers,
CEOs, professors, students--

00:48:43.078 --> 00:48:44.370
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Men and women?

00:48:44.370 --> 00:48:46.290
LEX FRIDMAN: Actors--
men and women.

00:48:46.290 --> 00:48:50.910
Obviously, primarily
men, but men and women.

00:48:50.910 --> 00:48:53.640
Much more than you would
see in other militaries,

00:48:53.640 --> 00:48:55.110
women are-- everybody.

00:48:55.110 --> 00:48:56.310
Everybody wants to fight.

00:48:56.310 --> 00:48:57.810
Everybody's proud of fighting.

00:48:57.810 --> 00:49:02.930
There's no discussion
of pacifism.

00:49:02.930 --> 00:49:04.090
Should we be fighting?

00:49:04.090 --> 00:49:04.990
Is this right?

00:49:04.990 --> 00:49:10.090
Is this-- everybody's
really proud of fighting.

00:49:10.090 --> 00:49:11.530
So there's this
kind of black hole

00:49:11.530 --> 00:49:13.990
that pulls everything,
all the resources,

00:49:13.990 --> 00:49:18.070
into the war effort that's
not just financial but also

00:49:18.070 --> 00:49:19.060
psychological.

00:49:19.060 --> 00:49:24.480
So it's like if you're a
scientist it feels like what--

00:49:24.480 --> 00:49:32.100
it feels almost like
you're dishonoring humanity

00:49:32.100 --> 00:49:35.730
by continuing to do things
you were doing before.

00:49:35.730 --> 00:49:38.160
There's a lot of people that
converted to being soldiers.

00:49:38.160 --> 00:49:40.890
They literally watch
a YouTube video

00:49:40.890 --> 00:49:43.210
of how to shoot
a particular gun,

00:49:43.210 --> 00:49:46.020
how to arm a drone
with a grenade.

00:49:46.020 --> 00:49:48.000
If you're a tech
person you know how

00:49:48.000 --> 00:49:50.310
to work with drones so
you're going to use that,

00:49:50.310 --> 00:49:53.400
use whatever skills you got,
figure out whatever skills

00:49:53.400 --> 00:49:57.210
you got and how to use them to
help the effort on the front.

00:49:57.210 --> 00:49:59.160
And so that's a big hit.

00:49:59.160 --> 00:50:02.700
But that said, I've talked
to a lot of folks in Kyiv--

00:50:02.700 --> 00:50:06.390
faculty primarily in the
tech economics space,

00:50:06.390 --> 00:50:08.820
so I didn't get a chance
to interact with folks who

00:50:08.820 --> 00:50:13.740
are on the biology, chemistry,
neuroscience side of things,

00:50:13.740 --> 00:50:15.130
but that still goes on.

00:50:15.130 --> 00:50:18.510
So one of the really
impressive things about Ukraine

00:50:18.510 --> 00:50:22.350
is that they're able to maintain
infrastructure like road, food

00:50:22.350 --> 00:50:27.060
supply, all that kind of stuff,
education, while the war is

00:50:27.060 --> 00:50:28.740
going on, especially in Kyiv.

00:50:28.740 --> 00:50:32.070
The war started
where nobody knew

00:50:32.070 --> 00:50:34.870
whether Kyiv was going to be
taken by the Russian forces.

00:50:34.870 --> 00:50:36.780
It was surrounded.

00:50:36.780 --> 00:50:42.770
And a lot of experts from
outside were convinced that

00:50:42.770 --> 00:50:45.660
Russia would take
Kyiv, and they didn't.

00:50:45.660 --> 00:50:49.047
And one of the really
impressive things as a leader--

00:50:49.047 --> 00:50:50.630
one of the things I
really experienced

00:50:50.630 --> 00:50:53.330
is that a lot of people
criticized Zelenskyy

00:50:53.330 --> 00:50:54.860
before the war.

00:50:54.860 --> 00:50:57.140
He only had about
30% approval rate.

00:50:57.140 --> 00:50:59.510
A lot of people
didn't like Zelenskyy.

00:50:59.510 --> 00:51:02.960
But one of the
great things he did

00:51:02.960 --> 00:51:05.660
as a leader, which I'm not
sure many leaders would

00:51:05.660 --> 00:51:11.060
be able to do, is when Kyiv
was clearly being invaded he

00:51:11.060 --> 00:51:13.040
chose to stay.

00:51:13.040 --> 00:51:14.330
He stayed in the capital.

00:51:14.330 --> 00:51:19.430
Everybody, all the American
military, the intelligence

00:51:19.430 --> 00:51:24.170
agencies, NATO, his own staff,
advisors all told him to flee,

00:51:24.170 --> 00:51:25.440
and he stayed.

00:51:25.440 --> 00:51:30.073
And so I think that was a
beacon, a symbol for the rest,

00:51:30.073 --> 00:51:31.490
for the universities,
for science,

00:51:31.490 --> 00:51:34.610
for the infrastructure
that we're staying too,

00:51:34.610 --> 00:51:37.820
and that kept the
whole thing going.

00:51:37.820 --> 00:51:41.970
There's an interesting social
experiment that happened,

00:51:41.970 --> 00:51:44.040
I think for folks who
are interested in gun

00:51:44.040 --> 00:51:46.330
control in this
country in particular,

00:51:46.330 --> 00:51:50.340
is one of the decisions
they made early on

00:51:50.340 --> 00:51:53.350
is to give guns to everybody.

00:51:53.350 --> 00:51:54.370
Semi-automatics.

00:51:54.370 --> 00:51:55.510
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Early on in the war?

00:51:55.510 --> 00:51:57.135
LEX FRIDMAN: Early
on in the war, yeah.

00:51:57.135 --> 00:51:59.240
So everybody got a gun.

00:51:59.240 --> 00:52:00.910
They also released
a bunch of prisoners

00:52:00.910 --> 00:52:03.940
from prison because
there was no staff

00:52:03.940 --> 00:52:09.110
to keep the prisons running.

00:52:09.110 --> 00:52:11.990
And so there's a
very interesting

00:52:11.990 --> 00:52:15.890
psychological experiment of,
like, how is this going to go?

00:52:15.890 --> 00:52:17.385
Everybody has a gun.

00:52:17.385 --> 00:52:19.010
Are they going to
start robbing places?

00:52:19.010 --> 00:52:20.677
Are they going to
start taking advantage

00:52:20.677 --> 00:52:21.950
of a chaotic situation?

00:52:21.950 --> 00:52:26.480
And what happened is
that crime went to zero.

00:52:26.480 --> 00:52:29.540
So it turned out that
this, as an experiment,

00:52:29.540 --> 00:52:30.710
worked wonderfully.

00:52:30.710 --> 00:52:32.960
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's a
case where love generalized.

00:52:32.960 --> 00:52:33.470
LEX FRIDMAN: Yes.

00:52:33.470 --> 00:52:34.790
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Or
at least hate did not.

00:52:34.790 --> 00:52:36.800
We don't know if it's
love or it's sort of lack

00:52:36.800 --> 00:52:40.070
of initiative for common
culture directed hate.

00:52:40.070 --> 00:52:40.820
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.

00:52:40.820 --> 00:52:43.280
I don't-- right.

00:52:43.280 --> 00:52:46.520
I think that's very correct
to say that it wasn't

00:52:46.520 --> 00:52:48.350
hate that was unifying people.

00:52:48.350 --> 00:52:50.930
It was love of country,
love of community.

00:52:50.930 --> 00:52:53.840
It's probably the same thing
that will happen to humans when

00:52:53.840 --> 00:52:56.570
aliens invade as well.

00:52:56.570 --> 00:52:57.890
It's the common effort.

00:52:57.890 --> 00:53:00.350
Everybody puts everything
else to the side.

00:53:00.350 --> 00:53:04.880
Plus just the sheer amount
of guns is similar to Texas.

00:53:04.880 --> 00:53:07.760
You realize, well,
there's going to be

00:53:07.760 --> 00:53:11.300
a self-correcting mechanism very
quickly because the rule of law

00:53:11.300 --> 00:53:14.680
was also put aside, right?

00:53:14.680 --> 00:53:18.400
Basically the police
force lost a lot of power

00:53:18.400 --> 00:53:20.230
because everybody else
has guns and they're

00:53:20.230 --> 00:53:23.140
kind of taking the law
into their own hands.

00:53:23.140 --> 00:53:25.780
That system, at least
in this particular case

00:53:25.780 --> 00:53:29.710
in this particular moment
in human history, worked.

00:53:29.710 --> 00:53:32.952
It's an interesting
lesson, you know?

00:53:32.952 --> 00:53:33.910
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It is.

00:53:33.910 --> 00:53:36.970
I had an interesting contrast
that I'll share with you

00:53:36.970 --> 00:53:38.200
because you mentioned Texas.

00:53:38.200 --> 00:53:40.195
So not so long ago
I was in Austin.

00:53:40.195 --> 00:53:42.970
I often visit you or others
in Austin, as you know.

00:53:42.970 --> 00:53:46.840
And many doors that I walked
past, including a school,

00:53:46.840 --> 00:53:50.312
said no firearms
past this point.

00:53:50.312 --> 00:53:51.520
It was a sticker on the door.

00:53:51.520 --> 00:53:53.080
You see this on
hospitals sometimes.

00:53:53.080 --> 00:53:56.560
I saw this at Baylor College
of Medicine, et cetera.

00:53:56.560 --> 00:53:59.110
Relatively common
to see in Texas,

00:53:59.110 --> 00:54:01.690
not so common in California.

00:54:01.690 --> 00:54:04.810
And then I flew to the
San Francisco Bay Area,

00:54:04.810 --> 00:54:08.410
was walking by an elementary
school in my old neighborhood,

00:54:08.410 --> 00:54:10.300
and saw a similar
sticker and looked at it

00:54:10.300 --> 00:54:14.830
and it said, no peanuts
or other allergy

00:54:14.830 --> 00:54:17.407
containing foods past
this point on the door

00:54:17.407 --> 00:54:18.490
of this elementary school.

00:54:18.490 --> 00:54:21.940
So quite a different
contrast, guns and peanuts.

00:54:21.940 --> 00:54:24.370
Now, peanut allergies,
obviously, are very serious

00:54:24.370 --> 00:54:26.537
for some people, although
there's great research out

00:54:26.537 --> 00:54:29.470
of Stanford showing that
early exposure to peanuts

00:54:29.470 --> 00:54:31.450
can prevent the allergies.

00:54:31.450 --> 00:54:34.000
But don't start rubbing yourself
in peanut butter, folks,

00:54:34.000 --> 00:54:34.990
if you have a peanut allergy.

00:54:34.990 --> 00:54:36.657
That's not the best
way to deal with it.

00:54:36.657 --> 00:54:38.350
In any case, the
contrast of what's

00:54:38.350 --> 00:54:44.710
dangerous, the contrast of
the familiarity with guns

00:54:44.710 --> 00:54:46.090
versus no familiarity.

00:54:46.090 --> 00:54:49.780
In Israel and elsewhere you see
machine guns in the airport.

00:54:49.780 --> 00:54:52.540
In Germany, Frankfurt, you see
machine guns in the airport.

00:54:52.540 --> 00:54:54.910
Not so common in
the United States.

00:54:54.910 --> 00:54:58.000
So again, I feel like there's
this aperture of vision.

00:54:58.000 --> 00:55:01.570
There's this aperture of
pleasures versus creature

00:55:01.570 --> 00:55:04.120
comforts and lack of
creature comforts,

00:55:04.120 --> 00:55:07.695
and then there's this
aperture of danger, right?

00:55:07.695 --> 00:55:09.070
People who are
familiar with guns

00:55:09.070 --> 00:55:10.987
are familiar with people
coming in and setting

00:55:10.987 --> 00:55:13.540
their firearm on the table
and eating dinner, you know?

00:55:13.540 --> 00:55:17.630
But if you're not accustomed
to that it's jarring, right?

00:55:17.630 --> 00:55:19.090
LEX FRIDMAN: I should mention--

00:55:19.090 --> 00:55:21.220
people know this
throughout human history--

00:55:21.220 --> 00:55:27.760
but the human ability
to get assimilated now,

00:55:27.760 --> 00:55:31.490
get used to violence
is incredible.

00:55:31.490 --> 00:55:34.570
So you could be living
in a peaceful time,

00:55:34.570 --> 00:55:38.380
like we're here now, and
there would be one explosion,

00:55:38.380 --> 00:55:40.180
like a 9/11 type of situation.

00:55:40.180 --> 00:55:41.440
That would be a huge shock.

00:55:41.440 --> 00:55:42.190
It's terrifying.

00:55:42.190 --> 00:55:43.310
Everybody freaks out.

00:55:43.310 --> 00:55:47.230
The second one is a huge drop
off in how freaked out you get.

00:55:47.230 --> 00:55:50.650
And in a matter of
days, sometimes hours,

00:55:50.650 --> 00:55:52.330
it becomes the normal.

00:55:52.330 --> 00:55:54.790
I've talked to so many
people in Kharkiv,

00:55:54.790 --> 00:55:58.930
which is one of the towns that's
seen a lot of heated battle.

00:55:58.930 --> 00:56:01.772
You ask them, is it safe there?

00:56:01.772 --> 00:56:02.980
In fact, when I went to the--

00:56:05.510 --> 00:56:07.580
closer and closer to the
war zone you ask people,

00:56:07.580 --> 00:56:09.220
is it safe?

00:56:09.220 --> 00:56:12.530
And their answer's usually,
yeah, it's pretty safe.

00:56:12.530 --> 00:56:15.790
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
all signal to noise.

00:56:15.790 --> 00:56:19.960
LEX FRIDMAN: Nobody has told
me except Western reporters

00:56:19.960 --> 00:56:21.910
sitting in the West
Side of Ukraine,

00:56:21.910 --> 00:56:23.950
it's really dangerous here.

00:56:23.950 --> 00:56:27.460
Everyone's like,
yeah, it's good.

00:56:27.460 --> 00:56:30.100
My uncle just died yesterday.

00:56:30.100 --> 00:56:32.200
He was shot.

00:56:32.200 --> 00:56:34.090
But it's pretty good.

00:56:34.090 --> 00:56:37.246
The farm is still running.

00:56:37.246 --> 00:56:38.950
How do I put it?

00:56:38.950 --> 00:56:40.720
They focus on the
positive, that's one.

00:56:40.720 --> 00:56:42.860
But there's a
deeper truth there,

00:56:42.860 --> 00:56:46.065
which is just get used
to difficult situations

00:56:46.065 --> 00:56:47.440
and the stuff that
make you happy

00:56:47.440 --> 00:56:49.360
and the stuff that
make you upset

00:56:49.360 --> 00:56:51.733
is relative to that new
normal that you establish.

00:56:51.733 --> 00:56:53.650
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
I grew up in California

00:56:53.650 --> 00:56:54.730
and there were a
lot of earthquakes.

00:56:54.730 --> 00:56:56.350
I remember the '89
quake, I remember

00:56:56.350 --> 00:56:58.060
the Embarcadero
Freeway pancaking

00:56:58.060 --> 00:56:59.560
on top of people and cars.

00:56:59.560 --> 00:57:01.562
I remember I moved to
Southern California,

00:57:01.562 --> 00:57:02.770
there was a Northridge quake.

00:57:02.770 --> 00:57:04.645
Wherever I move there
seem to be earthquakes.

00:57:04.645 --> 00:57:06.280
I never worry about
earthquakes, ever.

00:57:06.280 --> 00:57:07.060
I just don't.

00:57:07.060 --> 00:57:09.070
In fact, I don't
like the destruction

00:57:09.070 --> 00:57:10.900
they cause, but every once
in a while an earthquake will

00:57:10.900 --> 00:57:12.070
roll through and it's
kind of exciting.

00:57:12.070 --> 00:57:13.120
It sounds like a
train coming through.

00:57:13.120 --> 00:57:14.710
It's like, wow, like
the Earth is moving.

00:57:14.710 --> 00:57:15.210
You know?

00:57:15.210 --> 00:57:16.930
Again, I don't want
anyone to get harmed,

00:57:16.930 --> 00:57:22.510
but I enjoy a good rumble
coming through nonetheless.

00:57:22.510 --> 00:57:23.570
It's signal to noise.

00:57:23.570 --> 00:57:26.082
But if I saw a
tornado I'd freak out,

00:57:26.082 --> 00:57:28.540
and people from the Midwest
are probably comfortable with--

00:57:28.540 --> 00:57:30.325
Dan Gable, the great
wrestler from the Midwest

00:57:30.325 --> 00:57:31.870
that you know and I've
never met but I have

00:57:31.870 --> 00:57:34.000
great respect for, he's
probably-- sees a tornado

00:57:34.000 --> 00:57:34.780
and is like, ah, yeah.

00:57:34.780 --> 00:57:35.140
Maybe.

00:57:35.140 --> 00:57:35.680
Yeah.

00:57:35.680 --> 00:57:36.460
You know?

00:57:36.460 --> 00:57:40.730
So I think signal
to noise is real.

00:57:40.730 --> 00:57:43.250
Before I neglect,
although I won't

00:57:43.250 --> 00:57:47.690
forget, speaking of signal
to noise and environment,

00:57:47.690 --> 00:57:50.480
you are returning
to or have gone back

00:57:50.480 --> 00:57:54.110
to one of your original
natural habitats,

00:57:54.110 --> 00:57:57.980
which is the Massachusetts
Institute of Technology which

00:57:57.980 --> 00:57:58.480
is--

00:57:58.480 --> 00:57:59.210
LEX FRIDMAN: Natural
habitat, yeah.

00:57:59.210 --> 00:58:00.980
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's actually
difficult to pronounce in full.

00:58:00.980 --> 00:58:02.060
MIT, right?

00:58:02.060 --> 00:58:05.808
So you've been spending some
time there teaching and doing

00:58:05.808 --> 00:58:06.350
other things.

00:58:06.350 --> 00:58:08.390
Tell us what you're up
to with MIT recently.

00:58:08.390 --> 00:58:11.060
LEX FRIDMAN: Well, I'm
really glad that you,

00:58:11.060 --> 00:58:13.740
being on the West Coast, know
the difference between Boston,

00:58:13.740 --> 00:58:14.240
New York.

00:58:14.240 --> 00:58:17.040
I feel like a lot of people
think it's like the East Coast.

00:58:17.040 --> 00:58:19.040
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's
very different, especially

00:58:19.040 --> 00:58:20.397
the Bostonians and New Yorkers.

00:58:20.397 --> 00:58:21.980
LEX FRIDMAN: They
get very aggressive.

00:58:21.980 --> 00:58:22.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh my goodness.

00:58:22.670 --> 00:58:24.470
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah, I love it.

00:58:24.470 --> 00:58:28.385
I gave lectures there in
front of a in person crowd.

00:58:28.385 --> 00:58:30.260
ANDREW HUBERMAN: What
were you talking about?

00:58:30.260 --> 00:58:32.180
LEX FRIDMAN: For the
AI, so different aspects

00:58:32.180 --> 00:58:37.260
of AI and robotics, machine
learning-- machine learning.

00:58:37.260 --> 00:58:40.100
So for people who know the
artificial intelligence field

00:58:40.100 --> 00:58:41.570
they usually don't
use the term AI,

00:58:41.570 --> 00:58:43.700
and people from outside AIs.

00:58:43.700 --> 00:58:45.770
The biggest breakthroughs
in the machine

00:58:45.770 --> 00:58:49.370
learning field with some
discussion of robotics

00:58:49.370 --> 00:58:50.400
and so on.

00:58:50.400 --> 00:58:50.900
Yeah.

00:58:50.900 --> 00:58:51.770
It was in person.

00:58:51.770 --> 00:58:53.550
It was wonderful.

00:58:53.550 --> 00:58:54.500
I'm a sucker for that.

00:58:54.500 --> 00:58:58.280
I really avoided teaching
or any kind of interaction

00:58:58.280 --> 00:59:03.170
during COVID because people
put a lot of emphasis

00:59:03.170 --> 00:59:05.810
on but also got comfortable
with remote teaching,

00:59:05.810 --> 00:59:08.510
and I think nobody enjoyed it.

00:59:08.510 --> 00:59:13.540
Except there's a notion
that it's much easier to

00:59:13.540 --> 00:59:18.520
do because you don't
have to travel.

00:59:18.520 --> 00:59:20.870
You can do it in your
pajamas kind of thing.

00:59:20.870 --> 00:59:23.080
But when you actually
get to do it,

00:59:23.080 --> 00:59:25.720
you don't get the
same kind of joy

00:59:25.720 --> 00:59:27.340
that you do when
you're teaching.

00:59:27.340 --> 00:59:30.528
As a student you don't get the
same kind of joy of learning.

00:59:30.528 --> 00:59:32.570
It's not as effective and
all that kind of stuff.

00:59:32.570 --> 00:59:35.740
So to be in person together
with people, to see their eyes,

00:59:35.740 --> 00:59:38.710
to get their excitement, to
get the questions and all

00:59:38.710 --> 00:59:40.420
the interactions,
that was awesome.

00:59:40.420 --> 00:59:47.650
And I'm still a sucker and a
believer in the ideal of MIT,

00:59:47.650 --> 00:59:48.705
of the University.

00:59:48.705 --> 00:59:50.080
I think it's an
incredible place.

00:59:50.080 --> 00:59:52.300
There's something
in the air still.

00:59:52.300 --> 00:59:54.220
But it really hit--

00:59:54.220 --> 00:59:57.520
the pandemic hit
universities hard because--

00:59:57.520 --> 00:59:58.360
and I can say this.

00:59:58.360 --> 00:59:59.443
This is not you saying it.

00:59:59.443 --> 01:00:01.030
This is me saying it.

01:00:01.030 --> 01:00:05.020
That administrations--
as in all cases

01:00:05.020 --> 01:00:06.940
when people criticize
institutions,

01:00:06.940 --> 01:00:10.240
the pandemic has given more
power to the administration

01:00:10.240 --> 01:00:12.790
and taken away power from
the faculty and the students,

01:00:12.790 --> 01:00:14.930
and that's from
everybody involved,

01:00:14.930 --> 01:00:16.270
including the administration.

01:00:16.270 --> 01:00:18.010
That's a concern
because a university

01:00:18.010 --> 01:00:20.290
is about the teachers
and the students.

01:00:20.290 --> 01:00:21.640
That should be primary.

01:00:21.640 --> 01:00:23.740
And whenever you have
a pandemic there's

01:00:23.740 --> 01:00:26.110
an opportunity to increase
the amount of rules.

01:00:26.110 --> 01:00:28.340
One of the things that
really bothered me,

01:00:28.340 --> 01:00:32.740
and I'll scream from
the top of the MIT dome,

01:00:32.740 --> 01:00:37.690
about this is they've instituted
a new temp ticket system.

01:00:37.690 --> 01:00:41.240
Which is if you're a visitor
to the campus at MIT,

01:00:41.240 --> 01:00:42.268
you have to register.

01:00:42.268 --> 01:00:44.560
You have to, first of all,
show that you're vaccinated,

01:00:44.560 --> 01:00:47.710
but more importantly, there's
a process to visiting.

01:00:47.710 --> 01:00:50.080
You need to get
permission to visit.

01:00:50.080 --> 01:00:55.390
One of the reasons I loved MIT,
unlike some other institutions,

01:00:55.390 --> 01:00:59.590
MIT just leaves the
door open to anyone.

01:00:59.590 --> 01:01:02.710
In classrooms you can roll
in the ridiculous characters.

01:01:02.710 --> 01:01:05.950
The students that
are usually doing

01:01:05.950 --> 01:01:09.100
business stuff or economics
can roll into a physics class

01:01:09.100 --> 01:01:11.710
and just-- you're
kind of not allowed

01:01:11.710 --> 01:01:15.567
but it's a gray area
so you let that happen,

01:01:15.567 --> 01:01:17.650
and that creates a flourishing
of a community that

01:01:17.650 --> 01:01:18.400
was beautiful.

01:01:18.400 --> 01:01:23.260
And I think adding extra rules
puts a squeeze on and limits

01:01:23.260 --> 01:01:26.050
some of the
flourishing, and I hope

01:01:26.050 --> 01:01:27.820
some of that
dissipates over time

01:01:27.820 --> 01:01:33.543
as we kind of let go of
the risk aversion that

01:01:33.543 --> 01:01:34.710
was created by the pandemic.

01:01:34.710 --> 01:01:38.350
As we kind of enter
the normal return back

01:01:38.350 --> 01:01:39.950
some of that
flourishing can happen.

01:01:39.950 --> 01:01:43.900
But when you're actually
in there with the students,

01:01:43.900 --> 01:01:44.960
it was magic.

01:01:44.960 --> 01:01:45.500
I love it.

01:01:45.500 --> 01:01:46.240
I love it.

01:01:46.240 --> 01:01:47.620
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, some
of your earliest videos

01:01:47.620 --> 01:01:50.620
on your YouTube channel were
of you in the classroom, right?

01:01:50.620 --> 01:01:51.850
That's how this all started.

01:01:51.850 --> 01:01:52.270
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.

01:01:52.270 --> 01:01:52.770
Yeah.

01:01:52.770 --> 01:01:56.030
That's how YouTube--
putting stuff on YouTube

01:01:56.030 --> 01:01:57.405
is terrifying, right?

01:01:57.405 --> 01:01:58.780
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, especially

01:01:58.780 --> 01:01:59.947
at the time when you did it.

01:01:59.947 --> 01:02:03.190
Again, you're a
pioneer in that sense.

01:02:03.190 --> 01:02:04.960
You did that, Jordan
Peterson did that.

01:02:04.960 --> 01:02:08.740
Putting up lectures is--

01:02:08.740 --> 01:02:09.970
yeah.

01:02:09.970 --> 01:02:10.840
I teach still.

01:02:10.840 --> 01:02:12.520
Every winter I teach--

01:02:12.520 --> 01:02:14.830
direct a course, and I'll
be doing even more teaching

01:02:14.830 --> 01:02:15.500
going forward.

01:02:15.500 --> 01:02:20.680
But the idea of those
videos being on the web is--

01:02:20.680 --> 01:02:22.480
yeah, that spikes my
cortisol a little bit.

01:02:22.480 --> 01:02:23.230
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.

01:02:23.230 --> 01:02:24.730
It's terrifying
because you get--

01:02:24.730 --> 01:02:26.590
and everybody has a
different experience.

01:02:26.590 --> 01:02:31.000
For me being a junior
research scientist

01:02:31.000 --> 01:02:34.790
the kind of natural
concern is like, who am I?

01:02:34.790 --> 01:02:37.750
And when I was giving
this lecture it's like,

01:02:37.750 --> 01:02:38.917
I don't deserve any of this.

01:02:38.917 --> 01:02:41.125
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's your
humility coming through,

01:02:41.125 --> 01:02:42.670
and I actually
think that humility

01:02:42.670 --> 01:02:44.440
on the part of an
instructor is good

01:02:44.440 --> 01:02:50.440
because those that think that
they are entitled, and who else

01:02:50.440 --> 01:02:51.580
could give this lecture?

01:02:51.580 --> 01:02:53.742
Then I worry more.

01:02:53.742 --> 01:02:55.450
I once heard-- I don't
know if it's still

01:02:55.450 --> 01:02:58.450
true-- that at Caltech,
the great California

01:02:58.450 --> 01:03:01.330
Institute of Technology
not far from here,

01:03:01.330 --> 01:03:03.310
that many of the
faculty are actually

01:03:03.310 --> 01:03:04.833
afraid of the students.

01:03:04.833 --> 01:03:06.250
Not physically
afraid, but they're

01:03:06.250 --> 01:03:09.400
intellectually afraid because
the students are so smart.

01:03:09.400 --> 01:03:12.620
And teaching there can
be downright frightening,

01:03:12.620 --> 01:03:13.120
I've heard.

01:03:13.120 --> 01:03:13.870
But that's great.

01:03:13.870 --> 01:03:17.617
Keeps everybody on their toes.

01:03:17.617 --> 01:03:19.450
And you know, I've been
corrected in lecture

01:03:19.450 --> 01:03:21.520
before at Stanford
and elsewhere.

01:03:21.520 --> 01:03:24.110
When my lab was at UC San
Diego where someone will say,

01:03:24.110 --> 01:03:28.450
hey, wait, last lecture you said
this and now you said that--

01:03:28.450 --> 01:03:29.320
or on the podcast.

01:03:29.320 --> 01:03:29.820
You know?

01:03:29.820 --> 01:03:32.590
And I think it's that
moment where you sometimes

01:03:32.590 --> 01:03:35.413
feel that urge to defend and
you go, oh, you're right,

01:03:35.413 --> 01:03:37.330
and I think it depends
on how one was trained.

01:03:37.330 --> 01:03:40.360
My graduate advisor was
wonderful at saying,

01:03:40.360 --> 01:03:41.590
I don't know, all the time.

01:03:41.590 --> 01:03:45.700
And she went to Harvard,
Radcliffe, UCSF, and Caltech.

01:03:45.700 --> 01:03:47.290
Brilliant woman.

01:03:47.290 --> 01:03:49.070
And had no problem
saying, I don't know.

01:03:49.070 --> 01:03:50.120
LEX FRIDMAN: I don't
have that problem either.

01:03:50.120 --> 01:03:52.880
So I usually have two guys
that somebody speaks up,

01:03:52.880 --> 01:03:57.080
grab them, drag them out of
the room, never see them again.

01:03:57.080 --> 01:03:58.960
So everybody is
really supportive.

01:03:58.960 --> 01:04:01.320
You don't understand that the
amount of love and support

01:04:01.320 --> 01:04:01.820
I get is--

01:04:01.820 --> 01:04:03.610
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Especially when
the last few students are there

01:04:03.610 --> 01:04:06.065
and everybody seems to be
nodding as you're going.

01:04:06.065 --> 01:04:09.250
No, I think that I'd love to
sit-in on one of your lectures.

01:04:09.250 --> 01:04:12.310
I know very little about AI,
machine learning, or robotics.

01:04:12.310 --> 01:04:12.810
But--

01:04:12.810 --> 01:04:14.518
LEX FRIDMAN: Have you
ever talked at MIT?

01:04:14.518 --> 01:04:16.545
Have you ever given lectures?

01:04:16.545 --> 01:04:17.970
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, yeah.

01:04:17.970 --> 01:04:21.510
When I went on the job market
as a faculty member my final two

01:04:21.510 --> 01:04:23.790
choices were between
MIT Picower--

01:04:23.790 --> 01:04:25.260
I had an on-paper offer.

01:04:25.260 --> 01:04:26.190
Wonderful place.

01:04:26.190 --> 01:04:27.720
Wonderful place to
do neuroscience.

01:04:27.720 --> 01:04:32.003
And UC San Diego, which is a
wonderful neuroscience program.

01:04:32.003 --> 01:04:33.420
In the end it made
sense for me be

01:04:33.420 --> 01:04:35.045
on the West Coast
for personal reasons,

01:04:35.045 --> 01:04:38.220
but there's some amazing
neuroscience going on there.

01:04:38.220 --> 01:04:38.760
Goodness.

01:04:38.760 --> 01:04:41.620
And that's always been true
and is going to continue.

01:04:41.620 --> 01:04:44.400
It's been a long time since
I've been invited back there.

01:04:44.400 --> 01:04:47.160
Oddly enough when I started
doing more podcasting--

01:04:47.160 --> 01:04:51.660
and I still run a lab but I
shrunk my lab considerably

01:04:51.660 --> 01:04:53.460
as I've done more podcasting--

01:04:53.460 --> 01:04:55.710
I've received fewer
academic lecture

01:04:55.710 --> 01:04:56.940
invites, which makes sense.

01:04:56.940 --> 01:04:58.537
But now they're
sort of coming back.

01:04:58.537 --> 01:05:00.120
And so when people
invite now I always

01:05:00.120 --> 01:05:04.110
say, do you want me to talk
about the ventral thalamus

01:05:04.110 --> 01:05:07.020
and its role in
anxiety and aggression

01:05:07.020 --> 01:05:09.090
or do you want me to
talk about the podcast?

01:05:09.090 --> 01:05:10.290
And my big fear is
I'm going to go back

01:05:10.290 --> 01:05:11.910
to give a lecture about
the retina or something

01:05:11.910 --> 01:05:13.868
and I'll start off with
an Athletic Greens read

01:05:13.868 --> 01:05:15.810
or something like
that just reflexively.

01:05:15.810 --> 01:05:16.380
Just kidding.

01:05:16.380 --> 01:05:17.255
That wouldn't happen.

01:05:17.255 --> 01:05:20.430
But listen, I think
it's great to continue

01:05:20.430 --> 01:05:22.403
to keep a foot in both places.

01:05:22.403 --> 01:05:23.820
I was so happy to
hear that you're

01:05:23.820 --> 01:05:26.253
teaching at MIT because
podcasting is one thing,

01:05:26.253 --> 01:05:28.170
teaching is another, and
there's overlap there

01:05:28.170 --> 01:05:29.040
in the Venn diagram.

01:05:29.040 --> 01:05:31.445
But listen, the students
that get to sit-in

01:05:31.445 --> 01:05:32.820
on one of your
lectures-- and you

01:05:32.820 --> 01:05:34.487
may see me sitting
there in the audience

01:05:34.487 --> 01:05:36.735
soon when I creep
into your class.

01:05:36.735 --> 01:05:37.860
LEX FRIDMAN: In sunglasses.

01:05:37.860 --> 01:05:38.220
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's right.

01:05:38.220 --> 01:05:39.870
Wearing a red shirt.

01:05:39.870 --> 01:05:42.810
You won't recognize me.

01:05:42.810 --> 01:05:45.420
Are certainly
receiving a great gift.

01:05:45.420 --> 01:05:47.940
I've watched your lectures on
YouTube, even the early ones,

01:05:47.940 --> 01:05:51.447
and listen, I know you to
be a phenomenal teacher.

01:05:51.447 --> 01:05:53.280
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah,
there's something about--

01:05:53.280 --> 01:05:55.490
so I'm also doing--

01:05:55.490 --> 01:05:57.960
I stayed up pretty
late last night

01:05:57.960 --> 01:06:00.300
working for a
deadline on a paper.

01:06:00.300 --> 01:06:03.600
One of the things
that I hope to do

01:06:03.600 --> 01:06:08.080
for hopefully the rest of my
life is to continue publishing,

01:06:08.080 --> 01:06:12.210
and I think it's really
important to do that

01:06:12.210 --> 01:06:16.440
even if you continue the podcast
because you want to be just

01:06:16.440 --> 01:06:20.070
on your own intellectual
and scientific journey

01:06:20.070 --> 01:06:23.040
as you do podcasting.

01:06:23.040 --> 01:06:25.530
At least for me, and especially
on the engineering side

01:06:25.530 --> 01:06:28.410
because I want to
build stuff, and I

01:06:28.410 --> 01:06:32.180
think that keeps
your ego in check,

01:06:32.180 --> 01:06:34.820
keeps you humble
because I think if you

01:06:34.820 --> 01:06:38.540
talk too much on a microphone
you start getting--

01:06:38.540 --> 01:06:42.800
you might lose track
of the grounding that

01:06:42.800 --> 01:06:44.570
comes from engineering
and from science

01:06:44.570 --> 01:06:47.030
and the scientific process and
the criticisms that you get,

01:06:47.030 --> 01:06:47.870
all that kind of stuff.

01:06:47.870 --> 01:06:49.550
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And how
slow and iterative it is.

01:06:49.550 --> 01:06:52.250
We have two papers right now
that are in the revision stage,

01:06:52.250 --> 01:06:54.260
and it's been a very long road.

01:06:54.260 --> 01:06:56.930
And I was asked this recently
because I met with my chairman.

01:06:56.930 --> 01:06:58.400
He said, do you want to
continue to run a lab or are

01:06:58.400 --> 01:06:59.870
you just going to go
full time on the podcast?

01:06:59.870 --> 01:07:01.953
And Stanford has been very
supportive, I must say,

01:07:01.953 --> 01:07:04.640
as I know MIT has been of you.

01:07:04.640 --> 01:07:06.320
And I said, oh,
I absolutely want

01:07:06.320 --> 01:07:08.975
to continue to be involved
in research and do research.

01:07:08.975 --> 01:07:10.850
And when you start
talking about these papers

01:07:10.850 --> 01:07:12.920
and we're looking over my--
this was my yearly review

01:07:12.920 --> 01:07:14.090
and looking back
I'm like, goodness,

01:07:14.090 --> 01:07:16.380
these papers have been in
play for a very long time.

01:07:16.380 --> 01:07:19.340
So it's a long road but
you learn more and more,

01:07:19.340 --> 01:07:21.950
and the more time you
spend myopically looking

01:07:21.950 --> 01:07:24.920
at a bunch of data the more you
learn and the more you think.

01:07:24.920 --> 01:07:26.120
I totally agree.

01:07:26.120 --> 01:07:28.130
Talking to these
devices for podcasts

01:07:28.130 --> 01:07:30.120
is wonderful because it's fun.

01:07:30.120 --> 01:07:33.620
It relieves a certain itch that
we both have and hopefully it

01:07:33.620 --> 01:07:35.960
lands some important information
out there for people,

01:07:35.960 --> 01:07:40.865
but doing research is the--

01:07:40.865 --> 01:07:42.180
I guess if you know, you know.

01:07:42.180 --> 01:07:45.620
There's the unpeeling
of the onion,

01:07:45.620 --> 01:07:47.630
knowing that there could
be something there.

01:07:47.630 --> 01:07:49.460
There's just nothing like it.

01:07:49.460 --> 01:07:50.730
LEX FRIDMAN: I mean, you do--

01:07:50.730 --> 01:07:52.530
especially with the pandemic.

01:07:52.530 --> 01:07:56.960
And for me, both
Twitter and the podcast

01:07:56.960 --> 01:07:59.720
have made me much more
impatient about the slowness

01:07:59.720 --> 01:08:01.707
of the review process because--

01:08:01.707 --> 01:08:03.290
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Twitter will do that.

01:08:03.290 --> 01:08:04.373
LEX FRIDMAN: Twitter will.

01:08:04.373 --> 01:08:06.080
But even with podcast
you have a cool--

01:08:06.080 --> 01:08:09.050
you'll find something cool
and then you have ideas

01:08:09.050 --> 01:08:12.050
and you'll just say them and
they'll be out pretty quickly.

01:08:12.050 --> 01:08:13.730
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Then we do a
post right now about something

01:08:13.730 --> 01:08:15.650
that we both found interesting
and it's out in the world.

01:08:15.650 --> 01:08:15.950
Yep.

01:08:15.950 --> 01:08:16.850
LEX FRIDMAN: And
you can write up

01:08:16.850 --> 01:08:19.130
something, like there
is a culture in computer

01:08:19.130 --> 01:08:22.162
science of posting stuff
on arXiv and preprints

01:08:22.162 --> 01:08:24.620
that don't get annual review,
and sometimes they don't even

01:08:24.620 --> 01:08:27.590
go through the review process
ever because people just

01:08:27.590 --> 01:08:29.330
start using them if it's code.

01:08:29.330 --> 01:08:31.279
And it's like, what's
the point of this?

01:08:31.279 --> 01:08:32.540
It works.

01:08:32.540 --> 01:08:36.620
It's self evident that it works
because people are using it,

01:08:36.620 --> 01:08:40.250
and that I think applies
more to engineering fields

01:08:40.250 --> 01:08:42.260
because it's an actual
tool that works.

01:08:42.260 --> 01:08:45.050
It doesn't matter if-- you don't
have to scientifically prove

01:08:45.050 --> 01:08:45.689
that it works.

01:08:45.689 --> 01:08:47.689
It works because it's
using for a lot of people.

01:08:47.689 --> 01:08:49.981
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, sorry
to interrupt, but I just--

01:08:49.981 --> 01:08:51.835
for point of reference,
the famous paper

01:08:51.835 --> 01:08:53.210
describing the
double helix which

01:08:53.210 --> 01:08:55.250
earned Watson and
Crick the Nobel Prize

01:08:55.250 --> 01:08:58.550
and should have earned Rosalind
Franklin Nobel Prize too,

01:08:58.550 --> 01:09:02.840
of course, but they got it for
the structure of DNA of course.

01:09:02.840 --> 01:09:05.090
That paper was never
reviewed at Nature.

01:09:05.090 --> 01:09:08.750
They published it because its
importance was self evident,

01:09:08.750 --> 01:09:09.547
or whatever.

01:09:09.547 --> 01:09:10.130
They decided--

01:09:10.130 --> 01:09:11.510
LEX FRIDMAN: So the editors.

01:09:11.510 --> 01:09:13.130
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It was that
purely editorial decision,

01:09:13.130 --> 01:09:13.662
I believe.

01:09:13.662 --> 01:09:15.620
I mean, that's what I
was told by someone who's

01:09:15.620 --> 01:09:17.540
currently an editor at Nature.

01:09:17.540 --> 01:09:19.310
If that turns out
to not be correct

01:09:19.310 --> 01:09:21.380
someone will tell us in
the comments for sure.

01:09:21.380 --> 01:09:22.100
LEX FRIDMAN: Well, I think--

01:09:22.100 --> 01:09:23.149
ANDREW HUBERMAN: That's
pretty interesting, right?

01:09:23.149 --> 01:09:23.689
LEX FRIDMAN: That's
really interested.

01:09:23.689 --> 01:09:25.981
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Perhaps the
most significant discovery

01:09:25.981 --> 01:09:28.069
in biology and
bioengineering which

01:09:28.069 --> 01:09:29.840
was leading to
bioengineering as well,

01:09:29.840 --> 01:09:34.040
of course, of the last
century was not peer reviewed.

01:09:34.040 --> 01:09:35.060
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah, but--

01:09:35.060 --> 01:09:37.310
so Eric Weinstein,
but many others

01:09:37.310 --> 01:09:41.010
have talked about
this, which is, I mean,

01:09:41.010 --> 01:09:47.270
I don't think people understand
how poor the peer review

01:09:47.270 --> 01:09:48.410
process is.

01:09:48.410 --> 01:09:50.510
Just the amount of--
because you think peer

01:09:50.510 --> 01:09:54.590
review it means all the
best peers get together

01:09:54.590 --> 01:09:57.740
and they review your
stuff, but it's unpaid work

01:09:57.740 --> 01:09:59.490
and it's usually a
small number of people.

01:09:59.490 --> 01:10:01.590
And they have a very
select perspective

01:10:01.590 --> 01:10:03.590
so they might not be the
best person, especially

01:10:03.590 --> 01:10:04.940
if it's super novel work.

01:10:04.940 --> 01:10:06.200
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And it's
who has time to do it.

01:10:06.200 --> 01:10:08.150
I'm on a bunch of
editorial boards still.

01:10:08.150 --> 01:10:11.210
Why, I don't know, but I
enjoy the peer review process

01:10:11.210 --> 01:10:12.200
and sending papers out.

01:10:12.200 --> 01:10:14.420
Oftentimes the best
scientists are very busy

01:10:14.420 --> 01:10:15.770
and don't have time to review.

01:10:15.770 --> 01:10:19.550
And oftentimes the
more premiere journals

01:10:19.550 --> 01:10:22.700
will select from a kind
of a unique kit of very

01:10:22.700 --> 01:10:25.980
good scientists who are
very close to the work,

01:10:25.980 --> 01:10:27.980
sometimes the people are
very far from the work.

01:10:27.980 --> 01:10:28.670
It really depends.

01:10:28.670 --> 01:10:30.503
LEX FRIDMAN: And both
have negatives, right?

01:10:30.503 --> 01:10:32.210
If you're very close
to the work there's

01:10:32.210 --> 01:10:34.040
jealousy, and all those
basic human things.

01:10:34.040 --> 01:10:35.540
Very far from the
work you might not

01:10:35.540 --> 01:10:37.672
appreciate the
nuanced contribution,

01:10:37.672 --> 01:10:38.630
all that kind of stuff.

01:10:38.630 --> 01:10:39.620
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
there's psychology.

01:10:39.620 --> 01:10:41.995
Sorry to interrupt again, but
a good friend of mine who's

01:10:41.995 --> 01:10:44.030
extremely successful
neuroscientist,

01:10:44.030 --> 01:10:45.860
Howard Hughes
investigator, et cetera,

01:10:45.860 --> 01:10:47.495
always told me that they--

01:10:47.495 --> 01:10:49.920
I won't even say whether
or not who they are.

01:10:49.920 --> 01:10:52.160
They select their
reviewers on the basis

01:10:52.160 --> 01:10:55.167
of who has been publishing
very well recently because they

01:10:55.167 --> 01:10:56.750
assume that that
person is going to be

01:10:56.750 --> 01:10:58.610
more benevolent because
they have been doing

01:10:58.610 --> 01:11:00.205
well so that the love expands.

01:11:00.205 --> 01:11:02.330
LEX FRIDMAN: That's a good
point to that, actually.

01:11:02.330 --> 01:11:06.050
But the idea is that
editors might actually

01:11:06.050 --> 01:11:09.740
be the best reviewers, so that
was the traditional-- that's

01:11:09.740 --> 01:11:12.260
the thing I wanted to mention
that Eric Weinstein talks

01:11:12.260 --> 01:11:15.770
about, that back several
decades ago editors had

01:11:15.770 --> 01:11:16.670
much more power.

01:11:16.670 --> 01:11:19.880
And there is something to be
made for that because editors

01:11:19.880 --> 01:11:24.380
are the ones who are responsible
for crafting the journal.

01:11:24.380 --> 01:11:27.740
They really are
invested in this,

01:11:27.740 --> 01:11:30.040
and they're also
often experts, right?

01:11:30.040 --> 01:11:31.540
It makes sense for
an editor to have

01:11:31.540 --> 01:11:33.580
a bit of power in this case.

01:11:33.580 --> 01:11:38.030
Usually if an idea is truly
novel you could see it,

01:11:38.030 --> 01:11:41.110
And so it makes
sense for an editor

01:11:41.110 --> 01:11:42.700
to have more power
in that regard.

01:11:42.700 --> 01:11:45.220
Of course for me,
I think peer review

01:11:45.220 --> 01:11:46.850
should be done the
way tweets are done,

01:11:46.850 --> 01:11:49.187
which is crowdsourced
or Amazon reviews.

01:11:49.187 --> 01:11:50.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Let
the crowd decide.

01:11:50.770 --> 01:11:52.187
LEX FRIDMAN: Let
the crowd decide,

01:11:52.187 --> 01:11:59.770
and let the crowd add depth
and breadth and context

01:11:59.770 --> 01:12:01.096
for the contribution.

01:12:01.096 --> 01:12:06.290
So if the paper overstates
the degree of contribution,

01:12:06.290 --> 01:12:09.370
the crowd will
check you on that.

01:12:09.370 --> 01:12:12.520
If there's not enough support
or the conclusions are not

01:12:12.520 --> 01:12:16.390
supported by the evidence, the
crowd will check you on that.

01:12:16.390 --> 01:12:19.312
There could be, of course,
political bickering

01:12:19.312 --> 01:12:20.770
that enters the
picture, especially

01:12:20.770 --> 01:12:22.870
on very controversial
topics, but I

01:12:22.870 --> 01:12:24.878
think I trust the
intelligence of human beings

01:12:24.878 --> 01:12:25.670
to figure that out.

01:12:25.670 --> 01:12:28.900
And I think most of us
are trying to figure

01:12:28.900 --> 01:12:29.980
this whole process out.

01:12:29.980 --> 01:12:32.740
I just wish it was
happening much faster

01:12:32.740 --> 01:12:35.440
because on the important
topics, the review

01:12:35.440 --> 01:12:37.330
cycle could be faster.

01:12:37.330 --> 01:12:38.740
And we learned
that through COVID

01:12:38.740 --> 01:12:41.980
that Twitter was
actually pretty effective

01:12:41.980 --> 01:12:43.990
at doing science communication.

01:12:43.990 --> 01:12:45.920
It was really interesting.

01:12:45.920 --> 01:12:48.280
Some of the best
scientists took to Twitter

01:12:48.280 --> 01:12:51.880
to communicate their own
work and other people's work,

01:12:51.880 --> 01:12:54.130
and always putting
into the caveats

01:12:54.130 --> 01:12:58.060
that it's not peer reviewed and
so on, but it's all out there

01:12:58.060 --> 01:13:00.190
and the data just moves so fast.

01:13:00.190 --> 01:13:02.380
And if you want
stuff to move fast,

01:13:02.380 --> 01:13:04.930
Twitter is the best medium
of communication for that.

01:13:04.930 --> 01:13:05.882
It's cool to see.

01:13:05.882 --> 01:13:07.840
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'd like
to take a brief break

01:13:07.840 --> 01:13:10.750
and thank our sponsor,
InsideTracker.

01:13:10.750 --> 01:13:13.390
InsideTracker is a
personalized nutrition platform

01:13:13.390 --> 01:13:15.850
that analyzes data
from your blood and DNA

01:13:15.850 --> 01:13:17.560
to help you better
