01:13:28.955 --> 01:13:30.580
The problem with a
lot of blood and DNA

01:13:30.580 --> 01:13:32.770
tests out there, however,
is that you get data

01:13:32.770 --> 01:13:35.310
back about metabolic
factors, lipids, and hormones

01:13:35.310 --> 01:13:37.810
and so forth, but you don't
know what to do with those data.

01:13:37.810 --> 01:13:39.760
InsideTracker
solves that problem

01:13:39.760 --> 01:13:41.950
and makes it very easy
for you to understand

01:13:41.950 --> 01:13:45.580
what sorts of nutritional,
behavioral, maybe even

01:13:45.580 --> 01:13:47.830
supplementation
based interventions

01:13:47.830 --> 01:13:49.510
you might want to
take on in order

01:13:49.510 --> 01:13:52.210
to adjust the numbers of those
metabolic factors, hormones,

01:13:52.210 --> 01:13:53.890
lipids, and other
things that impact

01:13:53.890 --> 01:13:55.960
your immediate and long
term health to bring

01:13:55.960 --> 01:13:58.450
those numbers into the
ranges that are appropriate

01:13:58.450 --> 01:14:00.190
and, indeed, optimal for you.

01:14:00.190 --> 01:14:02.230
If you'd like to try
InsideTracker you can visit

01:14:02.230 --> 01:14:06.880
insidetracker.com/huberman to
get $200 off an ultimate plan

01:14:06.880 --> 01:14:11.410
or 34% off the entire site as
a special Black Friday deal now

01:14:11.410 --> 01:14:12.640
through the end of November.

01:14:12.640 --> 01:14:15.610
Again, that's
insidetracker.com/huberman,

01:14:15.610 --> 01:14:17.920
and use the code
Huberman at checkout.

01:14:17.920 --> 01:14:20.770
I'm now on Twitter
more regularly,

01:14:20.770 --> 01:14:22.570
and initially it
was just Instagram.

01:14:22.570 --> 01:14:27.190
And I remember you and I used to
have these over dinner or drink

01:14:27.190 --> 01:14:29.830
conversations where I'd say,
I don't understand Twitter.

01:14:29.830 --> 01:14:31.810
And you'd say, I don't
understand Instagram.

01:14:31.810 --> 01:14:33.560
And of course, we
understand how it worked

01:14:33.560 --> 01:14:35.720
and how to work each
respective platform,

01:14:35.720 --> 01:14:38.290
but I think we were both
trying to figure out

01:14:38.290 --> 01:14:41.350
what is driving the psychology
of these different venues

01:14:41.350 --> 01:14:44.800
because they are quite distinct
psychologies for whatever

01:14:44.800 --> 01:14:45.400
reason.

01:14:45.400 --> 01:14:47.900
I think I'm finally starting
to understand Twitter and enjoy

01:14:47.900 --> 01:14:49.150
it a little bit.

01:14:49.150 --> 01:14:52.960
Initially I wasn't
prepared for the level

01:14:52.960 --> 01:14:56.470
of reflexive scrutiny.

01:14:56.470 --> 01:14:58.000
It sounds a little
bit oxymoronic,

01:14:58.000 --> 01:14:59.800
but that people pick
up on one small thing

01:14:59.800 --> 01:15:01.930
and then drive it
down that trajectory.

01:15:01.930 --> 01:15:04.420
It didn't seem to be happening
quite as much on Instagram,

01:15:04.420 --> 01:15:06.130
but I love your tweets.

01:15:06.130 --> 01:15:10.510
I do have a question
about your Twitter account

01:15:10.510 --> 01:15:13.330
and how you-- do you have sort
of internal filters of what

01:15:13.330 --> 01:15:15.310
you'll put up and won't put up?

01:15:15.310 --> 01:15:17.470
Because sometimes you'll
put up things that

01:15:17.470 --> 01:15:18.790
are about life and reflections.

01:15:18.790 --> 01:15:21.730
Other times you'll put up
things like what you're

01:15:21.730 --> 01:15:24.670
excited about in
AI, or of course,

01:15:24.670 --> 01:15:26.890
point to various podcasts
including your own,

01:15:26.890 --> 01:15:29.110
but others as well.

01:15:29.110 --> 01:15:30.990
How do you approach
social media?

01:15:30.990 --> 01:15:32.923
Not in how do you
regulate your behavior

01:15:32.923 --> 01:15:34.840
on there in terms of how
much time, et cetera.

01:15:34.840 --> 01:15:36.465
I know you've talked
about that before.

01:15:36.465 --> 01:15:40.090
But you know, what's your
mindset around social media

01:15:40.090 --> 01:15:46.870
when you go on there to either
post or forage or respond

01:15:46.870 --> 01:15:48.020
to information?

01:15:48.020 --> 01:15:52.090
LEX FRIDMAN: I think
I try to add some--

01:15:52.090 --> 01:15:56.830
not the sound cliche, but some
love out there into the world

01:15:56.830 --> 01:16:01.300
into, as OJ Simpson
calls it, Twitter world.

01:16:01.300 --> 01:16:06.430
I think there is this viral
negativity that can take hold,

01:16:06.430 --> 01:16:11.620
and I try to find the right
language to add good vibes out

01:16:11.620 --> 01:16:12.130
there.

01:16:12.130 --> 01:16:14.170
And it's actually
really, really tricky

01:16:14.170 --> 01:16:20.260
because there's something about
positivity that sounds fake.

01:16:20.260 --> 01:16:22.450
I can't quite put
my finger on it,

01:16:22.450 --> 01:16:27.550
but whenever I talk about
love and positive and almost

01:16:27.550 --> 01:16:31.990
childlike in my curiosity
and positivity, people

01:16:31.990 --> 01:16:36.790
start to think, surely he
has skeletons in the closet.

01:16:36.790 --> 01:16:38.800
There's dead bodies
in his basement.

01:16:38.800 --> 01:16:40.090
This must be a fake--

01:16:40.090 --> 01:16:41.590
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
No, it's the attic.

01:16:41.590 --> 01:16:42.250
LEX FRIDMAN: It's the attic?

01:16:42.250 --> 01:16:42.670
ANDREW HUBERMAN: The attic.

01:16:42.670 --> 01:16:44.378
LEX FRIDMAN: I keep
mine in the basement.

01:16:44.378 --> 01:16:45.250
That's the details.

01:16:45.250 --> 01:16:46.270
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I was
referring to your attic.

01:16:46.270 --> 01:16:48.872
I don't have an attic or a
basement, nor dead bodies.

01:16:48.872 --> 01:16:50.080
I just want to be very clear.

01:16:50.080 --> 01:16:50.830
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.

01:16:50.830 --> 01:16:55.130
I do have an attic and actually
I haven't been up there.

01:16:55.130 --> 01:16:56.450
Maybe there is bodies up there.

01:16:56.450 --> 01:16:58.132
But yes, I prefer the basement.

01:16:58.132 --> 01:16:59.090
It's colder down there.

01:16:59.090 --> 01:17:00.620
I like it.

01:17:00.620 --> 01:17:04.190
No, but there's an assumption
that this is not genuine

01:17:04.190 --> 01:17:08.040
or it's disingenuous
in some kind of way.

01:17:08.040 --> 01:17:10.580
And so I try to find the
right language for that kind

01:17:10.580 --> 01:17:12.890
of stuff, how to be positive.

01:17:12.890 --> 01:17:18.140
Some of it I was really inspired
by Elon's approach to Twitter.

01:17:18.140 --> 01:17:23.090
Not all of it, but
when he just is silly.

01:17:23.090 --> 01:17:26.810
I found that silliness--

01:17:26.810 --> 01:17:33.833
I think it's Hermann Hesse
said something to paraphrase--

01:17:33.833 --> 01:17:35.000
one of my favorite writers--

01:17:35.000 --> 01:17:36.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah, same.

01:17:36.350 --> 01:17:38.750
LEX FRIDMAN: I
think in Steppenwolf

01:17:38.750 --> 01:17:42.590
said, learn what is
to be taken seriously

01:17:42.590 --> 01:17:45.110
and laugh at the rest.

01:17:45.110 --> 01:17:49.280
I think I try to be
silly, laugh at myself,

01:17:49.280 --> 01:17:52.910
laugh at the absurdity
of life, and then in part

01:17:52.910 --> 01:17:57.290
when I'm serious, try to
just be positive, just

01:17:57.290 --> 01:17:59.810
to see a positive perspective.

01:17:59.810 --> 01:18:03.728
And also, as you said,
people pick out certain words

01:18:03.728 --> 01:18:05.270
and so on and they
attack each other,

01:18:05.270 --> 01:18:09.170
attack me over certain usage
of words in a particular tweet.

01:18:09.170 --> 01:18:14.240
I think the thing I try to do is
think positively towards them,

01:18:14.240 --> 01:18:15.830
like do not escalate.

01:18:15.830 --> 01:18:18.740
So whenever somebody's
criticizing me and so on,

01:18:18.740 --> 01:18:20.840
I just smile.

01:18:20.840 --> 01:18:23.090
If there's a lesson to
be learned, I learn it

01:18:23.090 --> 01:18:26.150
and then I just send
good vibes their way.

01:18:26.150 --> 01:18:29.870
Don't respond, and
just hopefully,

01:18:29.870 --> 01:18:35.300
through karma and through the
ripple effect of positivity,

01:18:35.300 --> 01:18:38.870
have an impact on them and
the rest of the Twitter.

01:18:38.870 --> 01:18:42.740
And what you find
is that builds--

01:18:42.740 --> 01:18:45.080
your actions create
the community.

01:18:45.080 --> 01:18:49.250
So how I behave gets me
surrounded by certain people.

01:18:49.250 --> 01:18:54.230
But lately, especially Ukraine
is one topic like this,

01:18:54.230 --> 01:18:57.530
I also thought
about talking to--

01:18:57.530 --> 01:18:59.900
somebody who reached out
to me is Andrew Tate,

01:18:59.900 --> 01:19:02.180
who's extremely controversial.

01:19:02.180 --> 01:19:05.510
From the perspective of a lot
of people is a misogynist.

01:19:05.510 --> 01:19:07.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
I've heard his name

01:19:07.010 --> 01:19:09.343
and I know that there's a lot
of controversy around him.

01:19:09.343 --> 01:19:11.330
Maybe you could familiarize me.

01:19:11.330 --> 01:19:13.370
I've been pretty nose
down in podcast prep

01:19:13.370 --> 01:19:16.370
and I tried to do this
vacation thing for about three,

01:19:16.370 --> 01:19:17.090
four weeks.

01:19:17.090 --> 01:19:17.810
LEX FRIDMAN: I've
heard about that.

01:19:17.810 --> 01:19:18.727
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:19:18.727 --> 01:19:20.540
And it sort of worked.

01:19:20.540 --> 01:19:22.910
I did get some time in
the Colorado wilderness

01:19:22.910 --> 01:19:25.470
by myself, which was great.

01:19:25.470 --> 01:19:29.120
I did get some downtime.

01:19:29.120 --> 01:19:33.770
But in any event, it mainly
consisted of reading and--

01:19:33.770 --> 01:19:35.420
LEX FRIDMAN: And nature?

01:19:35.420 --> 01:19:38.330
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Reading
and nature, sauna, ice bath,

01:19:38.330 --> 01:19:41.640
working out, good food,
a little extra sleep,

01:19:41.640 --> 01:19:43.640
these kinds of things I
really felt I needed it.

01:19:43.640 --> 01:19:46.850
But I am pretty
naive when it comes

01:19:46.850 --> 01:19:49.400
to the kind of
current controversies

01:19:49.400 --> 01:19:51.720
but I've heard his
name, and I think

01:19:51.720 --> 01:19:53.720
he's been deplatformed
on a couple of platforms.

01:19:53.720 --> 01:19:54.560
Do I have that right?

01:19:54.560 --> 01:19:56.518
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah, he's
been-- so I should also

01:19:56.518 --> 01:19:59.940
admit that while I might
know more than you,

01:19:59.940 --> 01:20:01.470
it's not by much.

01:20:01.470 --> 01:20:03.260
So it's like a
five-year-old talking

01:20:03.260 --> 01:20:04.542
to a four-year-old right now.

01:20:04.542 --> 01:20:06.500
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Is he
an athlete, a podcaster?

01:20:06.500 --> 01:20:11.340
LEX FRIDMAN: So basic summary,
he used to be a fighter,

01:20:11.340 --> 01:20:13.260
a kickboxer, I believe.

01:20:13.260 --> 01:20:14.570
Was pretty successful.

01:20:14.570 --> 01:20:18.920
And then during
that and after that

01:20:18.920 --> 01:20:21.080
I think he was on
a reality show,

01:20:21.080 --> 01:20:25.820
and he had all these programs
that are basically pickup

01:20:25.820 --> 01:20:27.110
artist advice.

01:20:27.110 --> 01:20:29.900
He has this community of
people where he gives advice

01:20:29.900 --> 01:20:33.750
on how to pick up women, how to
be successful in relationships,

01:20:33.750 --> 01:20:37.730
how to make a lot of
money, and it costs money

01:20:37.730 --> 01:20:39.270
to enter those programs.

01:20:39.270 --> 01:20:43.310
So a lot of the criticism
that he gets is kind of--

01:20:43.310 --> 01:20:48.050
it's like a pyramid scheme
where you convince people

01:20:48.050 --> 01:20:50.010
to join so that they
can make more money

01:20:50.010 --> 01:20:52.593
and then they convince others
to join, and that kind of stuff.

01:20:52.593 --> 01:20:54.890
But that's not why I'm
interested in talking to him.

01:20:54.890 --> 01:20:57.590
I'm interested because
one of the guests--

01:20:57.590 --> 01:21:01.310
maybe I should mention who, but
one of the female guests I had,

01:21:01.310 --> 01:21:07.550
really a big scientist, said
that her two kids that are 13

01:21:07.550 --> 01:21:11.570
and 12 really look up
to Andrew to entertain--

01:21:11.570 --> 01:21:13.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Is it male
children, female children?

01:21:13.820 --> 01:21:14.810
LEX FRIDMAN: Male.

01:21:14.810 --> 01:21:16.740
And I hear this
time and time again.

01:21:16.740 --> 01:21:22.430
So he is somebody that a lot of
teens, young teens, look up to.

01:21:22.430 --> 01:21:26.960
So I haven't done
serious research.

01:21:26.960 --> 01:21:31.070
I usually try to avoid doing
research until I agree to talk

01:21:31.070 --> 01:21:32.880
and then I go deep.

01:21:32.880 --> 01:21:36.740
But there is an
aspect to the way

01:21:36.740 --> 01:21:43.000
he talks about women that, while
I understand and I understand

01:21:43.000 --> 01:21:46.090
certain dynamics in
relationships work for people

01:21:46.090 --> 01:21:51.110
and he's one such
person, but I think

01:21:51.110 --> 01:21:55.490
him being really disrespectful
towards women is not what I--

01:21:58.030 --> 01:22:01.980
it's not how I see what
it means to be a good man.

01:22:01.980 --> 01:22:04.140
So the conversation I
want to have with him

01:22:04.140 --> 01:22:05.430
is about masculinity.

01:22:05.430 --> 01:22:08.470
What does masculinity
mean in the 21st century?

01:22:08.470 --> 01:22:11.430
And so when I think
about that kind of stuff,

01:22:11.430 --> 01:22:14.080
and because we're
talking about Twitter,

01:22:14.080 --> 01:22:16.420
it's like going into a war zone.

01:22:16.420 --> 01:22:19.698
I'm a happy go
lucky person, but--

01:22:19.698 --> 01:22:21.740
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You're
like, send me to Ukraine,

01:22:21.740 --> 01:22:24.100
but I don't want to have
this conversation on Twitter.

01:22:24.100 --> 01:22:26.690
LEX FRIDMAN: Because
it's a really, really,

01:22:26.690 --> 01:22:30.580
really tricky one because
also, as you know,

01:22:30.580 --> 01:22:35.260
when you do a podcast,
everybody wants you to win.

01:22:38.590 --> 01:22:40.360
It's everything
you do is positive.

01:22:40.360 --> 01:22:43.700
Maybe you'll say the wrong
thing as inaccurate thing

01:22:43.700 --> 01:22:45.550
and you can correct yourself.

01:22:45.550 --> 01:22:50.320
With Andrew Tate, with Donald
Trump, with folks like this,

01:22:50.320 --> 01:22:51.580
you have to--

01:22:51.580 --> 01:22:53.470
I mean, it's a
professional boxing.

01:22:53.470 --> 01:22:55.600
I think you have
to push the person.

01:22:55.600 --> 01:22:57.202
You have to be really eloquent.

01:22:57.202 --> 01:22:59.410
You have to be also empathetic
because you can't just

01:22:59.410 --> 01:23:01.000
do what journalists
do, which is talk down

01:23:01.000 --> 01:23:02.260
to the person the entire time.

01:23:02.260 --> 01:23:03.230
That's easy.

01:23:03.230 --> 01:23:05.200
The hard thing is to
empathize with the person,

01:23:05.200 --> 01:23:08.290
to understand them, to
steel man their case,

01:23:08.290 --> 01:23:10.010
but also to make your own case.

01:23:10.010 --> 01:23:13.090
So in that case about what
it means to be a man, to me

01:23:13.090 --> 01:23:16.510
a strong man is somebody
who is respectful to women.

01:23:16.510 --> 01:23:19.240
Not out of weakness, not out
of social justice warrior

01:23:19.240 --> 01:23:22.240
signaling, and all that kind
of stuff, but out of that's

01:23:22.240 --> 01:23:24.130
what a strong man does.

01:23:24.130 --> 01:23:26.560
They don't need to be
disrespectful to prove

01:23:26.560 --> 01:23:28.060
their position in life.

01:23:28.060 --> 01:23:32.650
He is often-- now, a lot of
people say it's a character.

01:23:32.650 --> 01:23:34.690
He's being misogynistic.

01:23:34.690 --> 01:23:37.510
He's being a misogynist as a
kind of-- for entertainment

01:23:37.510 --> 01:23:38.090
purposes.

01:23:38.090 --> 01:23:39.548
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
So like an avatar.

01:23:39.548 --> 01:23:40.360
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.

01:23:40.360 --> 01:23:44.800
But to me, that avatar has a
lot of influence on young folks

01:23:44.800 --> 01:23:48.633
so the character has impact.

01:23:48.633 --> 01:23:50.050
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Oh, I don't think

01:23:50.050 --> 01:23:53.350
you can separate the
avatar and the person

01:23:53.350 --> 01:23:55.270
in terms of the
impact, as you said.

01:23:55.270 --> 01:23:57.340
In fact, there are
a number of accounts

01:23:57.340 --> 01:23:59.920
on Twitter and Instagram and
elsewhere which people have

01:23:59.920 --> 01:24:02.710
only revealed their first
names or they give themself

01:24:02.710 --> 01:24:05.170
another name or they're
using a cartoon image.

01:24:05.170 --> 01:24:07.807
And part of that, I
believe, in at least

01:24:07.807 --> 01:24:10.390
from some of these individuals
who actually know who they are,

01:24:10.390 --> 01:24:14.050
I understand as an attempt to
maintain their privacy, which

01:24:14.050 --> 01:24:17.050
is important to many people.

01:24:17.050 --> 01:24:22.960
And in some cases so that
they can be more inflammatory

01:24:22.960 --> 01:24:25.150
and then just pop up
elsewhere as something

01:24:25.150 --> 01:24:27.583
else without anyone knowing
that it's the same person.

01:24:27.583 --> 01:24:29.500
LEX FRIDMAN: Some of--
this is the dark stuff.

01:24:29.500 --> 01:24:34.120
I've been reading a lot about
Ukraine and Nazi Germany,

01:24:34.120 --> 01:24:35.920
so the '30s and
the '40s and so on,

01:24:35.920 --> 01:24:39.370
and you get to see how
much the absurdity turns

01:24:39.370 --> 01:24:40.490
to evil quickly.

01:24:40.490 --> 01:24:41.848
One of the things I worry--

01:24:41.848 --> 01:24:43.390
one of the things
I really don't like

01:24:43.390 --> 01:24:45.520
to see on Twitter
and the internet

01:24:45.520 --> 01:24:48.850
is how many statements
end with LOL.

01:24:48.850 --> 01:24:55.360
It's like you think just because
something is kind of funny

01:24:55.360 --> 01:24:58.030
or is funny or is
legitimately funny,

01:24:58.030 --> 01:25:02.360
it also doesn't have a
deep effect on society.

01:25:02.360 --> 01:25:05.680
So that's such a
difficult gray area

01:25:05.680 --> 01:25:09.760
because some of the best
comedy is dark and mean,

01:25:09.760 --> 01:25:12.670
but it reveals some important
truth that we need to consider.

01:25:12.670 --> 01:25:16.510
But sometimes comedy
is just covering up

01:25:16.510 --> 01:25:20.950
for destructive
ideology, and you

01:25:20.950 --> 01:25:23.260
have to know the line
between those two.

01:25:23.260 --> 01:25:26.950
Hitler was seen as a joke in
the late '20s and the '30s

01:25:26.950 --> 01:25:30.610
in Nazi Germany until the
joke became very serious.

01:25:30.610 --> 01:25:33.280
You have to be careful
to know the difference

01:25:33.280 --> 01:25:36.730
between the joke and the
reality and do all that.

01:25:36.730 --> 01:25:38.770
I mean, in a conversation--

01:25:38.770 --> 01:25:40.960
I'm just such a big
believer in conversation

01:25:40.960 --> 01:25:44.860
to be able to reveal something
through conversation,

01:25:44.860 --> 01:25:45.730
but I don't know.

01:25:45.730 --> 01:25:46.990
One of the big--

01:25:46.990 --> 01:25:49.420
you and I challenge
ourselves all the time.

01:25:49.420 --> 01:25:52.240
I don't know if I
have what it takes

01:25:52.240 --> 01:25:58.240
to have a good, empathetic,
but adversarial conversation.

01:25:58.240 --> 01:26:02.440
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I need to learn
more about this Tate person,

01:26:02.440 --> 01:26:03.580
or not learn about them.

01:26:03.580 --> 01:26:04.210
Yeah.

01:26:04.210 --> 01:26:05.860
It sounds like maybe
it's something to skip.

01:26:05.860 --> 01:26:08.500
I don't know because, again, I'm
not familiar with the content.

01:26:08.500 --> 01:26:10.450
But I was going to
ask you whether or not

01:26:10.450 --> 01:26:12.190
you've seeked out
or whether or not

01:26:12.190 --> 01:26:14.500
you would ever consider
having Donald Trump as a guest

01:26:14.500 --> 01:26:15.500
on your podcast.

01:26:15.500 --> 01:26:18.430
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah, I've
talked to Joe a lot about this

01:26:18.430 --> 01:26:28.680
and I really believe I can have
a good conversation with Donald

01:26:28.680 --> 01:26:35.320
Trump, but I haven't seen many
good conversations with him.

01:26:35.320 --> 01:26:38.890
So part of me thinks--

01:26:38.890 --> 01:26:41.260
part of me believes
it's possible,

01:26:41.260 --> 01:26:46.238
but he often effectively
runs over the interviewer.

01:26:46.238 --> 01:26:48.280
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You could
sit him down, give him

01:26:48.280 --> 01:26:50.260
an element in Athletic Greens.

01:26:50.260 --> 01:26:51.430
LEX FRIDMAN: Just relax.

01:26:51.430 --> 01:26:53.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That nice, cool, air

01:26:53.350 --> 01:26:56.290
conditioned black
curtain studio you've got

01:26:56.290 --> 01:26:58.480
and a different
side might come out.

01:26:58.480 --> 01:26:59.770
Context is powerful.

01:26:59.770 --> 01:27:01.880
LEX FRIDMAN: Well, Joe's
really good at this,

01:27:01.880 --> 01:27:03.520
which is relaxing the person.

01:27:03.520 --> 01:27:06.250
Like here, have a drink.

01:27:06.250 --> 01:27:08.830
Smoke a joint, or
whatever it is.

01:27:08.830 --> 01:27:11.020
But this energy of
just, let's relax,

01:27:11.020 --> 01:27:12.550
and there's laughter and so on.

01:27:12.550 --> 01:27:17.740
I don't think-- as
people know, I'm just

01:27:17.740 --> 01:27:19.460
not good at that kind of stuff.

01:27:19.460 --> 01:27:23.170
So I think the way I could have
a good conversation with him

01:27:23.170 --> 01:27:26.500
is to really understand
his worldview,

01:27:26.500 --> 01:27:28.180
be able to steel
man his worldview

01:27:28.180 --> 01:27:29.650
and those that support him.

01:27:29.650 --> 01:27:32.770
Which is, I'm sorry
to say for people

01:27:32.770 --> 01:27:35.290
who seem to hate Donald Trump,
is a very large percentage

01:27:35.290 --> 01:27:36.200
of the country.

01:27:36.200 --> 01:27:39.130
And so you have to really
empathize with those people.

01:27:39.130 --> 01:27:42.290
You have to empathize with
Donald Trump, the human being,

01:27:42.290 --> 01:27:46.850
and from that perspective,
ask him hard questions.

01:27:46.850 --> 01:27:48.350
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
So who do you think

01:27:48.350 --> 01:27:50.570
is the counterpoint
if you're going

01:27:50.570 --> 01:27:52.970
to seek balance in your guests.

01:27:52.970 --> 01:27:54.420
If you're going
to have Trump on,

01:27:54.420 --> 01:27:56.960
then you have to have who on?

01:27:56.960 --> 01:27:58.580
LEX FRIDMAN: Well,
that's interesting.

01:27:58.580 --> 01:28:01.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Anthony Fauci seems

01:28:01.250 --> 01:28:05.330
to be strongly associated
with counter values, at least

01:28:05.330 --> 01:28:07.910
in the eye of the public.

01:28:07.910 --> 01:28:10.675
I think he's retiring soon, but.

01:28:10.675 --> 01:28:12.050
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah,
he's retiring.

01:28:12.050 --> 01:28:15.380
So that's really
interesting, Anthony Fauci.

01:28:15.380 --> 01:28:18.500
Yeah, definitely, but I don't
think he's a counterbalance.

01:28:18.500 --> 01:28:21.920
He's a complicated,
fascinating figure

01:28:21.920 --> 01:28:25.500
who seems to have attracted
a lot of hate and distrust,

01:28:25.500 --> 01:28:26.000
but also--

01:28:26.000 --> 01:28:27.020
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
love from some people.

01:28:27.020 --> 01:28:27.530
LEX FRIDMAN: And love.

01:28:27.530 --> 01:28:29.322
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
love from some people.

01:28:29.322 --> 01:28:32.660
I mean, I know people, not
even necessarily scientists,

01:28:32.660 --> 01:28:35.540
who have pro-Fauci shirts.

01:28:35.540 --> 01:28:39.230
I've seen people with anti-Fauci
shirts, excuse me, certainly,

01:28:39.230 --> 01:28:40.693
but who adore him.

01:28:40.693 --> 01:28:42.860
There are people who adore
him in the same way there

01:28:42.860 --> 01:28:44.027
are people that adore Trump.

01:28:44.027 --> 01:28:47.750
It's so interesting that
one species of animal

01:28:47.750 --> 01:28:49.880
you get such divergent
neural circuitry.

01:28:49.880 --> 01:28:51.590
LEX FRIDMAN: It
almost feels like it's

01:28:51.590 --> 01:28:56.475
by design and every single topic
we find tension and division is

01:28:56.475 --> 01:28:57.350
fascinating to watch.

01:28:57.350 --> 01:29:01.430
I mean, I got to really witness
it from zero to a hundred

01:29:01.430 --> 01:29:07.340
in Ukraine, where there is
not huge significant division.

01:29:07.340 --> 01:29:10.860
There was in certain parts of
Ukraine, but across Europe,

01:29:10.860 --> 01:29:12.950
across the world there
was not that much division

01:29:12.950 --> 01:29:15.830
between Russia and Ukraine,
and it was just born

01:29:15.830 --> 01:29:18.740
overnight, this intense hatred.

01:29:18.740 --> 01:29:22.700
You see the same kind of stuff
with Fauci over the pandemic.

01:29:22.700 --> 01:29:27.830
At first we were all
huddled in uncertainty.

01:29:27.830 --> 01:29:29.653
There is a togetherness
with the pandemic.

01:29:29.653 --> 01:29:31.070
Of course, there
is more difficult

01:29:31.070 --> 01:29:32.210
because you're isolated.

01:29:32.210 --> 01:29:35.295
But then you start
to figure out--

01:29:35.295 --> 01:29:37.670
probably the politicians and
the media try to figure out,

01:29:37.670 --> 01:29:40.520
how can I take a side
here and how can I

01:29:40.520 --> 01:29:43.580
now start reporting on
this side or that side

01:29:43.580 --> 01:29:45.300
and say how the
other side is wrong?

01:29:45.300 --> 01:29:51.700
And so I think Anthony Fauci
is a part of just being used

01:29:51.700 --> 01:29:53.470
as a scapegoat
for certain things

01:29:53.470 --> 01:29:56.890
as part of that kind of
narrative of division.

01:29:56.890 --> 01:30:03.320
But I think-- so Trump is
a singular figure that,

01:30:03.320 --> 01:30:05.880
to me, represents something
important in American history.

01:30:05.880 --> 01:30:08.480
I'm not sure what
that is, but I think

01:30:08.480 --> 01:30:11.300
you have to think-- you
put on your historian hat,

01:30:11.300 --> 01:30:14.180
go forward in time,
and think back.

01:30:14.180 --> 01:30:18.530
How will he be remembered 20,
30, 40, 50 years from now?

01:30:18.530 --> 01:30:20.150
Who is the opposite of that?

01:30:22.900 --> 01:30:24.890
You have to--

01:30:24.890 --> 01:30:26.780
I would really have
to think about that

01:30:26.780 --> 01:30:28.640
because Trump was so singular.

01:30:28.640 --> 01:30:31.190
I think AOC is an
interesting one,

01:30:31.190 --> 01:30:35.360
but she's so young it's
unclear to know how--

01:30:35.360 --> 01:30:40.640
if she represents a legitimately
large scale movement or not.

01:30:40.640 --> 01:30:42.920
Bernie Sanders is an
interesting option,

01:30:42.920 --> 01:30:46.130
but I wish he would be
30, 40 years younger.

01:30:46.130 --> 01:30:47.780
The young Bernie
would be a good--

01:30:47.780 --> 01:30:50.030
ANDREW HUBERMAN: There are
scientists working on that.

01:30:50.030 --> 01:30:52.547
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah, I think so.

01:30:52.547 --> 01:30:54.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Not
him specifically, but--

01:30:54.380 --> 01:30:56.240
LEX FRIDMAN: Well, yeah.

01:30:56.240 --> 01:30:57.187
Maybe him.

01:30:57.187 --> 01:30:57.770
We never know.

01:30:57.770 --> 01:31:00.440
There is a big conspiracy
theory that Putin is--

01:31:00.440 --> 01:31:03.080
that that's a body double.

01:31:03.080 --> 01:31:04.105
It's no longer him.

01:31:04.105 --> 01:31:05.480
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Bernie is Putin?

01:31:05.480 --> 01:31:06.802
LEX FRIDMAN: No, no, no, no.

01:31:06.802 --> 01:31:09.260
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm having a
hard time merging that image.

01:31:09.260 --> 01:31:11.385
LEX FRIDMAN: The conspiracy
theory is-- no, no, no.

01:31:11.385 --> 01:31:15.350
That the Putin we see on
camera today is a body double.

01:31:15.350 --> 01:31:18.140
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
one thing that in science,

01:31:18.140 --> 01:31:21.950
and in particular,
in anatomy, there's

01:31:21.950 --> 01:31:25.910
a classification scheme for
different types of anatomists,

01:31:25.910 --> 01:31:29.210
which they either say you're
a lumper or a splitter.

01:31:29.210 --> 01:31:31.430
Some people like to call a
whole structure something,

01:31:31.430 --> 01:31:33.150
not necessarily
just for simplicity

01:31:33.150 --> 01:31:34.502
but for a lot of reasons.

01:31:34.502 --> 01:31:36.710
And then other people like
to microdivide the nucleus

01:31:36.710 --> 01:31:37.580
into multiple names.

01:31:37.580 --> 01:31:39.470
And of course, people used to
be able to name different brain

01:31:39.470 --> 01:31:40.890
structures after themselves.

01:31:40.890 --> 01:31:45.860
So that would be the nucleus of
Lex and the Huberman vesiculus

01:31:45.860 --> 01:31:47.600
or whatever.

01:31:47.600 --> 01:31:50.210
Less of that nowadays.

01:31:50.210 --> 01:31:54.090
And by the way, those structures
don't actually exist just yet.

01:31:54.090 --> 01:31:55.613
We haven't defined those yet.

01:31:55.613 --> 01:31:56.780
I was making those names up.

01:31:56.780 --> 01:32:01.820
But what's interesting is it
seems like in the last five

01:32:01.820 --> 01:32:05.270
years, there's been
a lot of trend--

01:32:05.270 --> 01:32:08.930
there's been a trend, excuse
me, toward a requirement

01:32:08.930 --> 01:32:10.190
for lumping.

01:32:10.190 --> 01:32:13.950
You can't say-- it seems that
it's not allowed, if you will,

01:32:13.950 --> 01:32:16.310
to say, hey, yeah, you know--

01:32:16.310 --> 01:32:17.980
and here I'm not stating my--

01:32:17.980 --> 01:32:19.490
I will never reveal
my preferences

01:32:19.490 --> 01:32:23.900
about pandemic related things
for hopefully obvious reasons.

01:32:23.900 --> 01:32:26.780
Some people will say
vaccines, yes, but masks, no.

01:32:26.780 --> 01:32:30.077
Or vaccines and masks,
yes, but let people work.

01:32:30.077 --> 01:32:32.160
And other people will say,
no, everyone stay home.

01:32:32.160 --> 01:32:34.610
And then other people will
say, no, no vaccines, no masks.

01:32:34.610 --> 01:32:35.510
Let everybody work.

01:32:35.510 --> 01:32:38.610
No one was saying no vaccines,
no masks, and stay home,

01:32:38.610 --> 01:32:40.130
I don't think.

01:32:40.130 --> 01:32:45.500
So there's this sort
of lumping, right?

01:32:45.500 --> 01:32:49.790
The boundaries around
ideology really

01:32:49.790 --> 01:32:51.732
did start to defy science.

01:32:51.732 --> 01:32:52.940
I mean, it wasn't scientific.

01:32:52.940 --> 01:32:56.810
It was one part science-ish
at times and sometimes

01:32:56.810 --> 01:32:57.920
really hardcore science.

01:32:57.920 --> 01:32:59.780
Other times it was
politics, economics.

01:32:59.780 --> 01:33:01.610
I mean, we really
saw the confluence

01:33:01.610 --> 01:33:03.710
of all these different
domains of society

01:33:03.710 --> 01:33:07.190
that use very different
criteria to evaluate the world.

01:33:07.190 --> 01:33:09.920
I mean, as a
scientist, I remember

01:33:09.920 --> 01:33:11.990
when the vaccines
first came out and I

01:33:11.990 --> 01:33:14.570
asked somebody, one
of the early concerns

01:33:14.570 --> 01:33:18.840
I had that was actually
satisfied for me was,

01:33:18.840 --> 01:33:20.257
how does this thing turn off?

01:33:20.257 --> 01:33:22.340
If you start generating
mRNA, how does it actually

01:33:22.340 --> 01:33:22.965
get turned off?

01:33:22.965 --> 01:33:26.360
So I asked a friend, they
know a lot about RNA biology.

01:33:26.360 --> 01:33:28.400
And I said, you know,
how does it turn off?

01:33:28.400 --> 01:33:31.565
They explained it to me and
I was like, OK, makes sense.

01:33:31.565 --> 01:33:34.370
I asked some other questions.

01:33:34.370 --> 01:33:36.680
But most people aren't
going to think about it

01:33:36.680 --> 01:33:38.510
at that level of
detail necessarily,

01:33:38.510 --> 01:33:40.760
but it did seem
that there was just

01:33:40.760 --> 01:33:43.430
kind of amorphous
blobs of ideology

01:33:43.430 --> 01:33:45.380
that they grabbed on
to things and then

01:33:45.380 --> 01:33:48.260
there was this need for
a chasm between them.

01:33:48.260 --> 01:33:52.100
It was almost felt like it
became illegal, in some ways,

01:33:52.100 --> 01:33:56.630
to want two of the things from
that menu and one of the things

01:33:56.630 --> 01:33:57.320
from that menu.

01:33:57.320 --> 01:33:59.090
I really felt like I
was being constrained

01:33:59.090 --> 01:34:01.490
by a kind of like
Bento box model

01:34:01.490 --> 01:34:04.220
where I didn't get to define
what was in the Bento box.

01:34:04.220 --> 01:34:08.150
I could either have Bento
box A or Bento box Z,

01:34:08.150 --> 01:34:09.297
but nothing in between.

01:34:09.297 --> 01:34:10.880
LEX FRIDMAN: And I
think on that topic

01:34:10.880 --> 01:34:15.620
and I think a lot of
topics, most people

01:34:15.620 --> 01:34:18.320
are in the middle with
humility, uncertainty,

01:34:18.320 --> 01:34:20.550
and they're just kind of
trying to figure it out.

01:34:20.550 --> 01:34:24.590
And I think there is just
the extremes defining

01:34:24.590 --> 01:34:26.505
the nature of this division.

01:34:26.505 --> 01:34:28.130
So I think it's the
role of a lot of us

01:34:28.130 --> 01:34:30.140
in our individual
lives, and also

01:34:30.140 --> 01:34:32.660
if you have a
platform of any kind,

01:34:32.660 --> 01:34:35.840
I think you have to try to walk
in the middle with the empathy

01:34:35.840 --> 01:34:36.440
and humility.

01:34:36.440 --> 01:34:40.440
And that's actually what science
is about is the humility.

01:34:40.440 --> 01:34:43.490
I'm still thinking about
who's the opposite of Trump.

01:34:43.490 --> 01:34:45.282
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
maybe there is none.

01:34:45.282 --> 01:34:47.180
I mean, maybe Fauci is
orthogonal to Trump.

01:34:47.180 --> 01:34:48.990
I mean, not everything
has an opposite.

01:34:48.990 --> 01:34:52.370
I mean, maybe he's
an n of 1 maybe

01:34:52.370 --> 01:34:54.320
he's in the minority
of one because he

01:34:54.320 --> 01:34:58.400
was an outsider from Washington
who then made it there.

01:34:58.400 --> 01:35:03.150
LEX FRIDMAN: But also I wonder--

01:35:03.150 --> 01:35:06.840
you have to pick your battles
because every battle you fight

01:35:06.840 --> 01:35:08.340
you should take very seriously.

01:35:08.340 --> 01:35:10.920
And just the amount
of hate I get,

01:35:10.920 --> 01:35:13.950
I got, and I still get
for having sat down

01:35:13.950 --> 01:35:17.550
with the Pfizer CEO, that was
a very valuable lesson for me.

01:35:17.550 --> 01:35:19.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, that
one got you a lot of heat?

01:35:19.860 --> 01:35:21.810
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah, it
still does because--

01:35:21.810 --> 01:35:24.333
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Because you
had some pretty controversial

01:35:24.333 --> 01:35:25.500
guests on from time to time.

01:35:25.500 --> 01:35:27.660
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah, that one--

01:35:27.660 --> 01:35:29.520
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Is he
still the Pfizer CEO?

01:35:29.520 --> 01:35:30.540
LEX FRIDMAN: I believe so.

01:35:30.540 --> 01:35:31.890
ANDREW HUBERMAN: CEOs
turn over like crazy.

01:35:31.890 --> 01:35:33.348
This is the thing
I didn't realize.

01:35:33.348 --> 01:35:36.370
In science if somebody moves
institutions it's a big deal.

01:35:36.370 --> 01:35:39.420
Most people don't have more
than two moves in their career,

01:35:39.420 --> 01:35:40.050
maybe.

01:35:40.050 --> 01:35:43.270
But they often move to the
next building is a big deal.

01:35:43.270 --> 01:35:45.570
But it in biotech--

01:35:45.570 --> 01:35:47.760
it's like have a former
colleague of mine

01:35:47.760 --> 01:35:49.440
from San Diego and
he's been a CEO here,

01:35:49.440 --> 01:35:50.357
then he's a CEO there.

01:35:50.357 --> 01:35:52.890
He went back to a company
he was a CEO before.

01:35:52.890 --> 01:35:54.390
He's probably back
at the university

01:35:54.390 --> 01:35:55.860
we worked at for all I know.

01:35:55.860 --> 01:35:57.810
It's amazing how much
moving around there.

01:35:57.810 --> 01:35:59.790
It is a very
itinerant profession.

01:35:59.790 --> 01:36:01.410
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah,
I think they're--

01:36:01.410 --> 01:36:03.900
in certain companies, I guess
in biotech would be the case,

01:36:03.900 --> 01:36:08.700
the CEO is more of like a
manager type so you can--

01:36:08.700 --> 01:36:10.920
jumping around benefits
your experience

01:36:10.920 --> 01:36:13.230
so you become better and
better being a manager.

01:36:13.230 --> 01:36:17.190
There's some leader
revolutionary CEOs

01:36:17.190 --> 01:36:19.620
that stick around for
longer because they're

01:36:19.620 --> 01:36:23.280
so critical to
pivoting a company,

01:36:23.280 --> 01:36:25.830
like the Microsoft
CEO currently.

01:36:25.830 --> 01:36:28.200
Sundar Pichai is
somebody like that.

01:36:28.200 --> 01:36:31.050
Obviously, Elon Musk is
somebody like that that

01:36:31.050 --> 01:36:34.660
is part of pivoting a company
into new domains constantly,

01:36:34.660 --> 01:36:35.160
but yeah.

01:36:35.160 --> 01:36:38.340
In biotech there's a machine.

01:36:38.340 --> 01:36:46.995
In the eyes of a lot of people,
big pharma is like big tobacco.

01:36:46.995 --> 01:36:51.780
It's the epitome of everything
that is wrong with capitalism.

01:36:51.780 --> 01:36:53.280
It's evil, right?

01:36:53.280 --> 01:36:55.380
And so I showed up
in the conversation

01:36:55.380 --> 01:36:57.330
where I thought with
a pretty open mind

01:36:57.330 --> 01:37:01.080
and really asked what I
thought were difficult

01:37:01.080 --> 01:37:02.490
questions of him.

01:37:02.490 --> 01:37:06.010
I don't think he's ever sat
down to a grilling of that kind.

01:37:06.010 --> 01:37:08.640
In fact, I'm pretty sure
they cut the interview short

01:37:08.640 --> 01:37:11.850
because of that, and
I thought literally it

01:37:11.850 --> 01:37:13.740
was hot in the room
and we're sweating

01:37:13.740 --> 01:37:15.840
and I was asking
tough questions.

01:37:15.840 --> 01:37:18.915
For somebody that half the
country or a large percent

01:37:18.915 --> 01:37:22.470
of the country believes he's
alleviated a lot of-- he

01:37:22.470 --> 01:37:26.700
helped, through the financial
resources that Pfizer has,

01:37:26.700 --> 01:37:29.173
helped alleviate a lot of
suffering in the world.

01:37:29.173 --> 01:37:30.840
And so I thought for
somebody like that,

01:37:30.840 --> 01:37:32.670
I was asking pretty
hard questions.

01:37:32.670 --> 01:37:36.420
Boy, did I get to
hear from the side--

01:37:36.420 --> 01:37:43.290
usually one of the sides is
more intense in their anger.

01:37:43.290 --> 01:37:48.550
So there are certain
political topics--

01:37:48.550 --> 01:37:52.480
like with Andrew
Tate, for example,

01:37:52.480 --> 01:37:56.230
I would hear from a very--

01:37:56.230 --> 01:37:58.510
it would probably be
the left, far left,

01:37:58.510 --> 01:38:01.330
that would write very angrily.

01:38:01.330 --> 01:38:03.670
And so that's a group
you'll hear from.

01:38:03.670 --> 01:38:08.200
The Pfizer CEO, I didn't
get almost any messages

01:38:08.200 --> 01:38:14.140
from people saying, why
did you go so hard on him?

01:38:14.140 --> 01:38:16.570
He's an incredible human,
incredible leader and CEO

01:38:16.570 --> 01:38:20.050
of a company that helped
us with the vaccine

01:38:20.050 --> 01:38:22.870
that nobody thought would be
possible to develop so quickly.

01:38:22.870 --> 01:38:24.070
ANDREW HUBERMAN: You did not
get letters of that sort?

01:38:24.070 --> 01:38:25.028
LEX FRIDMAN: I did not.

01:38:25.028 --> 01:38:28.150
I mean, here and there,
but the sea of people

01:38:28.150 --> 01:38:30.700
that said everything
from me being

01:38:30.700 --> 01:38:33.310
weak that I wasn't able
to call out this person,

01:38:33.310 --> 01:38:37.210
how do you sit down, how do
you platform this evil person,

01:38:37.210 --> 01:38:41.260
how do you make him look
human, all that kind of stuff.

01:38:41.260 --> 01:38:42.580
And you have to deal with that.

01:38:42.580 --> 01:38:45.670
You have to-- of
course, it's great.

01:38:45.670 --> 01:38:48.880
It's great because I have to
do some soul searching, which

01:38:48.880 --> 01:38:50.830
is like, did I?

01:38:50.830 --> 01:38:52.600
You have to ask
some hard questions.

01:38:52.600 --> 01:38:53.890
I love criticism like that.

01:38:53.890 --> 01:38:55.360
You get to--

01:38:55.360 --> 01:38:57.010
I had some low points.

01:38:57.010 --> 01:38:59.500
There's definitely some despair
and you start to wonder,

01:38:59.500 --> 01:39:01.830
was I too weak?

01:39:01.830 --> 01:39:04.230
Should I have talked to him?

01:39:04.230 --> 01:39:05.730
What is true?

01:39:05.730 --> 01:39:09.230
And you sit there alone
and just marinate in that.

01:39:09.230 --> 01:39:10.980
Hopefully over time
that makes you better,

01:39:10.980 --> 01:39:13.563
but I still don't know what the
right answer with that one is.

01:39:13.563 --> 01:39:17.310
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, I feel
that money plays a role here.

01:39:19.902 --> 01:39:22.740
When people think
big pharma, they

01:39:22.740 --> 01:39:24.550
think billions of dollars--

01:39:24.550 --> 01:39:26.490
maybe even trillions
of dollars, really.

01:39:26.490 --> 01:39:34.980
And certainly people who make a
lot of money get scrutiny that

01:39:34.980 --> 01:39:35.580
others don't.

01:39:35.580 --> 01:39:38.550
Part of it is that they are
often not always visible,

01:39:38.550 --> 01:39:42.450
but I think that there is
a natural and reflexive--

01:39:42.450 --> 01:39:44.310
and I'm not justifying it.

01:39:44.310 --> 01:39:46.800
I certainly don't
feel this because I

01:39:46.800 --> 01:39:49.020
know some people who
are very wealthy,

01:39:49.020 --> 01:39:50.970
some people who are very poor.

01:39:50.970 --> 01:39:54.670
I can't say it scales
with happiness at all.

01:39:54.670 --> 01:39:58.890
People are always
shocked to hear that,

01:39:58.890 --> 01:40:02.100
but that's what I've observed
in very wealthy people.

01:40:02.100 --> 01:40:04.590
But that people who
have a lot of money

01:40:04.590 --> 01:40:07.800
are often held to a
different standard

01:40:07.800 --> 01:40:11.700
because people resent
that, some people resent

01:40:11.700 --> 01:40:14.700
that, and maybe there are
other reasons as well.

01:40:14.700 --> 01:40:17.850
I mean, among people
who are very wealthy,

01:40:17.850 --> 01:40:21.510
oftentimes the wish
is for status, right?

01:40:21.510 --> 01:40:22.050
Not money.

01:40:22.050 --> 01:40:23.940
You get a bunch of
billionaires in a room,

01:40:23.940 --> 01:40:26.910
and unless one of
them is Elon, who

01:40:26.910 --> 01:40:30.900
also has immense status
for his accomplishments,

01:40:30.900 --> 01:40:33.450
typically if you put a
Nobel Prize winner in a room

01:40:33.450 --> 01:40:34.980
with a bunch of
billionaires they're

01:40:34.980 --> 01:40:36.780
all talking to that person.

01:40:36.780 --> 01:40:37.440
Right?

01:40:37.440 --> 01:40:39.930
And there are many very
interesting billionaires.

01:40:39.930 --> 01:40:47.460
But status is something that is
often but not always associated

01:40:47.460 --> 01:40:53.170
with money, but is a much
rarer form of uniqueness

01:40:53.170 --> 01:40:55.210
out there, a
positive uniqueness--

01:40:55.210 --> 01:40:59.740
if one considers status positive
because there's a downside to.

01:40:59.740 --> 01:41:03.910
So I wonder whether or not the
Pfizer CEO caught extra heat

01:41:03.910 --> 01:41:05.860
because people
assume, and I probably

01:41:05.860 --> 01:41:08.500
assume also, that his
salary is quite immense.

01:41:08.500 --> 01:41:09.250
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.

01:41:09.250 --> 01:41:11.203
So because I have a
lot of data on this.

01:41:11.203 --> 01:41:11.870
I can answer it.

01:41:11.870 --> 01:41:13.640
It's a very good hypothesis.

01:41:13.640 --> 01:41:15.100
Let's test that scientifically.

01:41:15.100 --> 01:41:16.810
ANDREW HUBERMAN: He's about to
tell me it's a great hypothesis

01:41:16.810 --> 01:41:18.100
but it's wrong.

01:41:18.100 --> 01:41:19.060
I know the smirk.

01:41:19.060 --> 01:41:19.842
I know the smirk.

01:41:19.842 --> 01:41:21.550
LEX FRIDMAN: I honestly
think it's wrong.

01:41:21.550 --> 01:41:23.770
That effect is there
for a lot of people,

01:41:23.770 --> 01:41:28.120
but I think the distrust
is not towards the CEO.

01:41:28.120 --> 01:41:29.800
The distrust is
towards the company.

01:41:29.800 --> 01:41:32.200
One of the really
difficult soul searching I

01:41:32.200 --> 01:41:36.160
had to do, which is just having
to interact with Pfizer folks

01:41:36.160 --> 01:41:39.530
at every level, from
junior to the CEO,

01:41:39.530 --> 01:41:41.910
they're all really nice people.

01:41:41.910 --> 01:41:43.200
They have a mission.

01:41:43.200 --> 01:41:45.522
They talk about trying
to really help people

01:41:45.522 --> 01:41:46.980
because that's the
best way to make

01:41:46.980 --> 01:41:49.170
money is to come
up with medicine

01:41:49.170 --> 01:41:51.060
that helps a lot of people.

01:41:51.060 --> 01:41:52.710
The mission is clear.

01:41:52.710 --> 01:41:55.890
They're all good people, a lot
of really brilliant people,

01:41:55.890 --> 01:41:57.240
PhDs.

01:41:57.240 --> 01:42:00.660
So you can have a system
where all the people are good,

01:42:00.660 --> 01:42:01.740
including the CEO.

01:42:01.740 --> 01:42:04.470
And by good, I mean
people that really

01:42:04.470 --> 01:42:05.940
are trying to do everything.

01:42:05.940 --> 01:42:08.580
They dedicate their
whole life to do good.

01:42:08.580 --> 01:42:14.400
And yet, you have to think
that that system can deviate

01:42:14.400 --> 01:42:19.200
from a path that does good
because you start to deceive

01:42:19.200 --> 01:42:21.840
yourself of what is good,
you turn it into a game

01:42:21.840 --> 01:42:25.470
where money does come into
play from a company perspective

01:42:25.470 --> 01:42:28.750
where you convince yourself
the more money you make,

01:42:28.750 --> 01:42:30.280
the more good you'll
be able to do.

01:42:30.280 --> 01:42:33.240
And then you start to focus
more and more and more

01:42:33.240 --> 01:42:38.070
on making more money, and then
you can really deviate and lose

01:42:38.070 --> 01:42:40.050
track of what is actually good.

01:42:40.050 --> 01:42:42.540
I'm not saying necessarily
Pfizer does that,

01:42:42.540 --> 01:42:44.040
but I think companies
could do that.

01:42:44.040 --> 01:42:47.130
You can apply that criticism
to social media companies,

01:42:47.130 --> 01:42:49.770
to big pharma companies.

01:42:49.770 --> 01:42:51.930
One of the big lessons for me--

01:42:51.930 --> 01:42:55.470
I don't know what the answer
is, but that all the people

01:42:55.470 --> 01:42:58.175
inside the company
can be good, people

01:42:58.175 --> 01:42:59.800
you would want to
hang out with, people

01:42:59.800 --> 01:43:04.500
you would want to work with,
but as a company is doing evil.

01:43:04.500 --> 01:43:05.610
That's a possibility.

01:43:05.610 --> 01:43:09.090
So the distrust I don't think
is towards the billionaire

01:43:09.090 --> 01:43:12.390
individual, which I do
see a lot of in this case.

01:43:12.390 --> 01:43:15.540
I think it's Wall
Street distrust,

01:43:15.540 --> 01:43:19.350
that the machinery of this
particular organization

01:43:19.350 --> 01:43:20.508
has gone off track.

01:43:20.508 --> 01:43:22.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It's the
generalization of hate again.

01:43:22.800 --> 01:43:23.760
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.

01:43:23.760 --> 01:43:27.075
And then good luck
figuring out what is true.

01:43:27.075 --> 01:43:28.290
This is the tough stuff.

01:43:28.290 --> 01:43:32.640
But I should say
the individuals--

01:43:32.640 --> 01:43:38.820
individual scientists
at the NIH and Pfizer

01:43:38.820 --> 01:43:42.411
are just incredible people.

01:43:42.411 --> 01:43:45.090
They're really brilliant people.

01:43:45.090 --> 01:43:47.910
I never trust the administration
or the business people--

01:43:47.910 --> 01:43:49.350
no offense, business people.

01:43:49.350 --> 01:43:52.260
But the scientists
are always good.

01:43:52.260 --> 01:43:55.230
They have the right
motivator in life.

01:43:55.230 --> 01:43:57.930
But again, they can
have blinders on.

01:43:57.930 --> 01:43:59.280
Too focused on the science.

01:43:59.280 --> 01:44:01.020
Nazi Germany has a
history of people

01:44:01.020 --> 01:44:03.120
just too focused on
the science and then

01:44:03.120 --> 01:44:05.702
the politicians use the
scientists to achieve

01:44:05.702 --> 01:44:06.660
whatever end they want.

01:44:06.660 --> 01:44:11.610
But if you just look narrowly
at the journey of a scientist,

01:44:11.610 --> 01:44:13.830
it's a beautiful one
because they're ultimately

01:44:13.830 --> 01:44:17.100
in it for the
curiosity, the moment

01:44:17.100 --> 01:44:19.320
of discovery versus money.

01:44:19.320 --> 01:44:22.440
I mean, prestige probably does
come into play later in life,

01:44:22.440 --> 01:44:25.470
but especially young scientists.

01:44:25.470 --> 01:44:28.740
They're after the--
it's like they're

01:44:28.740 --> 01:44:30.270
pulling at the
threat of curiosity

01:44:30.270 --> 01:44:31.945
to try to discover
something big.

01:44:31.945 --> 01:44:33.570
They get excited by
that kind of stuff,

01:44:33.570 --> 01:44:35.130
and it's beautiful to see.

01:44:35.130 --> 01:44:35.970
ANDREW HUBERMAN: It
is beautiful see.

01:44:35.970 --> 01:44:37.345
I have a former
graduate student,

01:44:37.345 --> 01:44:39.750
now a postdoc at
Caltech, and I don't even

01:44:39.750 --> 01:44:40.870
know she had a cell phone.

01:44:40.870 --> 01:44:43.680
She would come in the lab, put
her cell phone into the desk,

01:44:43.680 --> 01:44:45.510
and she was
tremendously productive.

01:44:45.510 --> 01:44:48.270
But that wasn't why
I brought it up.

01:44:48.270 --> 01:44:51.180
She was productive as a side
effect of just being absolutely

01:44:51.180 --> 01:44:53.160
committed and
obsessed to discover

01:44:53.160 --> 01:44:54.660
the answers to the
questions she was

01:44:54.660 --> 01:44:58.230
asking as best she could, and
it was-- you could feel it.

01:44:58.230 --> 01:45:02.130
You could just feel the
intensity, and just incredibly

01:45:02.130 --> 01:45:03.210
low activation energy.

01:45:03.210 --> 01:45:06.690
If there was an experiment to
do she would just go do it.

01:45:06.690 --> 01:45:08.490
You're teaching at MIT.

01:45:08.490 --> 01:45:12.030
You are obviously
traveling the world,

01:45:12.030 --> 01:45:14.760
you're right on the podcast
a lot of coverage of chess

01:45:14.760 --> 01:45:16.090
recently, which is interesting.

01:45:16.090 --> 01:45:17.170
I don't play chess but--

01:45:17.170 --> 01:45:18.840
LEX FRIDMAN: Oh, I have
some scientific questions

01:45:18.840 --> 01:45:19.530
to you about that.

01:45:19.530 --> 01:45:20.530
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, OK.

01:45:20.530 --> 01:45:22.020
Sure.

01:45:22.020 --> 01:45:23.220
Let's get to those for sure.

01:45:23.220 --> 01:45:23.790
And then--

01:45:23.790 --> 01:45:24.690
LEX FRIDMAN: You're
not going to like it.

01:45:24.690 --> 01:45:25.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, no.

01:45:25.690 --> 01:45:26.460
OK.

01:45:26.460 --> 01:45:29.100
And then also some very--

01:45:29.100 --> 01:45:31.552
do I have to spell
Massachusetts again?

01:45:31.552 --> 01:45:34.230
LEX FRIDMAN: Of course.

01:45:34.230 --> 01:45:36.180
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Also you still seem

01:45:36.180 --> 01:45:38.920
to have a proclivity for finding
guests that are controversial,

01:45:38.920 --> 01:45:39.420
right?

01:45:39.420 --> 01:45:40.920
You're thinking about Tate,
we're talking about Trump,

01:45:40.920 --> 01:45:43.770
we're talking about the Pfizer
CEO, we're talking about Fauci.

01:45:43.770 --> 01:45:45.730
These are intense people.

01:45:45.730 --> 01:45:48.060
And so what we're
getting folks is a--

01:45:48.060 --> 01:45:49.530
we're not doing
neuroimaging here

01:45:49.530 --> 01:45:51.600
in the traditional sense of
putting someone into a scanner.

01:45:51.600 --> 01:45:53.142
What we're doing
here is we're using,

01:45:53.142 --> 01:45:56.327
as the great Karl Deisseroth,
who was on your podcast--

01:45:56.327 --> 01:45:57.660
LEX FRIDMAN: Thank you for that.

01:45:57.660 --> 01:45:58.710
Thank you for connecting us.

01:45:58.710 --> 01:45:59.860
He's an incredible person.

01:45:59.860 --> 01:46:01.902
ANDREW HUBERMAN: He's an
incredible psychiatrist,

01:46:01.902 --> 01:46:03.990
bioengineer, and human
being and writer,

01:46:03.990 --> 01:46:06.880
and your conversation
with him was phenomenal.

01:46:06.880 --> 01:46:08.550
I listened to it twice.

01:46:08.550 --> 01:46:10.500
I actually have taken notes.

01:46:10.500 --> 01:46:12.150
We talk about it
in this household.

01:46:12.150 --> 01:46:14.280
We really do.

01:46:14.280 --> 01:46:17.892
His description of
love is not to be

01:46:17.892 --> 01:46:19.350
missed, I'll just
leave it at that,

01:46:19.350 --> 01:46:21.517
because if I try and say
it I won't capture it well.

01:46:21.517 --> 01:46:25.890
But we're getting
a language based

01:46:25.890 --> 01:46:30.730
map of at least a portion
of Lex Fridman's brain here.

01:46:30.730 --> 01:46:35.010
So what else is going on
these days in that brain

01:46:35.010 --> 01:46:38.130
as it relates to robotics, AI?

01:46:38.130 --> 01:46:41.040
Our last conversation
was a lot about robots

01:46:41.040 --> 01:46:43.440
and the potential for
robot-human interaction.

01:46:43.440 --> 01:46:46.020
Even what is a robot, et cetera.

01:46:46.020 --> 01:46:50.642
Are you still working on robots
or focused on robots, and where

01:46:50.642 --> 01:46:53.100
is science showing up in your
life besides the things we've

01:46:53.100 --> 01:46:54.040
already talked about?

01:46:54.040 --> 01:46:55.582
LEX FRIDMAN: So I
think the last time

01:46:55.582 --> 01:46:57.240
we talked was before Ukraine.

01:46:57.240 --> 01:46:58.320
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yes.

01:46:58.320 --> 01:47:00.420
You were just about to leave.

01:47:00.420 --> 01:47:01.720
LEX FRIDMAN: Yes.

01:47:01.720 --> 01:47:02.220
I mean--

01:47:02.220 --> 01:47:03.090
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So
that's why I went on.

01:47:03.090 --> 01:47:04.350
I was like, you
know, this might be

01:47:04.350 --> 01:47:06.300
the last-- you said you
wanted to come out here

01:47:06.300 --> 01:47:06.967
before or after.

01:47:06.967 --> 01:47:08.790
I was like, come
out there before.

01:47:08.790 --> 01:47:10.200
I want to see you before you go.

01:47:10.200 --> 01:47:13.470
But here you are in the flesh.

01:47:13.470 --> 01:47:15.510
LEX FRIDMAN: So a lot of--

01:47:15.510 --> 01:47:18.543
just a lot of my mind has
been occupied, obviously,

01:47:18.543 --> 01:47:19.710
with that part of the world.

01:47:19.710 --> 01:47:22.980
But most of the
difficult struggles

01:47:22.980 --> 01:47:26.070
that I'm still going through
is that I haven't launched

01:47:26.070 --> 01:47:27.810
the company that
I want to launch

01:47:27.810 --> 01:47:31.030
and the company
has to do with AI.

01:47:31.030 --> 01:47:33.000
I mean, it's maybe a
longer conversation,

01:47:33.000 --> 01:47:36.150
but the ultimate dream is
to put robots in every home.

01:47:36.150 --> 01:47:40.260
But short term I see
there a possibility

01:47:40.260 --> 01:47:42.300
of launching a
social media company,

01:47:42.300 --> 01:47:46.170
and it's a nontrivial
explanation why that

01:47:46.170 --> 01:47:47.550
leads to robots in the home.

01:47:47.550 --> 01:47:50.220
But it's basically
the algorithms

01:47:50.220 --> 01:47:54.060
that fuel effective
social robotics, so

01:47:54.060 --> 01:47:56.650
robots that you can form
a deep connection with.

01:47:56.650 --> 01:48:00.060
And so I've been really-- yeah,
I've been building prototypes

01:48:00.060 --> 01:48:05.070
but struggling that
I don't have maybe,

01:48:05.070 --> 01:48:10.350
if I were to be critical,
the guts to launch a company.

01:48:10.350 --> 01:48:11.825
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Or the time.

01:48:11.825 --> 01:48:13.200
LEX FRIDMAN: Well,
it's combined.

01:48:13.200 --> 01:48:14.160
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I think
you've got the guts.

01:48:14.160 --> 01:48:17.190
I mean, it's clear if you'll
do an interview with the Pfizer

01:48:17.190 --> 01:48:19.110
CEO and you're considering
putting this Tate

01:48:19.110 --> 01:48:22.590
fellow on your podcast and
you've gone to the Ukraine

01:48:22.590 --> 01:48:26.310
that you have the guts.

01:48:26.310 --> 01:48:28.427
It means not doing quite
a lot of other things.

01:48:28.427 --> 01:48:29.760
LEX FRIDMAN: That's what I mean.

01:48:29.760 --> 01:48:34.560
It does take-- the thing
is, as many people know,

01:48:34.560 --> 01:48:37.650
when you fill your
day and you're busy,

01:48:37.650 --> 01:48:43.080
that busyness becomes an excuse
that you use against doing

01:48:43.080 --> 01:48:44.730
the things that scare you.

01:48:44.730 --> 01:48:46.830
A lot of people use
family in this way.

01:48:46.830 --> 01:48:51.300
You know, my wife,
my kids, I can't.

01:48:51.300 --> 01:48:54.210
When in reality some of the most
successful people have a wife

01:48:54.210 --> 01:48:57.970
and have kids and have
families and they still do it.

01:48:57.970 --> 01:49:01.020
And so a lot of times we can
fill the day with busy work,

01:49:01.020 --> 01:49:03.201
with--

01:49:03.201 --> 01:49:06.220
yeah, of course, I have podcasts
and all this kind of stuff.

01:49:06.220 --> 01:49:08.670
And they make me happy and
they're all-- they're wonderful

01:49:08.670 --> 01:49:11.290
and there's research,
there's teaching, and so on.

01:49:11.290 --> 01:49:13.710
But all of that can just serve
as an excuse from the thing

01:49:13.710 --> 01:49:17.040
that my heart says is
the right thing to do,

01:49:17.040 --> 01:49:20.190
and that's why I don't
have the guts, the guts

01:49:20.190 --> 01:49:22.050
to say no to
basically everything

01:49:22.050 --> 01:49:23.640
and then to focus all out.

01:49:23.640 --> 01:49:28.950
Because part of it is
I'm unlikely to fail

01:49:28.950 --> 01:49:31.920
at anything in my life
currently because I've already

01:49:31.920 --> 01:49:34.095
found a comfortable place.

01:49:34.095 --> 01:49:36.840
With a startup it's
mostly going to be--

01:49:36.840 --> 01:49:38.790
most likely going to
be a failure, if not

01:49:38.790 --> 01:49:41.190
an embarrassing failure.

01:49:41.190 --> 01:49:43.320
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
the machine learning data

01:49:43.320 --> 01:49:44.360
that I'm aware of--

01:49:44.360 --> 01:49:46.110
I don't know a lot
about machine learning,

01:49:46.110 --> 01:49:48.090
but within the realm
of neuroscience,

01:49:48.090 --> 01:49:51.540
say that a failure
rate of about 15%

01:49:51.540 --> 01:49:54.720
is optimal for
neuroplasticity and growth.

01:49:54.720 --> 01:49:57.960
Whether or not that translates
to all kinds of practices

01:49:57.960 --> 01:50:02.010
isn't clear, but getting
trials right 85% of the time

01:50:02.010 --> 01:50:04.890
seems to be optimal
for language learning,

01:50:04.890 --> 01:50:07.410
seems to be optimal
for mathematics,

01:50:07.410 --> 01:50:10.680
and it seems to be optimal
for physical pursuits

01:50:10.680 --> 01:50:11.580
on average, right?

01:50:11.580 --> 01:50:13.405
I'm sure I'm going--

01:50:13.405 --> 01:50:16.297
you have more machine
learning geeks

01:50:16.297 --> 01:50:18.630
that listen to your podcast
than listen to this podcast,

01:50:18.630 --> 01:50:21.930
but it doesn't mean you have
to fail on 15% of your weight

01:50:21.930 --> 01:50:22.500
sets, folks.

01:50:22.500 --> 01:50:24.000
I mean, it could be 16%.

01:50:24.000 --> 01:50:26.520
No, I'm just kidding.

01:50:26.520 --> 01:50:30.093
It's not exact, but it's a
pretty good rule of thumb.

01:50:30.093 --> 01:50:32.010
LEX FRIDMAN: I think a
lot of startup founders

01:50:32.010 --> 01:50:37.170
would literally murder
for 85% chance of success.

01:50:37.170 --> 01:50:41.010
I think given all the
opportunities I have,

01:50:41.010 --> 01:50:44.820
the skill set, the funding,
all that kind of stuff,

01:50:44.820 --> 01:50:47.670
my chances are relatively
high for success.

01:50:47.670 --> 01:50:50.460
But what relatively high
means in the startup world

01:50:50.460 --> 01:50:53.790
is still far, far below 85.

01:50:53.790 --> 01:50:55.890
You're talking about
single digit percentages.

01:50:55.890 --> 01:50:57.062
Most startups fail.

01:50:57.062 --> 01:50:58.770
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
I think it means--

01:50:58.770 --> 01:51:01.270
the decision to focus on the
company and not on other things

01:51:01.270 --> 01:51:04.860
means the decision to close
the hatch on dopamine retrieval

01:51:04.860 --> 01:51:07.650
from all these other things that
are very predictable sources

01:51:07.650 --> 01:51:09.450
of dopamine.

01:51:09.450 --> 01:51:12.450
Not that everything is
dopamine, but dopamine

01:51:12.450 --> 01:51:15.750
is, I think, the primary
chemical driver of motivation.

01:51:15.750 --> 01:51:19.380
If you know that you can get
some degree of satisfaction

01:51:19.380 --> 01:51:22.740
from scrolling social media
or from that particular cup

01:51:22.740 --> 01:51:24.610
of coffee, that's what
you're going to do.

01:51:24.610 --> 01:51:27.450
That's what you're going to
consume unless you somehow

01:51:27.450 --> 01:51:31.140
invert the algorithm
and you say,

01:51:31.140 --> 01:51:34.710
it's actually my
denial of myself

01:51:34.710 --> 01:51:37.290
drinking that coffee that's
going to be the dopamine.

01:51:37.290 --> 01:51:37.790
Right?

01:51:37.790 --> 01:51:39.045
LEX FRIDMAN: Oh, interesting.

01:51:39.045 --> 01:51:39.900
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
that's the beauty

01:51:39.900 --> 01:51:42.690
of having a forebrain is that
you can make those decisions.

01:51:42.690 --> 01:51:44.650
This is the essence,
I do believe,

01:51:44.650 --> 01:51:46.370
of what we see of David Goggins.

01:51:46.370 --> 01:51:47.370
There's much more there.

01:51:47.370 --> 01:51:51.550
There's a person that none
of us know and only he knows,

01:51:51.550 --> 01:51:52.110
of course.

01:51:52.110 --> 01:51:57.940
But the idea that the pain
is the source of dopamine.

01:51:57.940 --> 01:52:00.180
The limbic friction, as I
sometimes like to call it,

01:52:00.180 --> 01:52:01.320
is the source of dopamine.

01:52:01.320 --> 01:52:04.230
That runs counter to how
most nervous systems work,

01:52:04.230 --> 01:52:06.630
but it's decision based, right?

01:52:06.630 --> 01:52:09.240
It's not because his
musculature is a certain way

01:52:09.240 --> 01:52:11.760
or he had CRISPR or something.

01:52:11.760 --> 01:52:14.940
It's because he decides that.

01:52:14.940 --> 01:52:17.065
And I think that's
amazing, but what

01:52:17.065 --> 01:52:18.690
it means in terms of
starting a company

01:52:18.690 --> 01:52:22.200
and changing priorities is
a closing the hatch on all

01:52:22.200 --> 01:52:24.310
or many of the current
sources of dopamine

01:52:24.310 --> 01:52:27.000
so that you can derive
dopamine from the failures

01:52:27.000 --> 01:52:28.920
within this narrow
context, and there's

01:52:28.920 --> 01:52:34.243
a very reductionist view
and neurocentric view

01:52:34.243 --> 01:52:35.410
of what we're talking about.

01:52:35.410 --> 01:52:37.020
But I think about this a lot.

01:52:37.020 --> 01:52:40.590
I mean, the decision to choose
one relationship versus another

01:52:40.590 --> 01:52:43.590
is a decision to close down
other opportunities, right?

01:52:43.590 --> 01:52:47.640
So I think that the
decision to order one thing

01:52:47.640 --> 01:52:50.700
off the menu versus others
is the decision to close down

01:52:50.700 --> 01:52:51.940
those other hatches.

01:52:51.940 --> 01:52:54.700
So I think that you
absolutely can do it.

01:52:54.700 --> 01:52:57.930
It's just a question of,
can you flip the algorithm?

01:52:57.930 --> 01:52:58.680
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.

01:52:58.680 --> 01:53:01.472
Remap the source of
dopamine to something else.

01:53:01.472 --> 01:53:02.430
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Right.

01:53:02.430 --> 01:53:06.240
And maybe go out there
not to succeed but make

01:53:06.240 --> 01:53:08.670
the-- the journey is the
destination type thing,

01:53:08.670 --> 01:53:11.795
but when you're financially
vested in your time--

01:53:11.795 --> 01:53:13.170
and as far as I
know, we only get

01:53:13.170 --> 01:53:15.360
one life, at least
on this planet

01:53:15.360 --> 01:53:19.080
and you want to spend
that wisely, right?

01:53:19.080 --> 01:53:23.960
LEX FRIDMAN: And a lot of the
people that surround you--

01:53:23.960 --> 01:53:27.020
people are really
important, and I

01:53:27.020 --> 01:53:33.558
don't have people around me that
say you should do a start up.

01:53:33.558 --> 01:53:35.600
It's very difficult to
find such people because--

01:53:35.600 --> 01:53:37.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Is Austin
big startup culture right now?

01:53:37.250 --> 01:53:37.820
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah, it is.

01:53:37.820 --> 01:53:37.970
It is.

01:53:37.970 --> 01:53:40.260
But it doesn't make sense
for me to do a startup.

01:53:40.260 --> 01:53:42.778
This is what the people
that love me my whole life

01:53:42.778 --> 01:53:44.570
have been telling me,
it doesn't make sense

01:53:44.570 --> 01:53:46.340
what you're doing right now.

01:53:46.340 --> 01:53:48.260
Just do the thing you
were doing previously.

01:53:48.260 --> 01:53:49.130
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Why
do I get the sense

01:53:49.130 --> 01:53:50.420
that because they
are saying this

01:53:50.420 --> 01:53:51.470
you're apt to go against them?

01:53:51.470 --> 01:53:51.560
LEX FRIDMAN: No.

01:53:51.560 --> 01:53:53.360
Actually, I was never
that, unfortunately.

01:53:53.360 --> 01:53:56.120
Unfortunately, I need--

01:53:56.120 --> 01:53:59.630
I've talked to people I love,
my parents, family, and so on,

01:53:59.630 --> 01:54:00.380
friends.

01:54:00.380 --> 01:54:03.980
I'm one of those people that
needs unconditional support

01:54:03.980 --> 01:54:05.540
for difficult things.

01:54:05.540 --> 01:54:09.980
I know myself coaching
wise is good--

01:54:09.980 --> 01:54:12.860
so here's how I
get coached best.

01:54:12.860 --> 01:54:14.390
Let's say wrestling.

01:54:14.390 --> 01:54:20.030
I like a coach that says,
you want to win the Olympics?

01:54:20.030 --> 01:54:22.258
They will not-- if
I say I want to win

01:54:22.258 --> 01:54:24.050
the gold medal at the
Olympics in freestyle

01:54:24.050 --> 01:54:30.050
wrestling I want a coach that
doesn't blink once and hears me

01:54:30.050 --> 01:54:32.990
and believes that I
can do it, and then

01:54:32.990 --> 01:54:39.620
is viciously intense and
cruel to me on that pursuit.

01:54:39.620 --> 01:54:41.770
If you want to do
this, let's do this.

01:54:41.770 --> 01:54:42.650
Right?

01:54:42.650 --> 01:54:45.350
But that's support.

01:54:45.350 --> 01:54:48.050
That positivity, I don't--

01:54:48.050 --> 01:54:54.290
I'm never-- I'm not energized,
nor do I see that as love,

01:54:54.290 --> 01:54:56.060
a person saying--

01:54:56.060 --> 01:54:58.370
basically criticizing that.

01:54:58.370 --> 01:55:03.890
Saying, you're too old to win
the Olympic gold medal, right?

01:55:03.890 --> 01:55:06.590
Or all the things
you can come up with.

01:55:06.590 --> 01:55:09.710
That's not helpful to me
and I can't find a dopamine,

01:55:09.710 --> 01:55:14.570
or I haven't yet, a dopamine
source from the haters.

01:55:14.570 --> 01:55:16.970
Basically people that are
criticizing you, trying

01:55:16.970 --> 01:55:19.430
to prove them wrong.

01:55:19.430 --> 01:55:20.870
It never got me off.

01:55:20.870 --> 01:55:22.808
It never--

01:55:22.808 --> 01:55:25.100
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Whereas some
people seem to like that.

01:55:25.100 --> 01:55:28.340
I mean, David Goggins
seems to come to mind.

01:55:28.340 --> 01:55:29.960
He seems driven by many sources.

01:55:29.960 --> 01:55:30.920
He has access--

01:55:30.920 --> 01:55:32.780
I don't know because
I've never asked him,

01:55:32.780 --> 01:55:35.250
but if I were to
venture a guess,

01:55:35.250 --> 01:55:38.300
I'd say that he probably
has a lot of options

01:55:38.300 --> 01:55:41.540
inside his head as how to
push through challenge.

01:55:41.540 --> 01:55:45.650
Not just overcome pain,
but he'll post sometimes

01:55:45.650 --> 01:55:47.690
about the fact that
people will say

01:55:47.690 --> 01:55:51.350
this or people will do this
and talk about the pushback

01:55:51.350 --> 01:55:51.860
approach.

01:55:51.860 --> 01:55:54.290
He'll also talk
about the pushback

01:55:54.290 --> 01:55:56.330
approach that's purely
internal that doesn't

01:55:56.330 --> 01:55:57.320
involve anyone else.

01:55:57.320 --> 01:55:58.550
Great versatility there.

01:55:58.550 --> 01:55:59.300
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.

01:55:59.300 --> 01:56:00.680
There's literally
like a voice he

01:56:00.680 --> 01:56:05.810
yells that represents
some kind of devil that

01:56:05.810 --> 01:56:09.680
wants him to fail,
and he calls them

01:56:09.680 --> 01:56:12.440
bitch and all kinds of things
saying, you know, fuck you.

01:56:12.440 --> 01:56:13.490
I'm not.

01:56:13.490 --> 01:56:16.910
There's always an enemy and
he's going against that enemy.

01:56:16.910 --> 01:56:17.872
I mean, I wish--

01:56:17.872 --> 01:56:18.830
maybe that's something.

01:56:18.830 --> 01:56:20.163
I mean, it's really interesting.

01:56:20.163 --> 01:56:25.280
Maybe you can remap it this
way so that you can construct--

01:56:25.280 --> 01:56:27.170
that's a kind of
obvious mechanism.

01:56:27.170 --> 01:56:31.580
Construct an amorphous
blob that is a hater that

01:56:31.580 --> 01:56:33.170
wants you to fail, right?

01:56:33.170 --> 01:56:35.540
That's kind of the
David Goggins thing.

01:56:35.540 --> 01:56:41.900
And that blob says you're
too weak, you're too dumb,

01:56:41.900 --> 01:56:43.970
you're too old,
you're too fat, you're

01:56:43.970 --> 01:56:47.550
too whatever, and getting you
to want to quit and so on.

01:56:47.550 --> 01:56:49.760
And then you start getting
angry at that blob,

01:56:49.760 --> 01:56:51.290
and maybe that's
a good motivator.

01:56:51.290 --> 01:56:53.180
I haven't personally
really tried that.

01:56:53.180 --> 01:56:56.420
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, I've
had external challenge when

01:56:56.420 --> 01:57:00.260
I was a postdoc, very
prominent laboratory--

01:57:00.260 --> 01:57:01.930
several prominent
laboratories, in fact,

01:57:01.930 --> 01:57:03.680
were working on the
same thing that I was,

01:57:03.680 --> 01:57:05.180
and I was just
this lowly postdoc

01:57:05.180 --> 01:57:06.860
working on a project
pretty independent

01:57:06.860 --> 01:57:07.820
from the lab I was in.

01:57:07.820 --> 01:57:10.040
And there was
competition but there

01:57:10.040 --> 01:57:13.820
was plenty of room for everybody
to win, but in my head--

01:57:13.820 --> 01:57:17.030
and frankly, I won't
disclose who this is.

01:57:17.030 --> 01:57:20.600
And because there was some
legitimate competition there

01:57:20.600 --> 01:57:22.730
and a little bit of
friction-- not too much,

01:57:22.730 --> 01:57:25.160
healthy scientific friction--

01:57:25.160 --> 01:57:28.880
yeah, I might have pushed
a few extra hours or more,

01:57:28.880 --> 01:57:29.450
a little bit.

01:57:29.450 --> 01:57:33.290
I have to say, it
felt metabolizing.

01:57:33.290 --> 01:57:36.860
It felt catabolic, right?

01:57:36.860 --> 01:57:38.240
I couldn't be sustained by it.

01:57:38.240 --> 01:57:40.940
And I contrast that with
the podcast or the work

01:57:40.940 --> 01:57:42.890
that my laboratory
is doing now focused

01:57:42.890 --> 01:57:47.960
on stress and human performance,
et cetera, and it's pure love.

01:57:47.960 --> 01:57:49.400
It's pure curiosity and love.

01:57:49.400 --> 01:57:51.800
I mean, there are hard
days, but I never-- there's

01:57:51.800 --> 01:57:54.530
no adversary in the picture.

01:57:54.530 --> 01:57:57.845
They're the practical
workings of life that--

01:57:57.845 --> 01:58:00.470
LEX FRIDMAN: That was the thing
that Joe really inspired me on,

01:58:00.470 --> 01:58:03.440
and people do create
adversarial relationships

01:58:03.440 --> 01:58:06.110
in podcasting because you get--

01:58:06.110 --> 01:58:08.471
YouTubers do this.

01:58:08.471 --> 01:58:10.890
They hate seeing somebody
else be successful.

01:58:10.890 --> 01:58:17.060
There's a feeling of
jealousy, and some people even

01:58:17.060 --> 01:58:19.610
see that as healthy.

01:58:19.610 --> 01:58:23.030
Mr. Beast is somebody, some
of these popular YouTubers,

01:58:23.030 --> 01:58:26.452
how do they get 100 million
views and I only get 20 views?

01:58:26.452 --> 01:58:28.410
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Mr.
Beast devoted his entire--

01:58:28.410 --> 01:58:30.100
according to him,
his entire life

01:58:30.100 --> 01:58:32.700
he's been focused on becoming
this massive YouTube channel.

01:58:32.700 --> 01:58:34.908
LEX FRIDMAN: Well, that,
he's inspiring in many ways,

01:58:34.908 --> 01:58:36.540
but there's some
people that become

01:58:36.540 --> 01:58:44.610
famous for doing much less
insane pursuit of greatness

01:58:44.610 --> 01:58:45.690
than Mr. Beast.

01:58:45.690 --> 01:58:48.930
People become famous and
on social media and so on,

01:58:48.930 --> 01:58:50.970
and it's easy to
be jealous of them.

01:58:50.970 --> 01:58:53.910
One of the early things
I've learned from Joe just

01:58:53.910 --> 01:58:55.800
being a fan of his
podcast is how much

01:58:55.800 --> 01:58:57.180
he celebrated everybody.

01:58:57.180 --> 01:59:00.900
And again, maybe I ruined
my whole dopamine thing

01:59:00.900 --> 01:59:05.040
but I don't get energized by
people that become popular.

01:59:05.040 --> 01:59:07.240
In the podcasting
space and YouTube,

01:59:07.240 --> 01:59:08.670
it doesn't-- it's awesome.

01:59:08.670 --> 01:59:11.820
All of it is awesome and
I'm inspired by that.

01:59:11.820 --> 01:59:14.280
But the problem is that's
not a good motivator.

01:59:14.280 --> 01:59:16.935
Inspiration is like, oh,
cool, humans can do this.

01:59:16.935 --> 01:59:18.000
This is beautiful.

01:59:18.000 --> 01:59:19.140
But it's not--

01:59:19.140 --> 01:59:19.800
I'm looking.

01:59:19.800 --> 01:59:23.140
I'm looking for a
forcing function.

01:59:23.140 --> 01:59:25.350
That's why I gave away
the salary from MIT.

01:59:25.350 --> 01:59:27.900
I was hoping my bank
account had zero.

01:59:27.900 --> 01:59:31.450
That would be a forcing
function to be like, oh shit.

01:59:31.450 --> 01:59:31.950
You know?

01:59:31.950 --> 01:59:34.090
And you're not allowed
to have a normal job,

01:59:34.090 --> 01:59:35.160
so I wanted to launch--

01:59:35.160 --> 01:59:38.800
and then the podcast
becomes a source of income.

01:59:38.800 --> 01:59:39.943
So it's like, goddammit.

01:59:39.943 --> 01:59:40.860
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

01:59:40.860 --> 01:59:41.360
Yeah.

01:59:41.360 --> 01:59:44.030
Well, and here I have
to confess my biases.

01:59:47.250 --> 01:59:52.375
You are so good at what you
do in the realm of podcast--

01:59:52.375 --> 01:59:54.250
and you're excellent at
other things as well,

01:59:54.250 --> 01:59:57.840
I just have less
experience in those things.

01:59:57.840 --> 02:00:00.208
I know here I'm taking
the liberty of speaking

02:00:00.208 --> 02:00:01.750
for many, many people
in just saying,

02:00:01.750 --> 02:00:07.420
I sure as hell hope you
don't shut down the podcast.

02:00:07.420 --> 02:00:09.760
But as your friend
and as somebody

02:00:09.760 --> 02:00:12.610
who cares very deeply about
your happiness and your deeper

02:00:12.610 --> 02:00:16.060
satisfaction, if it's
in your heart's heart

02:00:16.060 --> 02:00:20.980
to do a company, well then,
damn it, do the company.

02:00:20.980 --> 02:00:22.930
LEX FRIDMAN: And a lot
of it I wouldn't even

02:00:22.930 --> 02:00:24.580
categorize as happiness.

02:00:24.580 --> 02:00:27.250
I don't know if you have
things like that in your life,

02:00:27.250 --> 02:00:31.533
but I'm probably the happiest
I could possibly be right now.

02:00:31.533 --> 02:00:32.950
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That's wonderful.

02:00:32.950 --> 02:00:34.792
LEX FRIDMAN: But
the thing is there's

02:00:34.792 --> 02:00:36.250
a longing for the
start up that has

02:00:36.250 --> 02:00:37.540
nothing to do with happiness.

02:00:37.540 --> 02:00:38.710
It's something else.

02:00:38.710 --> 02:00:39.580
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That's that itch.

02:00:39.580 --> 02:00:40.288
That's that itch.

02:00:40.288 --> 02:00:43.090
LEX FRIDMAN: I'm pretty sure
I'll be less happy because it's

02:00:43.090 --> 02:00:46.750
a really tough process.

02:00:46.750 --> 02:00:50.620
I mean, to whatever degree
you can extract happiness

02:00:50.620 --> 02:00:52.330
from struggle, yes, maybe.

02:00:52.330 --> 02:00:53.390
But I don't see it.

02:00:53.390 --> 02:00:55.300
I think I'll have some
very, very low points.

02:00:55.300 --> 02:00:58.540
There's a lot of people
who find companies--

02:00:58.540 --> 02:01:01.930
found companies know about.

02:01:01.930 --> 02:01:03.740
And I also want to
be in a relationship,

02:01:03.740 --> 02:01:06.580
I want to get married,
and sure as hell

02:01:06.580 --> 02:01:08.620
a startup is not
going to increase

02:01:08.620 --> 02:01:10.758
the likelihood of that.

02:01:10.758 --> 02:01:12.550
ANDREW HUBERMAN: We
could start up a family

02:01:12.550 --> 02:01:13.832
and start a company.

02:01:13.832 --> 02:01:15.040
LEX FRIDMAN: Well, that's a--

02:01:15.040 --> 02:01:17.020
I'm a huge believer
in that, which

02:01:17.020 --> 02:01:20.320
is get in a relationship
at a low point

02:01:20.320 --> 02:01:23.305
in your life, which is--

02:01:23.305 --> 02:01:23.862
[LAUGHTER]

02:01:23.862 --> 02:01:24.820
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Sorry.

02:01:24.820 --> 02:01:28.570
I'm not disputing your stance,
nor am I agreeing with it.

02:01:28.570 --> 02:01:31.210
It's just every once
in a while there's

02:01:31.210 --> 02:01:35.840
a Lex Fridmanism that hits a
particular circuit in my brain.

02:01:35.840 --> 02:01:37.090
I have to just laugh out loud.

02:01:37.090 --> 02:01:39.190
LEX FRIDMAN: I just
think that it's

02:01:39.190 --> 02:01:42.040
easy to have a relationship
when everything is good.

02:01:42.040 --> 02:01:46.330
The relationships that become
strong and are tested quickly

02:01:46.330 --> 02:01:48.520
are the ones when
shit is going down.

02:01:48.520 --> 02:01:50.687
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, then
there's hope for me yet.

02:01:56.818 --> 02:01:59.530
Before we sat down I was
having a conversation

02:01:59.530 --> 02:02:02.635
with my podcast
producer, who is a--

02:02:02.635 --> 02:02:06.550
I wouldn't say avid, rather he's
a rabid consumer of podcasts

02:02:06.550 --> 02:02:11.270
and finds these amazing
podcasts, small podcasts

02:02:11.270 --> 02:02:12.730
and unique episodes.

02:02:12.730 --> 02:02:15.490
Anyway, we were talking
about some stuff

02:02:15.490 --> 02:02:18.110
that he had seen and read
in the business sector,

02:02:18.110 --> 02:02:22.000
and he was talking about
the difference between job,

02:02:22.000 --> 02:02:23.740
career, and a calling, right?

02:02:23.740 --> 02:02:25.540
And I think he was
extracting this

02:02:25.540 --> 02:02:30.760
from conversations of CEOs
and founders, et cetera.

02:02:30.760 --> 02:02:34.120
I forget the specific
founders that

02:02:34.120 --> 02:02:35.650
brought this to light for him.

02:02:35.650 --> 02:02:38.980
But that this idea that
if you focus on a job

02:02:38.980 --> 02:02:41.400
you can make an income, and
hopefully you enjoy your job

02:02:41.400 --> 02:02:42.400
or not hate it too much.

02:02:42.400 --> 02:02:45.250
A career represents a
sort of, in my mind,

02:02:45.250 --> 02:02:46.900
a kind of series of
evolutions that one

02:02:46.900 --> 02:02:49.540
can go through-- junior
professor, tenure, et cetera.

02:02:49.540 --> 02:02:55.300
But a calling has a whole
other level of energetic pull

02:02:55.300 --> 02:02:57.610
to it because it
includes career and job

02:02:57.610 --> 02:03:00.130
and it includes this
concept of a life.

02:03:00.130 --> 02:03:03.310
It's very hard to draw the line
between a calling in career

02:03:03.310 --> 02:03:05.660
and a calling in the
other parts of your life.

02:03:05.660 --> 02:03:09.670
So the question,
therefore, is, do

02:03:09.670 --> 02:03:13.600
you feel a calling
to start this company

02:03:13.600 --> 02:03:19.810
or is it more of a compulsion
that irritates you?

02:03:19.810 --> 02:03:21.640
Is it something you
wish would go away

02:03:21.640 --> 02:03:24.372
or is it something that
you hope won't go away?

02:03:24.372 --> 02:03:26.080
LEX FRIDMAN: No, I
hope it won't go away.

02:03:26.080 --> 02:03:26.770
It's a calling.

02:03:26.770 --> 02:03:27.840
It's a calling.

02:03:27.840 --> 02:03:29.257
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
That's beautiful.

02:03:29.257 --> 02:03:34.510
LEX FRIDMAN: It's like
when I see a robot--

02:03:34.510 --> 02:03:36.610
when I first
interacted with robots,

02:03:36.610 --> 02:03:39.190
and it became even stronger the
more sophisticated the robots

02:03:39.190 --> 02:03:42.310
I interacted, with
I see a magic there.

02:03:42.310 --> 02:03:44.290
And you're like,
you look around,

02:03:44.290 --> 02:03:47.200
does anyone else see this magic?

02:03:47.200 --> 02:03:50.410
It's kind of like maybe
when you fall in love,

02:03:50.410 --> 02:03:54.160
like that feeling.

02:03:54.160 --> 02:03:55.712
Does anyone else
notice this person

02:03:55.712 --> 02:03:56.920
that just walked in the room?

02:03:56.920 --> 02:03:59.920
I feel that way
about robots, and I

02:03:59.920 --> 02:04:04.150
can elaborate what that
means but I'm not even sure I

02:04:04.150 --> 02:04:05.470
can convert it into words.

02:04:05.470 --> 02:04:11.930
I just feel like the
social integration

02:04:11.930 --> 02:04:16.170
of robots in society will create
a really interesting world.

02:04:16.170 --> 02:04:19.160
And our ability to
anthropomorphize

02:04:19.160 --> 02:04:22.400
when we look at a robot and
our ability to feel things

02:04:22.400 --> 02:04:24.890
when we look at a robot is
something that most of us

02:04:24.890 --> 02:04:26.750
don't yet experience,
but I think

02:04:26.750 --> 02:04:29.760
everybody will experience
in the next few decades.

02:04:29.760 --> 02:04:34.430
And I just want to be
a part of exploring

02:04:34.430 --> 02:04:37.520
that because it hasn't been
really thoroughly explored.

02:04:37.520 --> 02:04:39.650
The best roboticists
in the world

02:04:39.650 --> 02:04:42.430
are not currently working
on that problem at all.

02:04:42.430 --> 02:04:44.570
They try to avoid human
beings completely,

02:04:44.570 --> 02:04:47.122
and nobody's really
working that problem

02:04:47.122 --> 02:04:48.830
in terms of when you
look at the numbers.

02:04:48.830 --> 02:04:51.290
All the big tech companies
that are investing money,

02:04:51.290 --> 02:04:54.230
the closest thing
to that is Alexa

02:04:54.230 --> 02:04:59.270
and basically being a servant
to help tell you the weather

02:04:59.270 --> 02:05:00.660
or play music and so on.

02:05:00.660 --> 02:05:02.670
It's not trying to
form a deep connection.

02:05:02.670 --> 02:05:07.340
And so sometimes you
just notice the thing.

02:05:07.340 --> 02:05:10.290
Not only do I notice the magic.

02:05:10.290 --> 02:05:12.750
There's a gut
feeling, which I try

02:05:12.750 --> 02:05:16.030
not to speak to because
there's no track record,

02:05:16.030 --> 02:05:22.140
but I feel like I can be good
at bringing that magic out

02:05:22.140 --> 02:05:23.430
of the robot.

02:05:23.430 --> 02:05:26.740
And there's no data that
says I would be good at that,

02:05:26.740 --> 02:05:27.817
but there's a feeling.

02:05:27.817 --> 02:05:28.650
It's just a feeling.

02:05:31.253 --> 02:05:32.670
Because I've done
so many things--

02:05:32.670 --> 02:05:36.960
I love doing playing guitar,
all that kind of stuff, jujitsu.

02:05:36.960 --> 02:05:38.430
I've never felt that feeling.

02:05:38.430 --> 02:05:40.800
When I'm doing
jujitsu I don't feel

02:05:40.800 --> 02:05:44.880
the magic of the genius
required to be extremely good.

02:05:44.880 --> 02:05:46.770
At guitar I don't
feel any of that.

02:05:46.770 --> 02:05:51.285
But I've noticed that in
others, great musicians,

02:05:51.285 --> 02:05:56.160
they notice the magic
about the thing they do

02:05:56.160 --> 02:05:57.340
and they ran with it.

02:05:57.340 --> 02:06:00.030
And I just always thought--

02:06:00.030 --> 02:06:03.510
I think it had a different
form before I knew robots

02:06:03.510 --> 02:06:05.490
existed, before I existed.

02:06:05.490 --> 02:06:15.145
The form was more about
the magic between humans.

02:06:15.145 --> 02:06:18.340
I think of it as love, but
the smile that two friends

02:06:18.340 --> 02:06:20.480
have towards each other
when I was really young.

02:06:20.480 --> 02:06:24.280
And people would be excited
when they first know each other

02:06:24.280 --> 02:06:28.090
and notice each other,
and there's that moment

02:06:28.090 --> 02:06:31.110
that they share that
feeling together.

02:06:31.110 --> 02:06:33.430
I was like, wow, that's
really interesting.

02:06:33.430 --> 02:06:35.250
It is really interesting
that these two

02:06:35.250 --> 02:06:37.020
separate intelligent
organisms are

02:06:37.020 --> 02:06:40.050
able to connect all of a sudden
on this deep emotional level.

02:06:40.050 --> 02:06:42.075
It's like, huh.

02:06:42.075 --> 02:06:45.000
It's just beautiful to see,
and I notice the magic of that.

02:06:45.000 --> 02:06:50.580
And then when I started a
programming-- programming,

02:06:50.580 --> 02:06:53.880
period, but then programming
AI systems, you realize, oh,

02:06:53.880 --> 02:06:54.800
that could be--

02:06:54.800 --> 02:06:56.550
that's not just between
humans and humans.

02:06:56.550 --> 02:07:01.420
That could be humans and
other entities, dogs, cats,

02:07:01.420 --> 02:07:02.220
and robots.

02:07:02.220 --> 02:07:06.300
And so I-- for some reason
it hit me the most intensely

02:07:06.300 --> 02:07:08.190
when I saw robots.

02:07:08.190 --> 02:07:09.960
So yeah, it's a calling.

02:07:09.960 --> 02:07:21.290
But it's a calling that I can
just enjoy the vision of it,

02:07:21.290 --> 02:07:24.890
the vision of a future world, of
an exciting future world that's

02:07:24.890 --> 02:07:27.920
full of cool stuff, or I can
be part of building that.

02:07:27.920 --> 02:07:29.840
And being part of
building that means

02:07:29.840 --> 02:07:32.090
doing the hard work
of capitalism, which

02:07:32.090 --> 02:07:37.610
is like raising funds from
people, which for me, right

02:07:37.610 --> 02:07:40.815
now, is the easy part, and
then hiring a lot of people.

02:07:40.815 --> 02:07:43.190
I don't know how much you know
about hiring, but hiring--

02:07:43.190 --> 02:07:44.330
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Hiring
excellent people.

02:07:44.330 --> 02:07:45.747
LEX FRIDMAN:
Excellent people that

02:07:45.747 --> 02:07:47.840
will define the
trajectory of not only

02:07:47.840 --> 02:07:50.810
your company, but your whole
existence as a human being.

02:07:50.810 --> 02:07:54.050
And building it up, not
failing them because now

02:07:54.050 --> 02:07:57.920
they all depend on you,
and not failing the world

02:07:57.920 --> 02:08:00.290
with an opportunity
to bring something

02:08:00.290 --> 02:08:01.460
that brings joy to people.

02:08:01.460 --> 02:08:05.358
And all of that
pressure, just non-stop

02:08:05.358 --> 02:08:06.650
fires that you have to put out.

02:08:06.650 --> 02:08:09.590
The drama, the having
to work with people

02:08:09.590 --> 02:08:13.010
you've never worked with like
lawyers and human resources

02:08:13.010 --> 02:08:14.510
and supply chain.

02:08:14.510 --> 02:08:20.990
And because this is
very compute heavy,

02:08:20.990 --> 02:08:23.900
the computer infrastructure,
managing security,

02:08:23.900 --> 02:08:27.930
cybersecurity, because you're
dealing with people's data.

02:08:27.930 --> 02:08:32.360
So now you have to understand
not only the cybersecurity

02:08:32.360 --> 02:08:35.090
of data and the privacy, how
to maintain privacy correctly

02:08:35.090 --> 02:08:37.700
with data, but also the
psychology of people trusting

02:08:37.700 --> 02:08:38.660
you with their data.

02:08:38.660 --> 02:08:44.960
And how, if you look at Mark
Zuckerberg and Jack Dorsey

02:08:44.960 --> 02:08:46.880
and those folks,
they seem to be hated

02:08:46.880 --> 02:08:48.440
by a large number of people.

02:08:48.440 --> 02:08:49.690
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Jack seemed--

02:08:49.690 --> 02:08:50.338
I didn't--

02:08:50.338 --> 02:08:51.630
LEX FRIDMAN: Much less so, yes.

02:08:51.630 --> 02:08:54.620
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I think I
always think of Jack as a loved

02:08:54.620 --> 02:08:56.210
individual, but--

02:08:56.210 --> 02:08:58.490
LEX FRIDMAN: Well, yeah, you
have a very positive view

02:08:58.490 --> 02:08:59.240
of the world, yes.

02:08:59.240 --> 02:09:01.850
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I like Jack a
lot and I like his mind and I--

02:09:01.850 --> 02:09:05.600
someone close to him
described him to me

02:09:05.600 --> 02:09:08.545
recently as he's an
excellent listener.

02:09:08.545 --> 02:09:09.920
That's what they
said about Jack,

02:09:09.920 --> 02:09:12.110
and that's my
experience of him too.

02:09:12.110 --> 02:09:14.240
Very private person so
we'll leave it at that.

02:09:14.240 --> 02:09:20.456
But listen, I think
Jack Dorsey is

02:09:20.456 --> 02:09:25.520
one of the greats of
the last 200 years

02:09:25.520 --> 02:09:29.145
and is just much quieter
about his stance on things

02:09:29.145 --> 02:09:30.020
than a lot of people.

02:09:30.020 --> 02:09:33.030
But much of what we see in
the world that's wonderful,

02:09:33.030 --> 02:09:35.040
I think we owe him
a debt of gratitude.

02:09:35.040 --> 02:09:37.133
I'm just voicing my
stance here, but--

02:09:37.133 --> 02:09:38.300
LEX FRIDMAN: And the person.

02:09:38.300 --> 02:09:39.140
This is really important.

02:09:39.140 --> 02:09:40.057
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:09:40.057 --> 02:09:42.740
LEX FRIDMAN: A wonderful
person, a brilliant person,

02:09:42.740 --> 02:09:45.770
a good person,
but you still have

02:09:45.770 --> 02:09:50.060
to pay the price of making
any kind of mistakes

02:09:50.060 --> 02:09:51.530
as the head of a company.

02:09:51.530 --> 02:09:55.640
You don't get any extra bonus
points for being a good person.

02:09:55.640 --> 02:09:57.830
ANDREW HUBERMAN: But his
willingness to go on Rogan

02:09:57.830 --> 02:10:03.423
and deal directly and say,
I don't know an answer

02:10:03.423 --> 02:10:04.340
to that in some cases.

02:10:04.340 --> 02:10:06.980
But to deal directly with some
really challenging questions

02:10:06.980 --> 02:10:09.092
to me earned him
tremendous respect.

02:10:09.092 --> 02:10:09.800
LEX FRIDMAN: Yes.

02:10:09.800 --> 02:10:10.790
As an individual.

02:10:10.790 --> 02:10:13.040
He was still part of him--

02:10:13.040 --> 02:10:15.350
you've said-- OK,
and I love Jack too,

02:10:15.350 --> 02:10:16.770
and I interact with him often.

02:10:16.770 --> 02:10:17.780
ANDREW HUBERMAN: He's
been on your podcast.

02:10:17.780 --> 02:10:18.950
LEX FRIDMAN: Yes.

02:10:18.950 --> 02:10:21.830
But he's also part of a
system, as we talked about,

02:10:21.830 --> 02:10:25.640
and I would argue that Jack
shouldn't have brought anyone

02:10:25.640 --> 02:10:28.580
else with him on that podcast.

02:10:28.580 --> 02:10:29.120
If you go--

02:10:29.120 --> 02:10:29.360
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Oh, that's right.

02:10:29.360 --> 02:10:30.710
He had a cadre of--

02:10:30.710 --> 02:10:36.710
LEX FRIDMAN: Oh, he had I
guess the head legal with him.

02:10:36.710 --> 02:10:40.220
And also it requires a
tremendous amount of skill

02:10:40.220 --> 02:10:42.410
to go on a podcast
like Joe Rogan

02:10:42.410 --> 02:10:45.620
and be able to win over
the trust of people

02:10:45.620 --> 02:10:47.750
by being able to be
transparent and communicate

02:10:47.750 --> 02:10:50.385
how the company really works
because the more you reveal

02:10:50.385 --> 02:10:52.010
about how a social
media company works,

02:10:52.010 --> 02:10:55.100
the more you open
up for security,

02:10:55.100 --> 02:10:57.320
the vector of attacks increases.

02:10:57.320 --> 02:10:59.810
Also, there's a lot of
difficult decisions in terms

02:10:59.810 --> 02:11:01.850
of censorship and
not that are made

02:11:01.850 --> 02:11:03.890
that if you make
them transparent

02:11:03.890 --> 02:11:06.568
you're going to get an order
of magnitude more hate.

02:11:06.568 --> 02:11:08.610
So you have to make all
those kinds of decisions,

02:11:08.610 --> 02:11:13.430
and I think that's one of
the things I have to realize

02:11:13.430 --> 02:11:20.840
is you have to take that
avalanche of potentially

02:11:20.840 --> 02:11:23.330
hate if you make mistakes.

02:11:23.330 --> 02:11:26.960
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, you
have a very clear picture

02:11:26.960 --> 02:11:29.630
of this architecture
of what's required

02:11:29.630 --> 02:11:31.920
in order to create a company.

02:11:31.920 --> 02:11:34.102
Of course, there's
division of labor too.

02:11:34.102 --> 02:11:36.560
I mean, you don't have to do
all of those things in detail,

02:11:36.560 --> 02:11:39.920
but finding people that
are excellent to do--

02:11:39.920 --> 02:11:43.973
to run the critical
segments is obviously key.

02:11:43.973 --> 02:11:45.390
I'll just say what
I said earlier,

02:11:45.390 --> 02:11:49.550
which is if it's in your heart's
heart to start a company,

02:11:49.550 --> 02:11:51.200
if that, indeed,
is your calling,

02:11:51.200 --> 02:11:55.552
and it sounds like it
is, then I can't wait.

02:11:55.552 --> 02:11:57.260
LEX FRIDMAN: Does the
heart have a heart?

02:11:57.260 --> 02:11:57.710
I don't know.

02:11:57.710 --> 02:11:59.090
What's that
expression even mean?

02:11:59.090 --> 02:12:00.500
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Probably not.

02:12:00.500 --> 02:12:01.040
LEX FRIDMAN: We
romanticize the heart.

02:12:01.040 --> 02:12:02.810
ANDREW HUBERMAN: In my lab
at one point, early days

02:12:02.810 --> 02:12:05.270
we worked on cuttlefish, and
they have multiple hearts,

02:12:05.270 --> 02:12:07.430
but they pump green
blood, believe it or not.

02:12:07.430 --> 02:12:09.470
Very fascinating animal.

02:12:09.470 --> 02:12:13.760
Speaking of hearts
and green blood,

02:12:13.760 --> 02:12:15.890
earlier today
before we sat down I

02:12:15.890 --> 02:12:21.342
solicited four questions on
Instagram in a brief post.

02:12:21.342 --> 02:12:22.550
LEX FRIDMAN: Do you want to--

02:12:22.550 --> 02:12:23.120
ANDREW HUBERMAN: If you'll--

02:12:23.120 --> 02:12:24.620
LEX FRIDMAN: --look
at some of them?

02:12:24.620 --> 02:12:27.200
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yes, let's
take these in real time.

02:12:27.200 --> 02:12:29.540
My podcast team is always
teasing me that I never

02:12:29.540 --> 02:12:31.190
have any charge on my phone.

02:12:31.190 --> 02:12:35.690
I'm one of these people that
likes to run in the yellow,

02:12:35.690 --> 02:12:36.502
or whatever it is.

02:12:36.502 --> 02:12:37.460
LEX FRIDMAN: An iPhone?

02:12:37.460 --> 02:12:37.835
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:12:37.835 --> 02:12:39.920
LEX FRIDMAN: It's funny
how always the iPhone

02:12:39.920 --> 02:12:41.070
people are out of battery.

02:12:41.070 --> 02:12:41.570
It's weird.

02:12:41.570 --> 02:12:42.410
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
I just got a new one.

02:12:42.410 --> 02:12:42.710
LEX FRIDMAN: So weird.

02:12:42.710 --> 02:12:44.180
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I mean, this
one has plenty of battery.

02:12:44.180 --> 02:12:46.190
I just got a new one so
I have different numbers

02:12:46.190 --> 02:12:50.210
for different things,
personal and work, et cetera.

02:12:50.210 --> 02:12:52.550
I'm trying that now.

02:12:52.550 --> 02:12:53.900
All right.

02:12:53.900 --> 02:12:56.210
Get into the--

02:12:56.210 --> 02:12:58.765
LEX FRIDMAN: I have a chess
thing too to mention to you.

02:12:58.765 --> 02:13:00.140
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Oh, yes, please.

02:13:00.140 --> 02:13:03.387
Will I insult you if I look up
these questions as you ask me?

02:13:03.387 --> 02:13:04.220
LEX FRIDMAN: No, no.

02:13:04.220 --> 02:13:06.530
But I will insult you by asking
you this question because I

02:13:06.530 --> 02:13:07.520
think it's hilarious.

02:13:07.520 --> 02:13:10.190
So there's been a controversy
about cheating where

02:13:10.190 --> 02:13:12.527
Hans Niemann, who
is a 2,700 player--

02:13:12.527 --> 02:13:13.610
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, yeah.

02:13:13.610 --> 02:13:14.480
LEX FRIDMAN: --was
accused of cheating.

02:13:14.480 --> 02:13:15.800
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I saw that
clip on your clips channel.

02:13:15.800 --> 02:13:17.120
By the way, I love
your clips channel,

02:13:17.120 --> 02:13:18.537
but I listen to
your full channel.

02:13:18.537 --> 02:13:22.130
LEX FRIDMAN: The big accusation
is that he cheated by having--

02:13:22.130 --> 02:13:24.410
I mean, it's half
joke but it's starting

02:13:24.410 --> 02:13:27.410
getting me to wonder whether--

02:13:27.410 --> 02:13:32.600
so that you can cheat by having
vibrating anal beads so you

02:13:32.600 --> 02:13:34.772
can send messages to--

02:13:34.772 --> 02:13:36.980
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well, let's
rephrase that statement.

02:13:36.980 --> 02:13:39.060
Not you can, but one can.

02:13:39.060 --> 02:13:39.935
LEX FRIDMAN: One can.

02:13:39.935 --> 02:13:40.370
One can.

02:13:40.370 --> 02:13:40.910
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Yeah, thank you.

02:13:40.910 --> 02:13:42.785
LEX FRIDMAN: That was
a personal attack, yes.

02:13:42.785 --> 02:13:44.272
But it made me realize, I mean--

02:13:44.272 --> 02:13:46.730
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm just going
to adjust myself in my seat

02:13:46.730 --> 02:13:47.863
here.

02:13:47.863 --> 02:13:49.280
LEX FRIDMAN: I use
it all the time

02:13:49.280 --> 02:13:51.620
for podcasting to
send myself messages

02:13:51.620 --> 02:13:54.530
to remind myself of notes.

02:13:54.530 --> 02:13:55.520
But it's interesting.

02:13:55.520 --> 02:13:56.970
I mean, it--

02:13:56.970 --> 02:13:59.630
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I'm not
going to call you again.

02:13:59.630 --> 02:14:02.960
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah, that's
exactly where I keep my phone.

02:14:02.960 --> 02:14:05.660
It did get me down this
whole rabbit hole of, well,

02:14:05.660 --> 02:14:10.920
how would you be able to
send communication in order

02:14:10.920 --> 02:14:12.270
to cheat in different sports?

02:14:12.270 --> 02:14:15.900
I mean, that doesn't even have
to do with chess in particular,

02:14:15.900 --> 02:14:18.150
but it's interesting
in chess and poker

02:14:18.150 --> 02:14:22.830
that there's mechanisms
modern day where

02:14:22.830 --> 02:14:26.160
you're streaming live
the competition so people

02:14:26.160 --> 02:14:27.510
can watch it on TV.

02:14:27.510 --> 02:14:33.980
If they can only send
you a signal back, they--

02:14:33.980 --> 02:14:36.350
it's just a fun little
thing to think about

02:14:36.350 --> 02:14:38.540
and if it's possible
to pull off.

02:14:38.540 --> 02:14:43.410
So I wanted to get your
scientific evaluation of that--

02:14:43.410 --> 02:14:45.380
ANDREW HUBERMAN: To
cheat using some sort

02:14:45.380 --> 02:14:46.730
of interoceptive device?

02:14:46.730 --> 02:14:47.480
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah.

02:14:47.480 --> 02:14:48.438
Vibrating of some kind.

02:14:48.438 --> 02:14:48.938
Yeah.

02:14:48.938 --> 02:14:49.550
Or no, no.

02:14:49.550 --> 02:14:51.950
That's one way to send
signals is, like, Morse code,

02:14:51.950 --> 02:14:52.730
basically.

02:14:52.730 --> 02:14:53.647
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:14:53.647 --> 02:14:55.550
So there's a famous--

02:14:55.550 --> 02:14:57.950
I believe there's
a famous real world

02:14:57.950 --> 02:15:00.722
story of physics students--

02:15:00.722 --> 02:15:02.180
I'm going to get
some of this wrong

02:15:02.180 --> 02:15:05.162
so I'm saying this in
kind of coarse form

02:15:05.162 --> 02:15:06.620
so that somebody
will correct this.

02:15:06.620 --> 02:15:11.180
But I believe it was physics
graduate students from UC Santa

02:15:11.180 --> 02:15:14.420
Cruz or somewhere else,
maybe it was Caltech--

02:15:14.420 --> 02:15:17.615
a bunch of universities
so that no one associates

02:15:17.615 --> 02:15:20.630
it with any one university
that went to Vegas

02:15:20.630 --> 02:15:27.380
and used some sort of tactile
device for card counting thing.

02:15:27.380 --> 02:15:30.575
This was actually
demonstrated also--

02:15:30.575 --> 02:15:32.450
not this particular
incident, I don't think--

02:15:32.450 --> 02:15:37.235
in the movie Casino
where they spotted a--

02:15:37.235 --> 02:15:38.990
I remember Robert
De Niro, who you

02:15:38.990 --> 02:15:43.700
have a not so vague resemblance
to, by the way, in Taxi Driver.

02:15:43.700 --> 02:15:47.390
LEX FRIDMAN: God, I wish I had
a De Niro impression right now.

02:15:47.390 --> 02:15:48.752
Travis Bickle.

02:15:48.752 --> 02:15:50.210
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Look it up, folks.

02:15:50.210 --> 02:15:54.500
Travis Bickle is if Lex ever
shaved his head into a Mohawk.

02:15:54.500 --> 02:15:55.410
LEX FRIDMAN: I would.

02:15:55.410 --> 02:15:58.418
ANDREW HUBERMAN: So he had a
tapping device on his ankle

02:15:58.418 --> 02:15:59.210
that was signaling.

02:15:59.210 --> 02:16:01.400
Someone else was counting
cards and then signaling

02:16:01.400 --> 02:16:02.270
to that person.

02:16:02.270 --> 02:16:06.290
So yeah, that could be
done in the tactile way.

02:16:06.290 --> 02:16:08.390
It could be done,
obviously, earpieces

02:16:08.390 --> 02:16:09.440
if it's deep earpiece.

02:16:09.440 --> 02:16:12.140
I think there are ways
that they look for that.

02:16:12.140 --> 02:16:14.270
Certainly any kind
of vibrational device

02:16:14.270 --> 02:16:17.540
in whatever orifice provided
someone could pay attention

02:16:17.540 --> 02:16:20.220
to that while still
playing the game.

02:16:20.220 --> 02:16:21.950
Yeah, I think it's
entirely possible.

02:16:21.950 --> 02:16:24.905
Now, could it be
done purely neurally?

02:16:24.905 --> 02:16:27.020
Could there be
something that was--

02:16:27.020 --> 02:16:29.370
and listen, it wouldn't have
to even be below the skull.

02:16:29.370 --> 02:16:31.970
This is where whenever people
hear about Neuralink or brain

02:16:31.970 --> 02:16:33.080
machine interface
they always think,

02:16:33.080 --> 02:16:34.830
oh, you have to drill
down below the skull

02:16:34.830 --> 02:16:36.650
and put a chip below
into the skull.

02:16:36.650 --> 02:16:38.650
I think there are people
walking around nowadays

02:16:38.650 --> 02:16:41.389
with glucose monitoring devices,
like Levels-- which I've used

02:16:41.389 --> 02:16:44.299
and it was very informative
for me, actually,

02:16:44.299 --> 02:16:45.590
as a kind of an experiment.

02:16:45.590 --> 02:16:48.027
Gave me a lot of interesting
insights about my blood sugar

02:16:48.027 --> 02:16:50.360
regulation, how it reacts to
different foods, et cetera.

02:16:50.360 --> 02:16:55.280
Well, you can implant
a tactile device

02:16:55.280 --> 02:16:57.950
below the skin with
a simple incision.

02:16:57.950 --> 02:16:59.930
Actually, one of the
neurosurgeons at Neuralink

02:16:59.930 --> 02:17:02.650
I know well because he
came up at some point

02:17:02.650 --> 02:17:04.400
through my laboratory
and was at Stanford,

02:17:04.400 --> 02:17:07.450
and he actually has put in a
radio receiver in his hand,

02:17:07.450 --> 02:17:08.450
and his wife has it too.

02:17:08.450 --> 02:17:10.878
And he can open locks
of his house and things

02:17:10.878 --> 02:17:12.170
like that, so he's been doing--

02:17:12.170 --> 02:17:12.860
LEX FRIDMAN: Under the skin?

02:17:12.860 --> 02:17:13.760
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Under the skin.

02:17:13.760 --> 02:17:14.690
You can go to--

02:17:14.690 --> 02:17:15.180
LEX FRIDMAN: How does that work?

02:17:15.180 --> 02:17:15.680
So how do you use--

02:17:15.680 --> 02:17:16.805
ANDREW HUBERMAN: A piercer.

02:17:16.805 --> 02:17:19.735
You go to a body
piercer type person

02:17:19.735 --> 02:17:21.360
and they can just
slide it under there,

02:17:21.360 --> 02:17:23.610
and it's got a battery
life of something

02:17:23.610 --> 02:17:25.299
and some fairly long duration.

02:17:25.299 --> 02:17:27.299
LEX FRIDMAN: How do you
experience the tactile--

02:17:27.299 --> 02:17:28.250
the haptics of it?

02:17:28.250 --> 02:17:28.520
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Oh, no.

02:17:28.520 --> 02:17:30.740
That just allows him to
open certain locks with just

02:17:30.740 --> 02:17:34.309
his hand, but you could easily
put some sort of tactile device

02:17:34.309 --> 02:17:35.180
in there.

02:17:35.180 --> 02:17:37.430
LEX FRIDMAN: But does it
have to connect to the nerves

02:17:37.430 --> 02:17:39.379
or is it just like--
just vibration?

02:17:39.379 --> 02:17:40.320
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
No, just vibration.

02:17:40.320 --> 02:17:41.209
LEX FRIDMAN: And
you can probably

02:17:41.209 --> 02:17:43.010
sense it even if it's
under the skin, I wonder.

02:17:43.010 --> 02:17:43.850
ANDREW HUBERMAN: And
it can be by-- it

02:17:43.850 --> 02:17:44.959
can be Bluetooth linked.

02:17:44.959 --> 02:17:47.540
I mean, I've seen--
there's an Engineering

02:17:47.540 --> 02:17:50.870
Laboratory at the University
of Illinois, Champaign-Urbana,

02:17:50.870 --> 02:17:52.632
that's got an amazing
device which is

02:17:52.632 --> 02:17:53.840
about the size of a Band-Aid.

02:17:53.840 --> 02:17:56.990
It goes on the
clavicles and it uses

02:17:56.990 --> 02:18:00.379
sound waves pinged into the
body to measure cavitation.

02:18:00.379 --> 02:18:02.219
Think about this for a moment.

02:18:02.219 --> 02:18:04.160
This is being used in
the military where,

02:18:04.160 --> 02:18:07.129
let's say, you're leading
an operation or something.

02:18:07.129 --> 02:18:09.870
People are getting shot,
shot at, and on a laptop

02:18:09.870 --> 02:18:12.552
you can see where the
bullet entry points are.

02:18:12.552 --> 02:18:13.219
Are people dead?

02:18:13.219 --> 02:18:14.950
Are they bleeding out?

02:18:14.950 --> 02:18:16.670
Entry, exit points.

02:18:16.670 --> 02:18:19.820
You can get-- take it out
of the battlefield scenario.

02:18:19.820 --> 02:18:23.299
You can get breathing, body
position 24 hours a day.

02:18:23.299 --> 02:18:25.549
There's so much that you can
do looking at cavitation.

02:18:25.549 --> 02:18:29.270
So these same sorts of
devices on 12 hour Bluetooth

02:18:29.270 --> 02:18:32.360
could be used to send
all sorts of signals.

02:18:32.360 --> 02:18:35.059
Maybe every time you're
supposed to hold your hand--

02:18:35.059 --> 02:18:37.730
I'm not a good gambler
so I only play roulette

02:18:37.730 --> 02:18:41.433
when I go to Vegas because
you just long, boring games,

02:18:41.433 --> 02:18:43.850
but you get some good mileage
out of each out of each run,

02:18:43.850 --> 02:18:44.389
usually.

02:18:44.389 --> 02:18:49.070
But maybe every time
you're supposed to hold,

02:18:49.070 --> 02:18:51.559
the person gets a
stomach cinching

02:18:51.559 --> 02:18:54.200
because this is stimulating
the vagus a little bit

02:18:54.200 --> 02:18:55.742
and they get a little
bit of an ache.

02:18:55.742 --> 02:18:57.770
So it doesn't have
to be Morse code.

02:18:57.770 --> 02:18:59.959
It can be yes, no, maybe.

02:18:59.959 --> 02:19:01.060
Right?

02:19:01.060 --> 02:19:04.245
It can be green, red,
yellow type signaling.

02:19:04.245 --> 02:19:06.620
It doesn't have to be very
sophisticated to give somebody

02:19:06.620 --> 02:19:08.450
a significant advantage.

02:19:08.450 --> 02:19:10.400
Anyway, I haven't thought
about this in detail

02:19:10.400 --> 02:19:12.290
before this conversation
but, oh, yeah,

02:19:12.290 --> 02:19:13.498
there's an immense landscape.

02:19:13.498 --> 02:19:15.040
LEX FRIDMAN: I don't
know if you know

02:19:15.040 --> 02:19:16.520
a poker player named Phil Ivey?

02:19:16.520 --> 02:19:19.190
ANDREW HUBERMAN: No, I don't
follow the gambling thing.

02:19:19.190 --> 02:19:20.120
LEX FRIDMAN: Well,
he's considered

02:19:20.120 --> 02:19:21.745
to be one of the
greatest poker players

02:19:21.745 --> 02:19:23.447
of all time legitimately.

02:19:23.447 --> 02:19:24.530
He's just incredibly good.

02:19:24.530 --> 02:19:29.120
But he got-- there's
this big case where

02:19:29.120 --> 02:19:32.284
he was accused of
cheating and prove--

02:19:32.284 --> 02:19:33.860
and it's not really
cheating, which

02:19:33.860 --> 02:19:35.450
is what's really fascinating.

02:19:35.450 --> 02:19:38.540
Is it turns out--

02:19:38.540 --> 02:19:39.680
so he plays poker.

02:19:39.680 --> 02:19:41.959
Texas Hold'em, mostly,
but all kinds of poker.

02:19:41.959 --> 02:19:45.950
It turns out that the grid
on the back of the cards

02:19:45.950 --> 02:19:49.600
is often printed a
little bit imperfectly,

02:19:49.600 --> 02:19:55.090
and so you can use the
asymmetry of the imperfections

02:19:55.090 --> 02:19:56.830
to try to figure
out certain cards.

02:19:56.830 --> 02:20:01.060
So if you play and you remember
that a certain card is, like--

02:20:01.060 --> 02:20:05.080
I think the 8 in that deck that
he was accused of-- an 8 and 9

02:20:05.080 --> 02:20:08.650
were slightly different
symmetry wise.

02:20:08.650 --> 02:20:12.580
So he can now ask
the dealer actually

02:20:12.580 --> 02:20:14.170
to rotate it to
check the symmetry.

02:20:14.170 --> 02:20:17.170
So you would ask the dealer
to rotate the card to see that

02:20:17.170 --> 02:20:19.240
there's-- to detect the
asymmetry of the back

02:20:19.240 --> 02:20:22.120
of the card, and now he
knows which cards are

02:20:22.120 --> 02:20:26.800
8's and 9's or likelier to be
8's and 9's, and he was using

02:20:26.800 --> 02:20:31.450
that information to play
poker and win a lot of money.

02:20:31.450 --> 02:20:33.400
But it's just a
slight advantage.

02:20:33.400 --> 02:20:37.000
And his case is-- and
in fact, the judge

02:20:37.000 --> 02:20:39.100
found this, that he's
not actually cheating,

02:20:39.100 --> 02:20:40.420
but it's not right.

02:20:40.420 --> 02:20:42.830
You can't use this kind
of extra information.

02:20:42.830 --> 02:20:45.430
So it's fascinating that you
can discover these little holes

02:20:45.430 --> 02:20:47.440
in games if you pay
close enough attention.

02:20:47.440 --> 02:20:48.360
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yeah.

02:20:48.360 --> 02:20:49.550
It's fascinating.

02:20:49.550 --> 02:20:53.350
And I think that I
did watch that clip

02:20:53.350 --> 02:20:56.050
about the potential of a
cheating event in chess,

02:20:56.050 --> 02:20:58.930
and the fact that a
number of chess players

02:20:58.930 --> 02:21:01.840
admit to cheating at some
point in their career.

02:21:01.840 --> 02:21:03.015
Very, very interesting.

02:21:03.015 --> 02:21:04.390
LEX FRIDMAN: Well,
it was online.

02:21:04.390 --> 02:21:07.180
So online cheating
is easier, right?

02:21:07.180 --> 02:21:09.400
When you're playing
online cheating

02:21:09.400 --> 02:21:12.040
in a game where the machine
is much better than the human,

02:21:12.040 --> 02:21:14.320
it's very difficult to
prove that you're human.

02:21:14.320 --> 02:21:16.900
And that applies, by the
way, another really big thing

02:21:16.900 --> 02:21:19.193
is in social media, the bots.

02:21:19.193 --> 02:21:20.860
If you're running a
social media company

02:21:20.860 --> 02:21:23.680
you have to deal with the
bots and they become--

02:21:23.680 --> 02:21:25.390
one of the really
exciting things

02:21:25.390 --> 02:21:28.000
in machine learning and
artificial intelligence, to me,

02:21:28.000 --> 02:21:31.720
is the very fast improvement
of language models.

02:21:31.720 --> 02:21:34.170
So neural networks
that generate text,

02:21:34.170 --> 02:21:37.195
that interpret text, that
generate from text, images

02:21:37.195 --> 02:21:38.320
and all that kind of stuff.

02:21:38.320 --> 02:21:44.080
But you're now going to
create incredible bots that

02:21:44.080 --> 02:21:45.970
look awfully a lot like humans.

02:21:45.970 --> 02:21:46.540
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, at least they're

02:21:46.540 --> 02:21:47.980
not going to be those
crypto bots that

02:21:47.980 --> 02:21:50.378
seem to populate my comment
section when I post anything

02:21:50.378 --> 02:21:50.920
on Instagram.

02:21:50.920 --> 02:21:52.753
I actually delete those
even though they add

02:21:52.753 --> 02:21:56.710
to the comment roster and if--

02:21:56.710 --> 02:21:59.410
they bother me so much.

02:21:59.410 --> 02:22:02.060
I spend at least 10, 15 minutes
on each post just deleting

02:22:02.060 --> 02:22:02.560
those.

02:22:02.560 --> 02:22:03.935
I don't know what
they need to do

02:22:03.935 --> 02:22:07.390
but I'm not interested
in those, whatever

02:22:07.390 --> 02:22:09.940
it is they're offering.

02:22:09.940 --> 02:22:11.680
Speaking of nonbots,
I'm going to assume

02:22:11.680 --> 02:22:13.720
that all the questions
are not from bots.

02:22:13.720 --> 02:22:15.460
There are a lot of
questions here--

02:22:15.460 --> 02:22:17.050
more than 10,000 questions.

02:22:17.050 --> 02:22:17.950
Goodness.

02:22:17.950 --> 02:22:20.350
I'll just take a few,
working from top to bottom.

02:22:26.520 --> 02:22:29.160
What ideas have you been
wrestling with lately?

02:22:29.160 --> 02:22:31.350
And I think about
the company as one,

02:22:31.350 --> 02:22:34.995
but as I scroll to the
next, what are some others?

02:22:34.995 --> 02:22:37.370
LEX FRIDMAN: Well, some of
the things we've talked about,

02:22:37.370 --> 02:22:48.460
which is the ideas of how
to understand what is true,

02:22:48.460 --> 02:22:50.890
what is true about a human
being, how to reveal that,

02:22:50.890 --> 02:22:52.840
how to reveal that
through conversation, how

02:22:52.840 --> 02:22:56.200
to challenge that
properly, that it leads

02:22:56.200 --> 02:22:57.790
to understanding not derision.

02:22:57.790 --> 02:23:00.160
So that applies to
everybody from Donald Trump

02:23:00.160 --> 02:23:01.165
to Vladimir Putin.

02:23:05.110 --> 02:23:08.980
Also another idea is there's
a deep distrust of science

02:23:08.980 --> 02:23:11.680
in trying to understand-- the
growing distrust of science,

02:23:11.680 --> 02:23:15.220
trying to understand what's
the role of those of us

02:23:15.220 --> 02:23:20.140
that have a foot in the
scientific community,

02:23:20.140 --> 02:23:21.805
how to regain some
of that trust.

02:23:24.700 --> 02:23:32.780
Also, there's-- as we talked
about, how to find and how to--

02:23:32.780 --> 02:23:35.510
yeah, how to find and how to
maintain a good relationship.

02:23:35.510 --> 02:23:37.085
I mean, that's really been--

02:23:37.085 --> 02:23:39.530
I've never felt
quite as lonely as I

02:23:39.530 --> 02:23:41.780
have this year with Ukraine.

02:23:41.780 --> 02:23:45.800
It's just like, so many
times I would just lay there

02:23:45.800 --> 02:23:51.260
and just feeling so deeply alone
because I felt that my home--

02:23:51.260 --> 02:23:54.500
not my home literally
because I'm an American.

02:23:54.500 --> 02:23:55.700
I'm a proud American.

02:23:55.700 --> 02:23:57.020
I'll die an American.

02:23:57.020 --> 02:24:00.080
But my home in the
sense of generationally,

02:24:00.080 --> 02:24:04.700
my family's home, is now going--

02:24:04.700 --> 02:24:07.010
has been changed forever.

02:24:07.010 --> 02:24:12.610
There's no more being proud of
being from the former Russia

02:24:12.610 --> 02:24:14.230
or Ukraine.

02:24:14.230 --> 02:24:17.680
It's now a political
message to say--

02:24:17.680 --> 02:24:20.380
to show your pride, and so
it's been extremely lonely.

02:24:20.380 --> 02:24:23.260
And within that world, with
all the things I'm pursuing,

02:24:23.260 --> 02:24:25.010
how do you find a
successful relationship?

02:24:25.010 --> 02:24:26.260
It has been tough.

02:24:26.260 --> 02:24:28.150
But obviously-- and
there's a huge number

02:24:28.150 --> 02:24:29.980
of technical ideas
with the startup of,

02:24:29.980 --> 02:24:32.720
like, how the hell do
you make this thing work?

02:24:32.720 --> 02:24:34.760
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Well,
the relationship topic

02:24:34.760 --> 02:24:36.450
is one we talked a
little bit about,

02:24:36.450 --> 02:24:40.350
and last time we touched on
in a little bit more detail.

02:24:40.350 --> 02:24:45.260
We're going to come back to
that, so I've made a note here.

02:24:45.260 --> 02:24:48.710
What or who inspired
Lex, you, to wear

02:24:48.710 --> 02:24:50.390
a suit every time you podcast?

02:24:50.390 --> 02:24:51.540
That's a good question.

02:24:51.540 --> 02:24:54.490
I don't know the answer to that.

02:24:54.490 --> 02:24:57.340
LEX FRIDMAN: So there's two
answers to that question.

02:24:57.340 --> 02:25:03.000
One is a suit and two is
a black suit and black tie

02:25:03.000 --> 02:25:05.290
because I used to do--

02:25:05.290 --> 02:25:08.363
I used to have more variety,
which is like it was always

02:25:08.363 --> 02:25:09.780
a black suit but
I would sometimes

02:25:09.780 --> 02:25:12.480
do a red tie and a blue tie.

02:25:12.480 --> 02:25:14.820
But that was mostly me
trying to fit in to society

02:25:14.820 --> 02:25:18.525
because varieties-- you're
supposed to have some variety.

02:25:18.525 --> 02:25:26.058
What inspired me at first
was a general culture

02:25:26.058 --> 02:25:28.080
that doesn't take
itself seriously

02:25:28.080 --> 02:25:30.760
in terms of how you present
yourself to the world.

02:25:30.760 --> 02:25:32.970
So in academia,
in the tech world,

02:25:32.970 --> 02:25:37.570
at Google, everybody was wearing
pajamas and very relaxed.

02:25:37.570 --> 02:25:38.107
In the tech.

02:25:38.107 --> 02:25:39.690
I don't know how it
is in the science,

02:25:39.690 --> 02:25:41.830
in the chemistry,
biology, and so on.

02:25:41.830 --> 02:25:47.120
But in computer science
everybody was very--

02:25:47.120 --> 02:25:50.720
I mean, very relaxed in
terms of the stuff they

02:25:50.720 --> 02:25:53.540
wear so I wanted to try
to really take myself

02:25:53.540 --> 02:25:55.880
seriously and take every
single moment seriously

02:25:55.880 --> 02:25:58.160
and everything I do
seriously, and the suit

02:25:58.160 --> 02:25:59.340
made me feel that way.

02:25:59.340 --> 02:26:01.920
I don't know how it looks,
but it made me feel that way.

02:26:01.920 --> 02:26:03.770
And I think, in terms
of people I look up

02:26:03.770 --> 02:26:07.130
to that wore a suit that made
me think of that is probably

02:26:07.130 --> 02:26:08.960
Richard Feynman.

02:26:08.960 --> 02:26:09.490
I see--

02:26:09.490 --> 02:26:11.490
ANDREW HUBERMAN: He was
a wonderful human being.

02:26:11.490 --> 02:26:15.410
LEX FRIDMAN: I see him as the
epitome of class and humor

02:26:15.410 --> 02:26:19.910
and brilliance, and
obviously I could never

02:26:19.910 --> 02:26:21.440
come close to that kind of--

02:26:24.240 --> 02:26:27.540
be able to simply explain
really complicated ideas

02:26:27.540 --> 02:26:31.980
and to have humor and wit,
but definitely aspire to that.

02:26:31.980 --> 02:26:34.890
And then there's
just the Mad Men,

02:26:34.890 --> 02:26:37.680
that whole era of the '50s,
the classiness of that.

02:26:37.680 --> 02:26:41.250
There's something
about a suit that

02:26:41.250 --> 02:26:45.180
both removes the importance
of fashion from the character.

02:26:45.180 --> 02:26:46.320
You see the person.

02:26:46.320 --> 02:26:50.800
I think not to--

02:26:50.800 --> 02:26:51.760
I forgot who said this.

02:26:51.760 --> 02:26:54.550
Might be, like, Coco Chanel
or somebody like this.

02:26:54.550 --> 02:27:02.860
Is that you wear a shabby dress
and everyone sees the dress.

02:27:02.860 --> 02:27:07.600
You wear a beautiful dress
and everybody sees the woman.

02:27:07.600 --> 02:27:10.360
So in that sense it was--

02:27:10.360 --> 02:27:12.520
hopefully I'm quoting
that correctly, but--

02:27:12.520 --> 02:27:14.100
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Sounds good.

02:27:14.100 --> 02:27:17.140
LEX FRIDMAN: I think
there's a sense in which

02:27:17.140 --> 02:27:22.810
a simple, classy suit
allows people to focus

02:27:22.810 --> 02:27:26.410
on your character and then do
so with the full responsibility

02:27:26.410 --> 02:27:28.850
of that, this is who I am.

02:27:28.850 --> 02:27:29.350
Yeah.

02:27:29.350 --> 02:27:31.225
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love
that, and I love what

02:27:31.225 --> 02:27:32.410
you said just prior to that.

02:27:32.410 --> 02:27:34.840
My father, who, again,
is always asking me

02:27:34.840 --> 02:27:39.280
why I don't dress formally
like you do always

02:27:39.280 --> 02:27:41.980
said to me growing
up, if you overdress

02:27:41.980 --> 02:27:46.180
slightly, at least people know
that you took them seriously.

02:27:46.180 --> 02:27:50.530
So it's a sign of respect for
your audience too in my eyes.

02:27:50.530 --> 02:27:55.510
Someone asked, is there an AI
equivalent of psychedelics?

02:27:55.510 --> 02:27:58.780
And I'm assuming they
mean is there something

02:27:58.780 --> 02:28:01.630
that machines can do
for themselves in order

02:28:01.630 --> 02:28:05.080
to alter their neural circuitry
through unconventional

02:28:05.080 --> 02:28:07.060
activation patterns.

02:28:07.060 --> 02:28:08.890
LEX FRIDMAN: Yes, obviously.

02:28:08.890 --> 02:28:11.590
Well, I don't know exactly
how psychedelics work,

02:28:11.590 --> 02:28:16.480
but you can see that with
all the diffusion models

02:28:16.480 --> 02:28:19.180
now with Dali and the
stable diffusion that

02:28:19.180 --> 02:28:20.680
generates from text, art.

02:28:24.300 --> 02:28:28.290
It's basically a small
injection of noise

02:28:28.290 --> 02:28:31.170
into a system that has
a deep representation

02:28:31.170 --> 02:28:34.300
of visual information.

02:28:34.300 --> 02:28:39.960
So it is able to convert text to
art in introducing uncertainty

02:28:39.960 --> 02:28:42.330
into that noise into that.

02:28:42.330 --> 02:28:43.762
That's kind of maybe.

02:28:43.762 --> 02:28:45.720
I could see that as a
parallel to psychedelics,

02:28:45.720 --> 02:28:48.405
and it's able to create
some incredible things.

02:28:48.405 --> 02:28:51.780
From a conceptual
understanding of a thing,

02:28:51.780 --> 02:28:56.250
it can create incredible
art that no human, I think,

02:28:56.250 --> 02:28:58.290
could have at least
easily created

02:28:58.290 --> 02:29:01.050
through a bit of
introduction of randomness.

02:29:01.050 --> 02:29:05.030
Randomness does a lot of work
in the machine learning world.

02:29:05.030 --> 02:29:06.740
Just enough.

02:29:06.740 --> 02:29:11.600
ANDREW HUBERMAN: There are
a lot of requests of you

02:29:11.600 --> 02:29:14.000
for relationship,
a lot of requests

02:29:14.000 --> 02:29:18.890
about statistics about you,
data about you specifically.

02:29:18.890 --> 02:29:23.450
Flipping past those,
what was the hardest belt

02:29:23.450 --> 02:29:24.500
to achieve in jujitsu?

02:29:24.500 --> 02:29:26.000
I would have assumed
the black belt,

02:29:26.000 --> 02:29:28.963
but is that actually true?

02:29:28.963 --> 02:29:29.630
LEX FRIDMAN: No.

02:29:29.630 --> 02:29:31.338
I mean, everybody has
a different journey

02:29:31.338 --> 02:29:33.710
through jujitsu, as people know.

02:29:33.710 --> 02:29:38.780
For me, the black belt
was the ceremonial belt,

02:29:38.780 --> 02:29:44.480
which is not usually the case,
because I fought the wars.

02:29:44.480 --> 02:29:47.730
I trained twice a day for I
don't know how many years--

02:29:47.730 --> 02:29:48.800
seven, eight years.

02:29:48.800 --> 02:29:51.020
I competed nonstop.

02:29:51.020 --> 02:29:53.240
I competed against people
much better than me.

02:29:53.240 --> 02:29:56.360
I competed against many
and beaten many black belts

02:29:56.360 --> 02:29:58.100
and brown belts.

02:29:58.100 --> 02:30:04.160
I think, for me personally,
the hardest belt

02:30:04.160 --> 02:30:10.760
was the brown belt because,
for people who know jujitsu,

02:30:10.760 --> 02:30:16.070
the size of tournament divisions
for blue belts and purple belts

02:30:16.070 --> 02:30:17.510
is just humongous.

02:30:17.510 --> 02:30:19.820
Like Worlds, when I competed
at Worlds it was, like,

02:30:19.820 --> 02:30:22.982
140 people in a division,
which means you have to win--

02:30:22.982 --> 02:30:25.190
I forget how many times,
but seven, eight, nine times

02:30:25.190 --> 02:30:28.010
in a row to medal.

02:30:28.010 --> 02:30:33.620
And so I just had to put in a
lot of work during that time.

02:30:33.620 --> 02:30:36.680
And especially for competitors,
instructors usually

02:30:36.680 --> 02:30:40.970
really make you earn a belt.
So to earn the purple belt was

02:30:40.970 --> 02:30:43.160
extremely difficult.
Extremely difficult. And then

02:30:43.160 --> 02:30:44.660
to earn the brown
belt means I had

02:30:44.660 --> 02:30:48.500
to compete nonstop against other
purple belts, which are young.

02:30:48.500 --> 02:30:51.560
You're talking about-- the
people that usually compete

02:30:51.560 --> 02:30:55.610
are, like, 23, 24,
25-year-olds that are shredded,

02:30:55.610 --> 02:30:57.180
incredible cardio.

02:30:57.180 --> 02:30:59.870
They can, for some reason, are
in their life where they can--

02:30:59.870 --> 02:31:01.010
no kids, nothing.

02:31:01.010 --> 02:31:03.510
They can dedicate
everything to this pursuit

02:31:03.510 --> 02:31:05.750
so they're training two,
three, four times a day.

02:31:05.750 --> 02:31:07.560
Diet is on point.

02:31:07.560 --> 02:31:12.920
You're going-- and for me,
because they're usually bigger

02:31:12.920 --> 02:31:15.260
and taller than me and
just more aggressive,

02:31:15.260 --> 02:31:18.500
actual good athletes, yeah, I
had to go through a lot of wars

02:31:18.500 --> 02:31:20.250
to earn that brown
belt. But then--

02:31:20.250 --> 02:31:21.680
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I got to
try this jujitsu thing.

02:31:21.680 --> 02:31:22.580
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah, you should.

02:31:22.580 --> 02:31:23.510
But it's a different--

02:31:23.510 --> 02:31:24.927
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, I tried it.

02:31:24.927 --> 02:31:27.710
I did the one class, but I
really want to embrace it.

02:31:27.710 --> 02:31:31.100
LEX FRIDMAN: As you know,
many pursuits like jujitsu

02:31:31.100 --> 02:31:33.830
are different if you're doing it
in your 20s and 30s and later.

02:31:33.830 --> 02:31:36.380
It's like it's a different--

02:31:36.380 --> 02:31:39.200
you're not-- you can have a
bit of an ego in your 20s.

02:31:39.200 --> 02:31:40.640
You can have that
fire under you,

02:31:40.640 --> 02:31:47.000
but you should be more
zenlike and wise and patient

02:31:47.000 --> 02:31:48.150
later in life.

02:31:48.150 --> 02:31:50.960
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, one would hope.

02:31:50.960 --> 02:31:52.310
That's the wisdom.

02:31:52.310 --> 02:31:54.380
LEX FRIDMAN: I think
Rogan is still a meathead.

02:31:54.380 --> 02:31:56.930
He still goes hard and
crazy and he's still

02:31:56.930 --> 02:31:59.210
super competitive on
that, so some people can--

02:31:59.210 --> 02:32:00.555
Jocko is somebody like that.

02:32:00.555 --> 02:32:02.180
ANDREW HUBERMAN:
Well, whatever they're

02:32:02.180 --> 02:32:04.555
doing they're doing something
right because they're still

02:32:04.555 --> 02:32:07.640
in it, and that's
super impressive.

02:32:07.640 --> 02:32:11.580
There were far too many
questions to ask all of them,

02:32:11.580 --> 02:32:16.760
but several, if not many, asked
a highly appropriate question

02:32:16.760 --> 02:32:19.400
for where we are in the
arc of this discussion.

02:32:19.400 --> 02:32:24.080
And this is one, admittedly,
that you ask in your podcast

02:32:24.080 --> 02:32:26.330
all the time, but I
get the great pleasure

02:32:26.330 --> 02:32:30.750
of being in the question
asker seat today.

02:32:30.750 --> 02:32:34.475
And so, what is your
advice to young people?

02:32:38.220 --> 02:32:40.070
LEX FRIDMAN: So I just
gave a lecture at MIT

02:32:40.070 --> 02:32:46.120
and the amount of love I
got there is incredible.

02:32:46.120 --> 02:32:49.360
And so of course, who
you're talking to is usually

02:32:49.360 --> 02:32:53.290
undergrads, maybe young
graduate students, and so there

02:32:53.290 --> 02:32:56.860
one person did ask for advice
as a question at the end.

02:32:56.860 --> 02:33:02.230
I did a bunch of Q&amp;A. So my
answer was that the world will

02:33:02.230 --> 02:33:10.900
tell you to find a work-life
balance, to explore,

02:33:10.900 --> 02:33:11.905
to try to--

02:33:16.360 --> 02:33:18.610
try different fields
to see what you really

02:33:18.610 --> 02:33:22.720
connect with, variety,
general education, all

02:33:22.720 --> 02:33:24.350
that kind of stuff.

02:33:24.350 --> 02:33:26.440
And I said in your
20s I think you

02:33:26.440 --> 02:33:29.710
should find one thing
you're passionate about

02:33:29.710 --> 02:33:33.250
and work harder at that than
you worked at anything else

02:33:33.250 --> 02:33:34.340
in your life.

02:33:34.340 --> 02:33:36.970
And if it destroys
you, it destroys you.

02:33:36.970 --> 02:33:39.370
That's advice for in your 20s.

02:33:39.370 --> 02:33:44.110
I don't know how universally
true that advice is,

02:33:44.110 --> 02:33:46.510
but I think at least
give that a chance.

02:33:46.510 --> 02:33:50.380
Sacrifice, real sacrifice
towards a thing you

02:33:50.380 --> 02:33:52.540
really care about,
and work your ass off.

02:33:52.540 --> 02:33:55.240
That said, I've
met so many people,

02:33:55.240 --> 02:33:58.720
and I'm starting to
think that advice is best

02:33:58.720 --> 02:34:02.420
applied or best tried in
the engineer disciplines,

02:34:02.420 --> 02:34:04.850
especially programming.

02:34:04.850 --> 02:34:07.670
I think there's a bunch of
disciplines in which you

02:34:07.670 --> 02:34:09.860
can achieve success
with much fewer hours,

02:34:09.860 --> 02:34:12.620
and it's much more
important to actually

02:34:12.620 --> 02:34:15.950
have a clarity of
thinking and great ideas

02:34:15.950 --> 02:34:17.900
and have an energetic mind.

02:34:17.900 --> 02:34:21.770
The grind in certain disciplines
does not produce great work.

02:34:21.770 --> 02:34:24.950
I just know that in computer
science and programming

02:34:24.950 --> 02:34:25.910
it often does.

02:34:25.910 --> 02:34:29.450
Some of the best people ever
that have built systems,

02:34:29.450 --> 02:34:32.990
have programmed systems are
usually like the John Carmack

02:34:32.990 --> 02:34:36.950
kind of people that drink
soda, eat pizza, and program

02:34:36.950 --> 02:34:38.640
18 hours a day.

02:34:38.640 --> 02:34:41.000
So I don't know actually.

02:34:41.000 --> 02:34:44.780
You have to, I think, really
go discipline specific.

02:34:44.780 --> 02:34:46.770
So my advice applies
to my own life

02:34:46.770 --> 02:34:49.610
which has been mostly
spent behind that computer,

02:34:49.610 --> 02:34:53.690
and for that you really, really
have to put in the hours.

02:34:53.690 --> 02:34:55.160
And what that means
is essentially

02:34:55.160 --> 02:34:57.530
it feels like a grind.

02:34:57.530 --> 02:35:01.400
I do recommend that you should
at least try it in your own.

02:35:01.400 --> 02:35:05.840
That if you interview some of
the most accomplished people

02:35:05.840 --> 02:35:08.900
ever, I think if
they're honest with you

02:35:08.900 --> 02:35:12.530
they're going to
talk about their 20s

02:35:12.530 --> 02:35:15.950
as a journey of a lot of pain
and a lot of really hard work.

02:35:15.950 --> 02:35:19.320
I think what really
happens, unfortunately,

02:35:19.320 --> 02:35:21.800
is a lot of those successful
people later in life

02:35:21.800 --> 02:35:23.840
will talk about
work-life balance.

02:35:23.840 --> 02:35:25.520
They'll say, you
know what I learned

02:35:25.520 --> 02:35:31.070
from that process is that it's
really important to get, like,

02:35:31.070 --> 02:35:33.005
sun in the morning,
to have health,

02:35:33.005 --> 02:35:34.130
to have good relationships.

02:35:34.130 --> 02:35:35.010
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Hire a chef.

02:35:35.010 --> 02:35:35.750
LEX FRIDMAN: Yeah, a chef.

02:35:35.750 --> 02:35:36.470
Exactly.

02:35:36.470 --> 02:35:40.370
But I think you have
forgot-- those people have

02:35:40.370 --> 02:35:43.820
forgotten the value
of the journey they

02:35:43.820 --> 02:35:45.410
took to that lesson.

02:35:45.410 --> 02:35:50.500
I think work-life balance is
best learned the hard way.

02:35:50.500 --> 02:35:52.270
My own perspective.

02:35:52.270 --> 02:35:54.800
There are certain things you
can only learn the hard way,

02:35:54.800 --> 02:35:57.010
and so you should learn
that the hard way.

02:35:57.010 --> 02:35:58.750
Yeah, so that's
definitely advice.

02:35:58.750 --> 02:36:04.900
And I should say that I
admire people that work hard.

02:36:04.900 --> 02:36:07.718
If you want to get
on my good side,

02:36:07.718 --> 02:36:09.760
I think there are the
people that give everything

02:36:09.760 --> 02:36:11.200
they got towards something.

02:36:11.200 --> 02:36:12.617
It doesn't actually
matter what it

02:36:12.617 --> 02:36:18.862
is, but towards achieving
excellence in a thing.

02:36:18.862 --> 02:36:23.980
That's the highest thing that
we can reach for as human beings

02:36:23.980 --> 02:36:27.100
I think is excellence
at a thing.

02:36:27.100 --> 02:36:28.440
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I love it.

02:36:28.440 --> 02:36:30.810
Well, speaking of
excellent at a thing.

02:36:30.810 --> 02:36:34.170
Whether or not it's teaching
at MIT or the podcast

02:36:34.170 --> 02:36:39.147
or the company that
resides in the near future

02:36:39.147 --> 02:36:39.855
that you create--

02:36:42.630 --> 02:36:45.120
once again, I'm speaking for
an enormous number of people

02:36:45.120 --> 02:36:49.350
that excellence and hard
work, certainly, are woven

02:36:49.350 --> 02:36:51.330
through everything that you do.

02:36:51.330 --> 02:36:54.570
Every time I sit down with
you I begin and finish

02:36:54.570 --> 02:36:58.620
with such an immense feeling
of joy and appreciation

02:36:58.620 --> 02:37:04.800
and gratitude, and it wouldn't
be a Lex Fridman podcast,

02:37:04.800 --> 02:37:08.640
or in case of Lex even
being a guest on a podcast,

02:37:08.640 --> 02:37:11.580
if the word love weren't
mentioned at least 10 times.

02:37:11.580 --> 02:37:16.470
So the feelings of gratitude
for all the work you do,

02:37:16.470 --> 02:37:19.590
for taking the time here today
to share with us what you're

02:37:19.590 --> 02:37:22.530
doing, your thoughts,
your insights, what

02:37:22.530 --> 02:37:25.948
you're perplexed about and what
drives you and your callings.

02:37:25.948 --> 02:37:27.240
LEX FRIDMAN: Can I read a poem?

02:37:27.240 --> 02:37:29.730
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Yes, please.

02:37:29.730 --> 02:37:31.110
He was trying to
cut me off post.

02:37:31.110 --> 02:37:32.640
That was getting a little long.

02:37:32.640 --> 02:37:33.307
LEX FRIDMAN: No.

02:37:33.307 --> 02:37:34.272
No, no, no.

02:37:34.272 --> 02:37:35.730
I was thinking
about this recently.

02:37:35.730 --> 02:37:40.155
It's one of my favorite
Robert Frost poems, and I--

02:37:40.155 --> 02:37:42.570
because I wrote several
essays on it, as you do,

02:37:42.570 --> 02:37:45.990
because I think it's a
popular one that's read.

02:37:45.990 --> 02:37:48.390
Essays being, like, trying
to interpret poetry,

02:37:48.390 --> 02:37:50.990
and it's one that
sticks with me.

02:37:50.990 --> 02:37:55.140
I mean, both its calm beauty,
but in the seriousness

02:37:55.140 --> 02:38:00.120
of what it means because I
ultimately think it's the--

02:38:00.120 --> 02:38:02.550
so "Stopping by a Woods
on a Snowy Evening."

02:38:02.550 --> 02:38:05.610
I think it's ultimately
a human being, a man,

02:38:05.610 --> 02:38:12.570
asking the old Sisyphus, the
old Camus question of, why live?

02:38:12.570 --> 02:38:15.150
I think this poem,
even though it

02:38:15.150 --> 02:38:18.810
doesn't seem like it is a
question of a man contending

02:38:18.810 --> 02:38:20.520
with suicide and
choosing to live.

02:38:24.070 --> 02:38:26.620
Whose woods these
are, I think I know.

02:38:26.620 --> 02:38:28.690
His house is in the
village, though.

02:38:28.690 --> 02:38:32.110
He will not see me stopping
here to watch his woods fill up

02:38:32.110 --> 02:38:33.310
with snow.

02:38:33.310 --> 02:38:35.800
My little horse
must think it queer

02:38:35.800 --> 02:38:39.640
to stop without a farmhouse near
between the woods and frozen

02:38:39.640 --> 02:38:42.470
lake, the darkest
evening of the year.

02:38:42.470 --> 02:38:44.660
He gives this
harness bells a shake

02:38:44.660 --> 02:38:47.070
to ask if there's some mistake.

02:38:47.070 --> 02:38:52.040
The only other sound's the sweep
of easy wind and downy flake.

02:38:52.040 --> 02:38:55.190
The woods are lovely,
dark, and deep,

02:38:55.190 --> 02:39:00.240
but I have promises to keep
and miles to go before I sleep,

02:39:00.240 --> 02:39:03.530
and miles to go before I sleep.

02:39:03.530 --> 02:39:06.050
The woods representing
the darkness,

02:39:06.050 --> 02:39:08.120
the comfort of the woods
representing death,

02:39:08.120 --> 02:39:11.840
and he's a man choosing to live.

02:39:11.840 --> 02:39:15.820
Yeah, I think about that often,
especially my darker moments

02:39:15.820 --> 02:39:19.090
is you have promises to keep.

02:39:22.450 --> 02:39:24.670
Thank you for having me, Andrew.

02:39:24.670 --> 02:39:26.537
You're a beautiful human being.

02:39:26.537 --> 02:39:27.370
I love you, brother.

02:39:27.370 --> 02:39:29.320
ANDREW HUBERMAN: I
love you, brother.

